title: What you produce on every task
---

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
content:
Every task ships 5 artifacts. All 5 are yours, end to end. The workspace is already built - you do not generate input files.

<br/>

1. **Prompt** - 1 complex, layered question in the persona's voice, grounded in what you saw in the environment.
2. **Critical Elements** - the specific facts, constraints, and outputs a correct answer must hit.
3. **Failure Justifications** - for each of the 3 model rollouts that failed, a 3-5 sentence justification with quoted output.
4. **Golden Trajectory** - the ideal response you had in mind when you wrote the prompt.
5. **Rubric Edits** - 20 to 35 weighted criteria, scored PASS / FAIL against each model, with difficulty and discrimination bars confirmed.
\`\`\`

When you claim a task, the platform shows you 2 pieces of context. You author the prompt **FOR** this persona, **ABOUT** this category. The combination shapes everything: the question you ask, the answer you have in mind, and every criterion in the rubric.

## Persona

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: The persona is your raw material
content:
On each task, you'll see a persona for one of our retail consumers. Their bank statement, transaction history, and any other workspace files are already loaded in the sandbox, so read them before you write a single word of the prompt. Let the persona's life, occupation, income, family, where they shop, what they owe, leak into the prompt you write.

<br/>

A prompt that could have been written by anyone is too generic. Pull at least two specific details from the workspace into the question - names, dollar amounts, employer, dependents, debts. That specificity is what makes the model do real work.

<br/>

Full instructions [here](/personas).
\`\`\`

## Category

One of 6 in-scope topics. Your prompt must clearly land in the assigned category.

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: In scope
content:
- Budgeting / spending analysis
- Subscriptions / cash flow
- Retail investment + portfolio analysis
- Credit decisions
- Goal-based / scenario planning
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: important
title: Out of scope
content:
- Tax planning (1040-level individual)
- IB or corporate finance
- Institutional asset management
- Specific securities recommendations (regulated advice)
\`\`\`

## Time expectations

- **15 to 20 minutes** reviewing the environment
- **30 minutes** writing the prompt
- _Model rollouts run while you wait_
- **15 to 20 minutes** reviewing the model responses
- **30 to 45 minutes** writing failure justifications
- <u>**60 to 90 minutes** authoring the rubric and scoring</u>

**Total: 2.5 to 3.5 hours per task.**
`,Y1='---\ntitle: Changelog\n---\n\nLast updated: June 5, 2026\n\n## June 5, 2026\n\nShared blocks now handle project-specific custom-token data more gracefully when a reusable page includes custom display scaffolding.\n\n- **Non-breaking custom-token handling** - Shared-block export and import now remove `custom-tokens` blocks with a warning instead of failing the whole shared block, while still rejecting unknown block types and unsafe content.\n- **Callout link styling** - Markdown links inside Callouts now render with the same underlined link treatment as body copy.\n\n## May 28, 2026\n\nCitadel now treats its tested resource-adjacent capabilities as active documentation instead of previews. The Resources section also has a clearer path for profile data, form submission, eval-agent feedback, and milestone tracking.\n\n- **Submit to LLM guide** - Added a Resources child page focused on how to prompt for eval-agent feedback experiences while keeping implementation details inside the eval-submission skill.\n- **Active capability labels** - Removed draft and coming-soon treatment from Form data, Shared blocks, Component development, and Milestones documentation.\n- **Resources navigation** - Moved User data, Form data, Submit to LLM, and Milestones under Resources in the sidebar.\n- **Sidebar child indentation** - Adjusted expanded child items so they sit slightly to the right of the parent label, matching the intended left-rail hierarchy.\n\n## May 19, 2026\n\nCitadel now gives production-task training stronger defaults for operational judgment, active practice, and annotation-style decisions. These updates help fellows practice with verifiable criteria instead of vague labels.\n\n- **Task-specific audit labels** - FlagAndFix now supports custom decision labels and button order for annotation, review, approve/reject, and mark/do-not-mark drills.\n- **Operational practice guidance** - Extended example documentation now covers quality-band precision, timeline-transcript highlighting, segmentation-style coverage matching, and edge-case accordions.\n- **Practice feedback behavior** - Knowledge checks and FlagAndFix drills can reveal incomplete work instead of blocking blank submissions.\n- **Scenario and slider polish** - ScenarioReveal starts with a warning/evidence panel, and BeforeAfterSlider fades corner labels near the handle to prevent collisions.\n\n## May 18, 2026\n\nCitadel now treats hyphenated title words as a single phrase for sentence casing. This keeps compact labels readable without accidentally turning the second half of a compound into a new title word.\n\n- **Hyphenated title casing** - Unspaced hyphens no longer restart capitalization, so titles like `FIRST-LAST approval` render as "First-last approval" while spaced divider dashes still start a new segment.\n\n## May 14, 2026\n\nCitadel now has a first-class extended example component catalog for critique, scoring, matching, grouping, and visual comparison practice.\n\n- **Extended example components** - New documentation page demonstrates ScenarioReveal, FlagAndFix, GoodNeutralBadTable, BeforeAfterSlider, ZoneHighlighter, CoverageMatcher, GroupingDrill, RatingDrill, and Scorer with live examples and copyable fenced-block syntax.\n- **Slider sample assets** - Added small before/after PNG examples so the BeforeAfterSlider demonstrates a subtle visual difference without relying on placeholder art.\n- **Stable wrapped sidebar labels** - Active sidebar items now use medium weight with the same font size and fixed line-height as inactive items, so 2-line labels do not visually shrink or shift when selected.\n- **Accordion image loading states** - Markdown images inside accordions now reserve space and show a muted loading shimmer until the image loads.\n\n## May 13, 2026\n\nSidebar selection now emphasizes the active page without causing long labels to rewrap. This keeps navigation steady for fellows as they move through modules with tightly fitting titles.\n\n- **Stable sidebar wrapping** - Active sidebar labels now use metric-stable emphasis instead of a real text-weight change, preventing selected titles from changing line breaks.\n\n## May 12, 2026\n\nCitadel now has the template-side foundation for shared blocks, so standard markdown pages can be exported as private artifacts and consuming apps can reserve manifest positions for live shared content.\n\n- **Shared-block parser support** - Manifest parsing now recognizes `sharedBlocks` export declarations and required or optional `{{SHARED domain:block}}` placeholders.\n- **Private artifact generation** - `npm run build` now validates shared-block declarations and writes private producer or consumer metadata under `dist/.private/shared-blocks/`.\n- **Runtime shared-content registry** - The app shell can merge fetched shared pages into navigation and page lookup data, with non-production placeholder pages and production failure handling.\n- **Shared-block skill** - New operator guidance explains how to export portable pages, consume producer blocks, and preserve the domain-scoped authorization boundary.\n- **Coming-soon documentation** - A new draft Rules page introduces the shared-blocks skill, explains the export/consume workflow, and renders through the generic standard page path instead of a generated page component.\n- **Section navigation scroll tracking** - SectionNav now listens to the main scroll container, so the right-side section rail updates correctly as learners scroll page content.\n\n## May 05, 2026\n\nCitadel\'s page chrome now adapts more cleanly to the space available around lesson content. The base template also adopts the HAI Course visual refinements, giving navigation, status surfaces, and instructional components a softer, more consistent presentation.\n\n- **Dynamic content centering** - Main content now centers in the available area and only reserves right-side space when the SectionNav rail is actually visible at wide breakpoints.\n- **SectionNav state styling** - Inactive hover and active section states now share the `--bg-secondary` surface and `--fg-primary` text treatment, with active state distinguished by weight.\n- **SectionNav rail card** - Section navigation now appears as a rounded grey card in the right gutter with inset, rounded items.\n- **Softer app canvas** - Page and sidebar backgrounds now use a soft `#fafafa` surface, with muted foreground handled as 60% foreground opacity.\n- **Status surface refresh** - Positive and info status backgrounds now use opaque pastel values for more predictable contrast inside cards and callouts.\n- **Sidebar polish** - Sidebar icons use lighter strokes, the logo renders as a fixed square mark, Maintaining uses a wrench icon, Changelog uses refresh arrows, and sub-items render labels exactly as authored.\n- **Instructional component polish** - Highlight marginalia aligns multi-digit references, StepSequence connectors are solid hairlines, and accordion chevrons use secondary foreground.\n- **Watermark demo promoted** - The forensic watermarking demo now lives under Content and no longer shows the Soon badge.\n- **Template development guide** - A new Rules page documents the iterate-template skill, local branch lifecycle, baseline capture, validation workflow, and migration-plan handoff.\n- **Accordion title hover** - Accordion rows now highlight the title text itself on hover with a close-fitting active-state background.\n- **Loading watermark placeholders** - Visible watermarks now replace unknown email and user values with dash placeholders until current user identity resolves.\n\n## May 04, 2026\n\nCitadel\'s watermark demo now shows the full confidential watermark behavior without turning the template itself into a globally watermarked app. This keeps the template safe for ordinary documentation while preserving a reusable pattern for projects that need forensic protection.\n\n- **Watermark demo scoped to 1 page** - The Citadel template keeps `watermarked: false` and renders visible and invisible watermarks only on `/watermark-demo`.\n- **Readable visible marks** - Desktop confidential marks now render as unrotated lower-left and lower-right rectangles with multiline wrapping instead of diagonal truncated labels.\n- **Opaque watermark labels** - Fixed visible watermarks now use opaque danger-tinted backgrounds so sidebar and page text cannot bleed through the confidential label.\n- **Mobile banner behavior** - Small screens still collapse the visible watermark into 1 fixed bottom banner while reserving scroll space so content remains readable.\n- **Toast stacking** - Toast notifications now render above the watermark layers.\n- **Watermark skill contract** - New forensic watermark guidance documents how projects that opt into `watermarked: true` should apply both invisible and visible user-identifying layers across their routes.\n\n## April 30, 2026\n\nCitadel now documents the work-in-progress component development workflow in the template itself. This helps operators distinguish project-specific custom components from reusable Citadel component infrastructure before they ask an agent to build new interactivity.\n\n- **Component development guide** - A new top-level Content page at `/content/component-development` gives contributors a full workflow for proposing, designing, implementing, documenting, and validating reusable Citadel components.\n- **Prompt wizard** - The component development page now includes an interactive wizard that turns contributor answers into a structured prompt for `skills/component-development.md`, replacing the flat proposal template.\n- **Component development skill documentation** - The Skills page now explains `skills/component-development.md`, including its work-in-progress status and its focus on reusable Citadel template components.\n- **Custom versus reusable guidance** - The Component catalog and FAQ now point operators to the custom component path for project-specific work and the component development skill for reusable Citadel infrastructure.\n\n## April 29, 2026\n\nGuided Scaffold is now the default scaffold experience. The agent treats a source document as the beginning of a training design conversation, not just a file conversion job.\n\n- **Guided Scaffold default** - `AGENTS.md` and `skills/scaffold.md` now require the agent to clarify learner outcome, training mode, source-fidelity boundaries, and enhancement permission before writing scaffold output.\n- **Fast scaffold escape hatch** - Operators who need the old mechanical path can explicitly ask for `fast scaffold`, `unguided scaffold`, or `/scaffold --fast`; the guided path remains the ordinary workflow.\n- **Filler with integrity** - `skills/pedagogy.md` now labels proposed additions as Source-derived, Inferred bridge, Practice/example, or Needs SME input, and forbids invented policy, compliance, organizational, metric, tool, or obligation claims.\n- **Decision-oriented review** - `skills/qa-check.md` persona review now reports severity, evidence, suggested fix, whether the user must decide, and whether a safe auto-fix is allowed.\n- **Operator docs updated** - Remix/scaffold, skills, and FAQ pages now explain Guided Scaffold, pedagogical analysis, source-basis labels, and the discreet fast scaffold option.\n- **Guided Scaffold evals** - `npm run eval:workflows` now scores guided scaffold fixtures for intake length, learning outcomes, learning arcs, source-basis labels, pedagogical component rationale, editorial backlog, SME gaps, and the fast-path contract.\n- **Editorial marker QA** - `npm run qa:content` now warns on unresolved scaffold/editorial markers such as `TK - Verify`, `TODO:`, `Needs SME input:`, rhythm placeholders, unaccepted agent suggestions, placeholder tokens, and lorem ipsum filler.\n- **Guided Scaffold example** - Remix documentation and `skills/scaffold.md` now include a concrete sample conversation showing inferred defaults, bounded intake questions, a learning arc, source-basis labels, and an editorial backlog.\n- **Workflow eval CI** - GitHub Actions now runs `npm run eval:workflows` on pull requests and pushes to `main`, backing up the local pre-push hook with remote enforcement.\n- **Repeatable Guided Scaffold dogfood** - `npm run dogfood:guided-scaffold` now builds a repeatable private-source/synthetic-source test pack without copying private source bodies into app content, and `npm run clean:scaffold-sources` plus `npm run check:private-sources` guard scaffold/remix output from retaining those test materials.\n- **Remix-first scaffold enforcement** - Citadel remixes now explicitly require Guided Scaffold as the first operator-facing step before customization, styling, feature work, or content edits; workflow evals enforce this contract.\n- **Lovable first-response transcript** - `docs/golden-transcripts/lovable-guided-scaffold-first-response.md` now captures the exact scaffold-first opening for remixed Lovable apps, and workflow evals protect the transcript.\n- **Reviewed content QA suppressions** - `npm run qa:content` now filters reviewed pre-existing template-documentation rhythm warnings through `config/content-qa-suppressions.json`, keeping future unsuppressed warnings actionable instead of noisy.\n- **Readable code snippets** - CodeBlock parsing now decodes legacy literal `\\n` sequences into real line breaks, and the Guided Scaffold example uses multiline snippets so operator docs are easier to read.\n- **Changelog QA exception** - Content QA now skips component-density and rhythm/text-wall checks for `changelog.md` because it is an append-only operator audit log, not learner training prose; the brittle changelog warning suppressions were removed.\n- **Resource code-block QA exception** - Content QA now permits adjacent `code-block` examples on resource reference pages where copyable prompt/config/code samples are the point of the page, removing another batch of brittle exact warning suppressions.\n- **Malformed component fence QA** - Content QA now flags XML-style component closing tags such as `</step-sequence>` so fenced component blocks must close with triple backticks and render correctly.\n- **Generator fence contracts** - Component generator tests now assert every fenced-block generator emits the expected opening fence, closes with triple backticks, avoids XML-style component closers, and round-trips through the content parser.\n- **Step title normalization** - Step-prefixed page headings and `step-sequence` item titles now normalize to `Step N - Sentence case text` (for example, `Step 1 - choose a dataset` becomes `Step 1 - Choose a dataset`), while Project-style proper names are wrapped in `^caret^` markers during scaffold so names like `Project Sapphire` keep their capitalization in sidebar/page rendering. Render-time sentence casing also resets after punctuation and separators, so headings such as `Step 1 - Choose a dataset` no longer become `Step 1 - choose a dataset`. Non-step component headings keep polished title casing.\n- **Scaffold cleanup and navigation guardrails** - Route QA now fails on stale page components left behind by scaffold runs and on generated markdown-backed pages that import or render `PageNavigation`/`SectionNav`, preventing doubled Previous/Next controls and forcing old scaffold files to be deleted before completion. The scaffold writer now deletes stale template `.md` files from `src/content/` and writes a fresh manifest instead of appending new routes to old template navigation.\n- **Checklist alternative guard** - Scaffold checklist detection now distinguishes tasks to complete from menus of alternatives. Lists introduced as choices, options, alternatives, approaches, or contextual “use X / use Y” paths stay as ordinary markdown bullets unless the source uses explicit checkbox syntax.\n\n## April 28, 2026\n\nThe scaffold pipeline has been overhauled to produce visually varied, rhythmic apps instead of walls of prose. Changes span the fuzzy parser (wider detection), the converter (component interleaving), and the skill docs (enforceable density rules).\n\n- **Components interleaved through prose** - `convertSectionToContent` no longer appends all detected components after the full text block. Prose is split into 2-paragraph groups and components are woven between them, producing a natural alternating rhythm rather than a text wall followed by a component pile.\n- **`injectRhythmPlaceholders`** - New post-interleave pass scans for stretches still exceeding 200 words or 3 paragraphs without a component and inserts a named TK callout placeholder. Multiple placeholders can fire per section, proportional to length.\n- **Plain numbered list → StepSequence** - 3 or more consecutive `1. item` lines (no bold, no "Step:" prefix) now produce a StepSequence. Previously the most common instructional pattern was invisible to the parser.\n- **Ordinal prose → StepSequence** - Paragraphs beginning with "First,", "Second,", "Third," etc. are now grouped into a StepSequence. Catches sequences described in prose without any list formatting.\n- **`detectHighlight`** - New detector for standalone bold sentences and short paragraphs (≤ 40 words) where ≥ 50% of visible text is inside `**...**`. Wired into the full detection pipeline.\n- **Checklist detection relaxed** - 5+ item lists always emit a Checklist; 3–4 item lists emit without any action-verb requirement. Previously, noun-phrase bullet lists (the most common instructional format) were silently dropped. Action-verb keyword list expanded from 27 to ~55 terms.\n- **Flowchart threshold raised** - Single if-then sentences no longer produce a trivial 3-node chart. At least 3 branching conditions are now required, eliminating noise from ordinary conditional prose.\n- **Component density floor** - All skill files updated: every page now requires ≥ 2 components (up from 1); pages over 300 words require 1 additional component per 250 words. Codified in `thresholds.md` and enforced by QA §10.\n- **Prose-pattern detection table** - `skills/scaffold.md` §14 now includes a micro-table mapping 8 paragraph-level prose patterns to specific components, so LLMs catch what the parser misses.\n- **Mandatory rhythm audit table** - `skills/scaffold.md` §15 now requires a filled per-page audit table (word count, component count, violations, action taken) before the step is considered complete. A blank table is a QA violation.\n- **Callout-as-default anti-pattern** - Skills now explicitly state that Callout is the fallback, not the default. StepSequence, Checklist, ExamplePair, Accordion, and Highlight are checked first.\n- **`previewScaffold` updated** - Preview output now uses the same interleave logic as the real scaffold, so `--test` output accurately reflects what the final pages will look like. `highlightCount` added to preview stats.\n- **Scaffold hardening pass** - Checklist detection now requires action or checklist context for noun-phrase lists, preventing category lists from becoming fake checklists. Plain numbered and ordinal StepSequence source text is removed after conversion so generated pages do not duplicate content. Preview/scaffold parity is covered by tests, detector debug candidates expose confidence/source windows, route/content consistency has an executable check, and scaffold health reports now include rhythm and strict-mode findings.\n- **Project Sapphire dry-run regression** - Tested the scaffold pipeline against the Project Sapphire playbook. The previous baseline could not scaffold the source because the document title was a bold-formatted H1; the hardened parser now accepts bold document headings, detects Google Docs-style `Step N` headings as a StepSequence, suppresses bogus flowcharts from dense rubric prose, and avoids duplicate checklist/prose output.\n\n## April 27, 2026\n\nA large content quality pass corrected bugs introduced by wrong component field names, wired up the previously unrendered NoteCardStepper, and aligned the operator-facing documentation with how the system actually behaves. Several skill files were updated to close gaps between what the rules say and what the QA checklist enforces.\n\n- **NoteCardStepper now renders** - The `notecard` fenced block type was parsed but the renderer returned `null`. Wired `NoteCardStepper` into `ContentBlockRenderer`. Ghost buttons throughout; Continue disables on the last card rather than switching to a "Complete" label.\n- **StepSequence field name bug fixed** - All `description:` fields in `milestones.md` renamed to `content:`. The parser silently dropped unrecognised field names, producing blank step bodies with no error.\n- **Scaffold health report now catches empty step content** - `scaffold-health.ts` flags step-sequence blocks where all steps have empty `content`, with a message pointing to the likely cause (wrong field name).\n- **Highlight duplicate ID warning** - `Highlight.tsx` logs a dev-mode `console.warn` when 2 marginalia entries share the same id, preventing silent note overwriting.\n- **`{draft}` nav type** - Manifest entries with type `{draft}` now render a "Soon" badge in the sidebar. Milestones, Form data, Splash screen, and Watermark demo marked accordingly.\n- **Scaffold auto-activation documented** - `skills/scaffold.md` and operator docs updated to reflect that the scaffold skill triggers automatically when a source document is provided; `/scaffold` is the explicit fallback, not the primary path.\n- **QA check expanded** - Added §19 (cross-reference integrity), consecutive same-type component threshold, and hard-requirement checks for legacy fenced-block format, inline `form_registration_id`, and direct `/submitFormData` calls.\n- **Skill `# Referenced by:` comments** - 10 skill files now carry a comment listing the content pages that describe them, making it easier to find stale references after a skill changes.\n- **Content fixes** - Decision guide moved to top of component catalog; `rules-voice.md` duplicate Highlight marker corrected; `rules-skills.md` self-contradiction and stale QA counts resolved; `resources.md` dangling callout fixed; FAQ items 4 and 8 corrected; home page Slack CTA converted from h2 to callout.\n- **`getting-started.md` rewritten** - Intro now frames the 2-phase process as a problem to solve rather than a list of steps.\n- **Post-scaffold workflow documented** - `getting-started-remix.md` now describes all 5 mandatory post-scaffold steps (QA, interactive content review, rhythmic variety, health report, persona review).\n- **`/sync` documented** - New section in `maintaining.md` covers the 3 sync strategies. FAQ items added for sync and migrate.\n\n- **Splash screen (WIP)** - New full-screen acknowledgement overlay for legal/ToU content before users enter training. Configured via `splash:` block in manifest frontmatter. Features: WCAG AA contrast enforcement when a custom `background_color` is set (auto-computed foreground tone); optional decorative doodle image (aria-hidden); scroll-to-bottom gate that disables the Continue button until the user has read through the legal text; "Don\'t show again" checkbox persisted to `localStorage` keyed per authenticated user profile ID; full keyboard navigation with focus trap. App boot now shows a neutral `AppLoadingScreen` while `useCurrentUserProfile` resolves, ensuring profile ID is available before the splash key is computed. New documentation page at `/splash-screen` with live preview button. New `skills/splash-screen.md` operator skill. Marked work-in-progress - configuration API may change before stable release.\n\n## April 24, 2026\n\n- **Forensic watermarking demo** - New experimental-only page at `/watermark-demo` demonstrating a steganographic technique: the current user\'s profile ID is tiled diagonally across the viewport as a near-invisible overlay using `mix-blend-mode: difference` over a canvas-rendered PNG tile. At normal viewing the text is imperceptible; boosting the Curves black-point slider to ~230 in Photoshop reveals it in any screenshot. A MutationObserver prevents DOM removal, `display: none`, `visibility: hidden`, or `opacity: 0` from hiding the overlay. Marked "Very experimental" - works for screenshots, not phone camera photos due to sensor noise floor.\n- **StepSequence connector line fix** - Connector lines between step circles now use absolute positioning, correcting vertical misalignment when step content varies in height.\n\n## April 23, 2026\n\n- **Publish workflow streamlined** - New `trigger-publish.yml` workflow added for event-driven deploys; existing `publish-to-gcs.yml` simplified by removing redundant configuration.\n\n## April 21–22, 2026\n\n- **Form submission resource** - New `useSubmitFormData` hook, `FormConfig` type, and `skills/form-submission.md` for wiring forms to `/submitFormData`. Configuration (form registration ID + key schema) is provided statically by the operator from the admin tool - no runtime metadata fetch is needed. No-ops with console logging in local/Lovable environments; posts JSON with `credentials: \'include\'` in production. New `/resources/form-data` documentation page with operator-facing wiring instructions.\n- **Form payload shape corrected** - `FormPayload` is now coerced to a `string → string` map via a `toFormPayload` helper before submission. Payload structure matches backend: top-level `formRegistrationId` field with a nested `data` map.\n- **FormConfig stored in app-config.json** - Form configuration now lives alongside `defaultProjectId` in `app-config.json`. The form submission skill references forms by name rather than requiring config to be re-pasted inline.\n\n## April 18, 2026\n\n- **Project role support** - `ProjectRole` and `ProjectRoleProject` types added to `resource-manifest.ts`; `projectRoles` field added to `UserProfile`. Mock fixture updated with 3 sample roles across 2 projects. `hasProjectRole(profile, role, projectId)` added to `userProfileHelpers.ts` with bidirectional substring matching and a `PROJECT_ROLE_ALIASES` table for common annotation role synonyms; `projectId` is always required. `app-config.json` created at project root to store `defaultProjectId`. Skill updated with Rule 5, project role shape documentation, and the app-config protocol.\n- **"Using user data" documentation page** - New page at `/resources/user-data` covering all 5 profile-based conditional rendering patterns: expertise/major, domain, internal user, email domain, and project role. Includes fixture customization guide and a complete multi-pattern example.\n\n## April 17, 2026\n\n- **Profile attribute types corrected** - `ProfileAttributeValue` now typed to match the real API shape: `value: string`, `metadata: string`, `categoryId: string | null`, `required: boolean`, `category: { name: string } | null`. New `ProfileAttributeCategory` interface added to `resource-manifest.ts`.\n- **Mock fixture updated** - `resources/mocks/current-user-profile.json` attributes now include the full `categoryId`, `required`, and `category` fields matching the live API envelope.\n- **Taxonomy-aware profile helpers** - `src/lib/userProfileHelpers.ts` replaced with a full domain/subdomain taxonomy covering all 13 top-level domains and ~60 subdomains. Each canonical value maps to a comprehensive alias list (abbreviations, colloquial shorthand, related terms). Matching uses 3 tiers: exact canonical, substring (3+ char queries), and exact alias - preventing short tokens like `"me"` from spuriously matching unrelated fields.\n- **Profile interpreter skill updated** - `skills/current-user-profile.md` now documents all 4 interpreter rules with alias-aware examples (`"CS"`, `"pre-med"`, `"ML"`, `"orgo"`, `"neuro"`), alias tables for all domains, and an explanation of the 3-char threshold.\n\n## April 16, 2026\n\n- **User profile schema updated** - `subStatus` removed, `handshakeUserId` changed from `string` to `number`, and new `attributes` array added with `{ id, attribute: { value, metadata } }` structure. Mock fixture, types, tests, and docs updated to match the backend schema (learn-apps PR #21).\n- **Current user profile resource** - New `currentUserProfile` resource fetches the authenticated user\'s Handshake profile data (name, email, role, location, onboarding stage). Uses the resource system with fixture data in development and `credentials: \'include\'` cookie auth in production, matching the milestone routing pattern.\n- **useCurrentUserProfile hook** - Convenience hook wrapping `useResource<UserProfile>("currentUserProfile")` with automatic `console.table` logging.\n- **fetchOptions support in resource system** - `ResourceDefinition` now accepts an optional `fetchOptions` field, allowing individual resources to pass credentials or custom headers to `fetch()`.\n- **Current user profile skill** - New agent skill (`skills/current-user-profile.md`) activates whenever a user asks for profile data, email, user identity, or any known profile field.\n- **Resources page updated** - Now dogfoods the user profile resource with a live data table showing fixture data in Lovable and real Handshake data in production.\n\n## April 15, 2026\n\n- **Knowledge check component** - Replaced `MultipleChoiceQuiz` with a new `KnowledgeCheck` component matching the Figma design. Supports multiple questions shown 1 at a time, a progress stepper bar, explicit "Check answer" submission with per-answer feedback, and a results summary screen with score and retry. New markdown syntax uses `<letter>Answer text || Feedback text</letter>` inside numbered question tags. Old single-question quiz format still works via backward-compat adapter.\n- **Progress bar color token** - Added `--interactive-progress` (#00d3d8) color token and Tailwind `interactive.progress` mapping for the quiz stepper bar.\n\n## April 13, 2026\n\n- **Favicon updated** - Default favicon changed from the Lovable icon to the Handshake logo (`src/assets/handshake-logo.png`).\n\n## April 10, 2026\n\n- **Migrate skill rewritten** - `skills/migrate.md` now uses a relocate-first strategy: move the entire existing app into `/old`, install all Citadel Core files fresh into the project root, then read the old app like a source document and scaffold content from it. Replaces the previous in-place surgical merge approach which failed to copy source files reliably in Lovable\'s auto-commit environment. 6 phases: pre-flight with inventory, relocate to `/old`, install Citadel Core, read-and-extract from old JSX, scaffold content, reconcile and validate.\n- **Migration parser** - New `src/lib/migration-parser.ts` converts JSX/TSX files into markdown that feeds into the existing scaffold-parser detection pipeline. Token-based state machine handles nested tags, JSX expressions, and string literals correctly. Extracts routes, classifies components, flags dynamic content, and assigns confidence scores. Pages where the parser fails (`needsManualExtraction`) gracefully fall back to agent-written conversion.\n- **Migration runs continuously** - Removed all inter-phase confirmation gates from `/migrate`. All 6 phases execute as a single uninterrupted operation. Decisions are logged in the migration report for post-migration review instead of blocking execution. QA check (all 17 automated checks from `skills/qa-check.md`) is explicitly mandatory in Phase 6.\n- **Coaching persona refined** - Citadel is now a calm guide that turns design system constraints into educational moments. When an editor asks for something outside best practices, coaching digs for the real need behind the request and proposes an elegant alternative within the system, teaching a small lesson along the way. Replaces the transactional "tip drop" tone with a more collaborative, curiosity-driven voice.\n\n## April 9, 2026\n\n- **Coaching skill** - New `skills/coaching.md` teaches users better workflows as the agent works, rather than blocking execution to lecture. 2 modes: coach-while-doing (non-blocking tips alongside completed work) and coach-then-ask (blocking only for hard constraints, ambiguity, or throwaway work). Covers 18 trigger scenarios across content editing, component matching, design system locks, protected files, unsupported features, and prompt quality.\n\n## April 8, 2026\n\n- **Milestones documentation page** - New template page documenting the full milestone workflow: setting up milestones in the training admin, wiring them with the `/milestone` skill, and choosing a tracking strategy. Includes a "Coming soon" banner while backend infrastructure is finalized.\n- **Milestone system rewrite** - Milestones are now registered in the manifest frontmatter with validation against navigation routes and button IDs. The API simplified to POST `{milestoneId}` to `/earnMilestone` with cookie-based auth (`credentials: \'include\'`), replacing the old localStorage JWT approach.\n- **Milestone validation library** - New `src/lib/milestone-validation.ts` enforces unique IDs, valid visit-page routes, and single-occurrence click button IDs.\n- **Milestone skill updated** - `/milestone` now walks through manifest registration, smart page/element lookup, and handler wiring with full validation.\n- **QA check §17 added** - Milestone integrity checks verify manifest entries match wired handlers and vice versa.\n\n## April 7, 2026\n\n- **QA scope enforcement** - QA checks now skip Citadel core files entirely and only run against user-modifiable files (content, new pages, custom components). A new Template Integrity check (#15) blocks commits that modify protected infrastructure.\n- **Link color** - Links now use `fg/primary` (#121212) instead of the shadcn `--primary` HSL token.\n- **Subheading color** - h2 elements updated from `fg/secondary` to `fg/primary` (#121212).\n- **Footnote color** - Highlight footnotes now use `fg/secondary` (60% opacity) instead of `muted-foreground`.\n- **Section top spacing** - Consistent 32px spacing above every h2, regardless of block position.\n- **Foreground color corrected** - `--foreground` HSL updated from #020817 to #121212 to match the design system\'s `--fg-primary` token. Sidebar and ring tokens updated to match.\n- **Body text size** - Content paragraphs now render at 15px (`text-body-md`) instead of the browser-default 16px.\n- **Subheadings restyled** - h2 elements use `text-body-xl-medium` (19px) with `fg/secondary` color instead of the previous 17px `text-body-lg-medium` in foreground.\n- **font-semibold weight** - Overridden globally to 500 (from Tailwind default 600) to match the design system\'s medium weight.\n- **Highlight colors** - Mark background changed to #FFD999 at 60% opacity; superscript markers now use `fg/primary` instead of yellow-700.\n- **Numbered list indent** - Tightened from `pl-6` to `pl-5` for cleaner number/text alignment.\n- **CodeBlock font size** - Monospace code reduced from 15px to 14px.\n- **Callout and ExamplePair titles** - Downsized from `text-body-lg-medium` (17px) to `text-body-md-medium` (15px).\n- **ExamplePair context note** - Now uses informational (blue) callout style instead of muted background.\n- **ExamplePair icons** - Reduced from 1.5rem to 1.3rem and vertically centered with title text.\n- **Inline code** - Background updated to `bg/secondary` (rgba 6% opacity) with `fg/primary` text color.\n\n## April 3, 2026\n\n- **Highlight marginalia stacked** - Footnotes now appear below the paragraph text instead of in a side-by-side grid, matching the updated Figma spec.\n- **SectionNav hidden on short pages** - The right-rail section navigation no longer appears when there is only 1 heading or when the page is too short for scroll navigation to work (less than 150% of viewport height).\n\n## April 1, 2026\n\nEmbedded assets larger than 5MB can permanently bloat git history - and in Lovable\'s auto-commit environment, there is no opportunity to intercept after the fact. This update adds multi-layer enforcement that prevents oversized or non-image assets from ever being written to disk.\n\n- **Asset size gate in scaffold resolver** - Base64 data URIs are checked for MIME type and decoded size before any file write. Non-image types (audio, video, font) are always rejected; images over 5MB are replaced with a placeholder and logged for the health report.\n- **Blob guard utility** - New `scanForLargeBlobs()` function detects embedded base64 blobs exceeding 5MB in any content file, catching assets that entered through non-scaffold paths.\n- **QA check §13 added** - Asset size limits are now a critical automated check: no file over 5MB, no large data URIs, no embedded audio/video/font.\n- **Asset thresholds codified** - `skills/thresholds.md` now includes an Asset Limits section with the 5MB cap and forbidden MIME types.\n- **StepSequence redesigned** - Smaller dark circles (20px), dashed connecting lines, flat layout without card borders, and tip callouts in the standard info blue. Matches the Figma spec.\n- **Legacy TwoUp type error fixed** - The old markdown-header parser was creating TwoUp blocks with an `items` array instead of flat `heading`/`image`/`caption` fields. Legacy format now read-only; all new content must use YAML fenced blocks.\n- **Image base64 threshold** - Images under 5MB are acceptable as base64 during scaffolding. The 5MB hard limit rejects anything larger.\n- **Asset rule clarified** - The only criterion for rejecting an image is decoded size > 5MB. Whether the image is base64-encoded is irrelevant. Always run `document--parse_document` on uploaded files before scaffolding to extract images into copyable paths. Never assume size from base64 string length - calculate `base64_length * 3 / 4`.\n- **Fenced block format documented** - Multi-item blocks must use numbered `<N>...</N>` tags, not YAML list syntax. Generator functions in `src/lib/component-generators.ts` must be used to produce all fenced blocks.\n- **Lovable knowledge trimmed** - Removed duplicated rules that belong in AGENTS.md. Knowledge file now covers only Lovable-specific essentials: scaffolding workflow, generator requirement, image size rule, and hard constraints.\n- **Post-scaffold workflow made explicit** - All 6 post-scaffold steps (QA, interactive content, rhythmic variety, health report, persona review, changelog) now documented as automatic and mandatory. Do not wait for user prompts between steps.\n- **ContentBlockRenderer template fixed** - Scaffold skill showed the wrong API (`block={block} allBlocks={blocks}` per-item). Corrected to `blocks={blocks}` as a single prop.\n\n## March 31, 2026\n\nThe scaffolding system now verifies that no source content gets silently dropped during conversion. Every fenced-block generator lives in a single module with roundtrip tests, so the output format is deterministic and parseable. A new post-scaffold health report catches empty or malformed blocks before they reach fellows.\n\n- **Scaffold health report** - New `verifyScaffoldHealth()` utility validates every content block and flags empty fields, dense sections, and missing source segments.\n- **Thresholds consolidated** - All numeric thresholds (text density, table formatting, compaction) live in a single reference file instead of being scattered across skills.\n- **QA checklist restructured** - 14 automated checks plus a 3-persona review that replaces the former holistic review.\n- **Component generators module** - All 15 generators centralized in `src/lib/component-generators.ts` with content fidelity verification.\n- **Scaffold skill streamlined** - Redundant inline examples removed; generators are the single source of truth.\n- **Changelog page added** - New default page that tracks content changes after publication.\n- **Image blocks flattened** - Image fenced blocks now use flat YAML instead of numbered tags, matching the rest of the single-item components.\n\n## March 30, 2026\n\nThe template was rewritten to document itself - every page now demonstrates the technique it describes. This update also introduced several new content block types and formalized the rules around visual variety so that scaffolded projects avoid walls of unbroken text.\n\n- **Template rewritten as self-documentation** - Pages demonstrate the features they describe. Accordion styling and scroll-to-top fixed.\n- **Custom-tokens block type** - Projects can define bespoke components that read content from `custom-tokens` fenced blocks.\n- **Visual variety rules** - Interactive content awareness, rhythmic variety step, and fellow experience review added to scaffold and QA.\n- **Example-pair format simplified** - Flattened to single-item YAML. Live video and embed demos added to the component catalog.\n- **Scaffold skill hardened** - JSON-to-YAML migration completed. Page and route generation documented.\n\n## March 19, 2026\n\nThe style system got a formal specification, and all page titles now render in sentence case automatically. This was a big cleanup pass that touched typography, spacing, sidebar labels, and heading behavior across the entire template.\n\n- **17 style rules codified** - Sidebar labels, spacing, heading casing, and related patterns documented.\n- **Sentence-casing enforced on page titles** - `toSentenceCase()` applied to all h1 headings with step-prefix support.\n- **Button-group content block** - New block type for grouped actions. SectionNav scroll tracking fixed.\n- **Tips and techniques page** - Template-only authoring guide for content authors.\n- **Button hover fixed** - Overlay uses transparent white on dark backgrounds instead of replacing the color.\n\n## March 18, 2026\n\nA small but noticeable fix for sidebar labels that wrap to 2 lines - they were getting clipped instead of receiving proper padding.\n\n- **Sub-item height override fixed** - 2-line sidebar labels now get correct padding.\n\n## March 17, 2026\n\nIframe-based embeds are now a first-class content block, so you can drop in YouTube, Vimeo, or Loom videos without writing custom HTML. The sidebar also got a round of spacing and prefix cleanup.\n\n- **Embed content block** - New `embed` block type for iframe-based media.\n- **Sidebar sub-item spacing fixed** - Redundant numeric prefixes stripped.\n- **Chrome menu customization** - Native 3-dot menu hidden and gear icon repositioned.\n',Q1=`---
title: Component development
---

Component development is the path for contributing new reusable interactive components to Citadel itself. Use it when a learning pattern should become available to every Citadel project, not when 1 training needs a one-off custom display.

\`\`\`callout
variant: important
title: Template maintenance only
content:
Component development changes reusable Citadel infrastructure. Use this workflow when the operator has explicitly entered template-maintenance mode and the proposed interaction should be available across projects.
\`\`\`

## Choose the right path

Most component requests do not need new Citadel infrastructure. First decide whether the interaction is project-specific, already covered by an existing component, or genuinely reusable.

\`\`\`example-pair
good: "We need a reusable ScenarioLab component because several trainings ask learners to read a situation, choose a response, compare consequences, and reflect. Checklist and Quiz do not support that judgment loop."
bad: "We need a new card component because this page would look better with cards."
context: New components add parser, generator, rendering, scaffold, testing, and documentation responsibilities. A visual preference alone is not enough; the component needs a distinct learning behavior.
\`\`\`

| Need | Use |
| ---- | --- |
| 1 project needs bespoke layout or branding-safe custom display | \`skills/custom-component.md\` with \`custom-tokens\` |
| Existing Citadel component fits with better content | Use the existing fenced-block component |
| Existing component is close but missing a reusable behavior | Consider extending the existing component |
| Multiple Citadel projects need a new interaction pattern | \`skills/component-development.md\` |

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: Start smaller when possible
content:
If the learning goal can be met by clearer content, a stronger existing component, or a project-specific custom component, choose that path first. Reusable infrastructure is worthwhile only when the pattern will repeat across projects.
\`\`\`

## What a reusable component must define

A reusable Citadel component is more than a React file. It needs a complete authoring, rendering, scaffolding, and maintenance contract.

\`\`\`checklist
title: Component definition checklist
invitation: Confirm each contract before implementation starts.
completionPrompt: If any item is uncertain, pause and resolve it with the operator before coding.
<1>
id: rendering-contract
label: Define how the component renders in code
hint: Include props, parsed block type, empty state, accessibility behavior, export, and ContentBlockRenderer case.
</1>
<2>
id: fenced-block-contract
label: Define the fenced-block syntax
hint: Specify the fence type, fields, required values, optional values, and whether the component is single-item or numbered multi-item.
</2>
<3>
id: usage-contract
label: Define when to use and when not to use it
hint: Compare against existing components and name the pedagogical behavior the new component supports.
</3>
<4>
id: scaffold-contract
label: Define how scaffolding recognizes it
hint: Document headings, tables, repeated labels, list patterns, keywords, and ambiguous cases that should trigger a question.
</4>
<5>
id: design-system-contract
label: Define how it uses the design system
hint: Use semantic tokens, approved typography classes, standard spacing, and shadcn/ui primitives where appropriate.
</5>
<6>
id: skill-contract
label: Create the attendant component skill
hint: Future agents need a skill file that explains purpose, authoring format, detection cues, pedagogy notes, and QA checks.
</6>
\`\`\`

## Contribution workflow

The skill deliberately starts with conversation. The goal is to prevent component sprawl and protect Citadel's content model.

\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: Start from the latest Citadel
content: When using \`skills/component-development.md\`, create or use a \`feature-[feature-name]\` branch and rebase from the newest Citadel before implementation. The branch rule applies to component-development work, not ordinary documentation maintenance.
</1>
<2>
title: Run intake
content: Ask what learner action the component supports, which existing component is closest, whether the component is reusable or project-specific, what source patterns scaffolding should recognize, and what interaction behavior is required.
</2>
<3>
title: Analyze overlap
content: Compare the proposal against KnowledgeCheck, NoteCardStepper, TwoUp, Checklist, ContentImage, Callout, ExamplePair, AccordionSection, StepSequence, Flowchart, Highlight, CodeBlock, VideoPlayer, and EmbedPlayer.
</3>
<4>
title: Write a proposal
content: Document the pedagogical purpose, fenced-block syntax, content schema, rendering behavior, scaffold recognition rules, files expected to change, and tests expected.
</4>
<5>
title: Implement the full contract
content: Build the component only after the proposal is approved. Update parser, generator, renderer, exports, scaffold detection if needed, component map, attendant skill, and tests together.
</5>
<6>
title: Rebase and validate
content: Rebase from main again before final QA. Run generator/parser round-trip tests, content QA when documentation changes, route checks when pages or routes change, and workflow evals before push.
</6>
\`\`\`

## Fenced-block design

The fenced block is the authoring API. It should be readable, stable, and generated through \`src/lib/component-generators.ts\`.

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Treat syntax as a public interface
content:
Once a fenced-block format ships, future content and scaffold output depend on it. Prefer simple fields and numbered items that authors can read without knowing React.
\`\`\`

Single-item components use key/value fields:

\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
[three backticks]component-type
title: Optional title
content:
Markdown body content.
[three backticks]
\`\`\`

Multi-item components use numbered \`<N>...</N>\` tags:

\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
[three backticks]component-type
<1>
id: item-1
title: First item
content:
First item content.
</1>
<2>
id: item-2
title: Second item
content:
Second item content.
</2>
[three backticks]
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: important
title: Generators are part of the contract
content:
Do not make agents hand-write complex component syntax. Add a generator to \`src/lib/component-generators.ts\`, test it, and use it from scaffold or sync code. The generator output should parse back into the same component block shape.
\`\`\`

## Files a contributor usually touches

A first-class component change often spans multiple layers. Do not update 1 layer and leave the others stale.

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
title: Rendering layer
content:
Create \`src/components/interactive/[ComponentName].tsx\`, export it from \`src/components/interactive/index.ts\`, and add a case to \`src/components/ContentBlockRenderer.tsx\`. The component should receive parsed content as props and should not contain learner-facing instructional copy.
</1>
<2>
title: Content model layer
content:
Update \`src/lib/content.ts\` with the block type, known fields, parser behavior, and fallback handling. If the component has repeated items, use numbered tag parsing unless a graph-like structure truly needs YAML.
</2>
<3>
title: Generator layer
content:
Add \`generate[ComponentName]FencedBlock()\` to \`src/lib/component-generators.ts\`. It should emit valid syntax, handle empty input gracefully, and be covered by generator tests.
</3>
<4>
title: Scaffold layer
content:
If source documents should auto-detect this pattern, update \`src/lib/scaffold-parser.ts\` and any converter or sync paths that need to emit the new fenced block. Ambiguous patterns should ask the operator instead of flattening possible interactivity into prose.
</4>
<5>
title: Skill and documentation layer
content:
Create \`skills/[component-name].md\`, update \`skills/component-authoring.md\`, and update template documentation when contributors or operators need to understand the new capability.
</5>
<6>
title: Tests and validation layer
content:
Add or update parser, generator, renderer, scaffold detection, and content QA tests as appropriate. At minimum, prove that generated fenced-block syntax round-trips through \`parseContent()\`.
</6>
\`\`\`

## Design-system requirements

A component must feel like Citadel before it becomes Citadel. Study 2 or 3 existing interactive components before implementing a new 1.

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Match the system, not the screenshot
content:
Use semantic color tokens, approved type classes, and existing spacing rhythm. Avoid hardcoded hex values, raw RGB/HSL, non-semantic Tailwind colors, inline color styles, decorative-only visuals, and one-off layout tricks.
\`\`\`

Recommended references:

- \`src/components/interactive/Checklist.tsx\`
- \`src/components/interactive/StepSequence.tsx\`
- \`src/components/interactive/Callout.tsx\`
- \`src/components/interactive/ExamplePair.tsx\`
- \`skills/style.md\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: important
title: Design review is functional review
content:
A component that bypasses tokens or spacing rules is harder to maintain and harder for learners to trust. Visual consistency is part of the contribution contract, not polish after the fact.
\`\`\`

## Scaffolding recognition

Scaffold detection should describe source patterns, not vibes. A contributor should be able to explain why a source block maps to the new component.

\`\`\`example-pair
good: Convert a repeated "Situation / Choice / Consequence / Reflection" table into the new component because the source exposes a stable item structure.
bad: Convert any paragraph that feels scenario-like because it mentions a decision.
context: Scaffold detection needs visible evidence - a heading, table shape, repeated label, or list pattern. If the evidence is weak, the agent should ask instead of guessing.
\`\`\`

| Detection cue | Example question |
| ------------- | ---------------- |
| Heading pattern | Does a heading like "Scenario", "Decision", or "Practice case" reliably signal this component? |
| Table shape | Do repeated columns such as Context, Choice, Consequence, and Reflection imply the interaction? |
| List structure | Are items independent tasks, ordered steps, alternatives, or nested decision points? |
| Repeated labels | Does the source repeat a stable field group that should become 1 component item? |
| Ambiguous case | Should the agent ask before converting this pattern? |

\`\`\`callout
variant: warning
title: Ask when detection is ambiguous
content:
Interactive-eligible content should never be silently rendered as plain prose, but uncertain detection should not become aggressive conversion. Ask the operator to choose the best component, adapt an existing one, or proceed with the new component proposal.
\`\`\`

## Prompt wizard

Use the wizard below to turn the contribution idea into a structured prompt for \`skills/component-development.md\`. The generated prompt gives the agent enough context to start the intake conversation, compare existing components, and produce a proposal before coding.

\`\`\`custom-tokens
component-development-wizard: true
wizard-title: Component development prompt wizard
wizard-description: Answer the planning questions, then copy the generated prompt into Claude, Pi, or another Citadel-aware agent.
wizard-component-name-label: Proposed component name
wizard-component-name-hint: Use a clear reusable name. It can be tentative.
wizard-component-name-placeholder: ScenarioLab
wizard-learner-action-label: What should the learner do?
wizard-learner-action-hint: Name the behavior, judgment, reflection, or practice the component supports.
wizard-learner-action-placeholder: Learners read a realistic situation, choose a response, compare consequences, and reflect.
wizard-scope-label: Scope
wizard-scope-hint: Decide whether this belongs in Citadel core or one project.
wizard-select-placeholder: Choose one
wizard-scope-reusable: Reusable Citadel component
wizard-scope-custom: Project-specific custom component
wizard-scope-unsure: Unsure - analyze first
wizard-purpose-label: Pedagogical purpose
wizard-purpose-hint: Pick the closest learning pattern.
wizard-purpose-reflection: Reflection
wizard-purpose-sequencing: Sequencing
wizard-purpose-comparison: Comparison
wizard-purpose-decision: Decision-making
wizard-purpose-practice: Practice/application
wizard-purpose-reference: Reference lookup
wizard-closest-label: Closest existing component
wizard-closest-hint: Name the approved component that almost fits.
wizard-closest-placeholder: Quiz, Checklist, StepSequence, Flowchart, Highlight...
wizard-gap-label: Why does the existing component fail?
wizard-gap-hint: Explain the missing behavior, schema, or interaction.
wizard-gap-placeholder: Quiz checks correctness, but this needs judgment practice without a single right answer.
wizard-source-label: Source patterns scaffolding should recognize
wizard-source-hint: List headings, tables, repeated labels, or structural cues.
wizard-source-placeholder: Tables with Situation / Choice / Consequence / Reflection columns; headings called Practice scenario.
wizard-interaction-label: Required interaction behavior
wizard-interaction-hint: Describe state, choices, reveal behavior, progress, or reflection.
wizard-interaction-placeholder: Learner selects an option, sees consequence notes, then writes or reads a reflection prompt.
wizard-visual-label: Visual or layout behavior
wizard-visual-hint: Describe layout needs while staying within the design system.
wizard-visual-placeholder: Card layout with muted context, primary action, and reflection panel using semantic tokens.
wizard-notes-label: Additional constraints or open questions
wizard-notes-hint: Include accessibility, data, testing, or SME questions.
wizard-notes-placeholder: Needs keyboard support and no hardcoded learner-facing text.
wizard-output-title: Generated prompt
wizard-output-empty: Fill out the wizard fields to generate a component-development prompt.
wizard-copy-label: Copy prompt
wizard-copy-confirmation: Copied
wizard-prompt-opening: Use skills/component-development.md to help me plan a reusable Citadel component. Do not implement immediately; first run the intake, overlap analysis, and component proposal workflow.
wizard-prompt-closing: Please confirm whether this should be a first-class Citadel component, an extension of an existing component, or a project-specific custom component. If it should proceed, propose the fenced-block schema, rendering contract, scaffold detection cues, design-system plan, attendant skill, files to change, and tests to add.
wizard-output-component-name: Proposed component name
wizard-output-learner-action: Learner action
wizard-output-closest-component: Closest existing component
wizard-output-existing-gap: Why the existing component fails
wizard-output-scope: Scope
wizard-output-purpose: Pedagogical purpose
wizard-output-source-patterns: Source patterns for scaffold detection
wizard-output-interaction: Required interaction behavior
wizard-output-visual: Visual or layout behavior
wizard-output-notes: Additional constraints or open questions
\`\`\`

## Final review checklist

Before a reusable component contribution is ready for review, the contributor should be able to answer every item below.

\`\`\`checklist
title: Review readiness
invitation: Use this before opening a pull request or asking for final review.
completionPrompt: If the checklist exposes a gap, resolve the contract before merging.
<1>
id: branch-ready
label: The work was done on \`feature-[feature-name]\` and rebased from latest main
</1>
<2>
id: overlap-ready
label: Existing components were compared and the new component is justified
</2>
<3>
id: pedagogy-ready
label: The component has a named pedagogical purpose and is not decorative
</3>
<4>
id: content-ready
label: Learner-facing text comes from markdown fenced blocks, not JSX literals
</4>
<5>
id: parser-generator-ready
label: Generator output parses back into the expected \`ContentBlock\` shape
</5>
<6>
id: design-ready
label: The component uses semantic tokens, approved typography, and standard spacing
</6>
<7>
id: scaffold-ready
label: Scaffold detection rules and ambiguous cases are documented
</7>
<8>
id: skill-ready
label: The attendant component skill exists and explains usage, format, detection, pedagogy, and QA
</8>
<9>
id: tests-ready
label: Parser, generator, renderer, and scaffold behavior are tested where applicable
</9>
\`\`\`
`,K1=`---
title: Component catalog
---

Every interactive element in a Citadel app is authored as a fenced code block inside a markdown file. You write structured fields between triple backticks with a type marker, and the ContentBlockRenderer turns it into a live component.

This page demonstrates every component type with a live example followed by the syntax that produces it. Before reading through each component, use this guide to find the right 1 for your content - then jump to its section for the full syntax.

## Choosing the right component

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: When in doubt, use a component
content:
A wall of text is a failure of communication. If you are writing a fourth consecutive paragraph without a component, stop and ask: what component would make this clearer?
\`\`\`

| Content pattern       | Component              |
| --------------------- | ---------------------- |
| Key term to annotate  | **Highlight**          |
| Visual demonstration  | **TwoUp** or **Image** |
| Decision logic        | **Flowchart**          |
| Tasks to complete     | **Checklist**          |
| Procedural steps      | **StepSequence**       |
| Good vs. bad approach | **ExamplePair**        |
| Dense reference, FAQ  | **Accordion**          |
| Key aside             | **Callout**            |
| Comprehension check   | **Quiz**               |
| Audit or mark/do-not-mark decision | **FlagAndFix** |
| Requirement matching  | **CoverageMatcher**    |
| Grouping or taxonomy practice | **GroupingDrill** |
| Code to copy          | **CodeBlock**          |
| Video file            | **Video**              |
| External embed        | **Embed**              |
| Navigation buttons    | **ButtonGroup**        |
| Custom component data | **CustomTokens**       |

## Callout

4 variants for inline messaging: \`note\`, \`warning\`, \`tip\`, \`important\`. Each gets a colored background and a title. Keep callouts concise - 1 to 3 sentences.

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: When to use callouts
content:
Use callouts to surface information that interrupts the main flow - a prerequisite the reader must know, a shortcut worth highlighting, or a risk they should avoid. If the information fits naturally in a paragraph, keep it there.
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: When to use callouts
content:
Use callouts to surface information that interrupts the main flow - a prerequisite the reader must know, a shortcut worth highlighting, or a risk they should avoid. If the information fits naturally in a paragraph, keep it there.
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Variants: \`note\` (general context), \`warning\` (pitfalls), \`tip\` (shortcuts), \`important\` (hard prerequisites).

## Code block

Syntax-highlighted code with a copy button. Specify the language for proper highlighting.

\`\`\`code-block
language: bash
content:
npm install
npm run dev
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`code-block
language: bash
content:
npm install
npm run dev
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Two-up

Image and caption pairs for visual storytelling - before/after comparisons, annotated screenshots, or any content where seeing and reading reinforce each other.

\`\`\`two-up
heading: Visual demonstration
image: /placeholder.svg
caption: The two-up layout pairs an image with explanatory text. Every image in your source document becomes a TwoUp during scaffolding.
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`two-up
heading: Visual demonstration
image: /placeholder.svg
caption: The two-up layout pairs an image with explanatory text.
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Checklist

Interactive task lists with progress tracking. Learners check items off as they complete them. Optional hints provide guidance without cluttering the main label.

\`\`\`checklist
title: Component review
invitation: Work through each step as you learn the component system.
completionPrompt: Nice work - what component would you use first?
<1>
id: read
label: Read the component description
hint: The description explains when and why to use the component.
</1>
<2>
id: syntax
label: Study the syntax block
hint: Copy the fenced block pattern and modify it for your content.
</2>
<3>
id: test
label: Test in the browser
hint: Save the markdown file and check that the component renders correctly.
</3>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`checklist
title: Component review
invitation: Work through each step as you learn the component system.
completionPrompt: Nice work - what component would you use first?
<1>
id: read
label: Read the component description
hint: The description explains when and why to use the component.
</1>
<2>
id: syntax
label: Study the syntax block
hint: Copy the fenced block pattern and modify it for your content.
</2>
<3>
id: test
label: Test in the browser
hint: Save the markdown file and check that the component renders correctly.
</3>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Title, invitation, and completionPrompt are optional top-level fields above the numbered items.

## Example pair

Side-by-side good/bad comparisons with context explaining why 1 approach is preferred. Use for do/don't patterns, before/after rewrites, or approach contrasts.

\`\`\`example-pair
good: Edit the markdown file at \`src/content/page-name.md\` to change what the page says.
bad: Prompt the AI: "Change the text on the page to say..." - this embeds text in component code.
context: Content-structure separation is a hard rule. All text must live in markdown files so it can be edited, scaffolded, and synced independently of the code.
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`example-pair
good: Edit the markdown file at \`src/content/page-name.md\` to change what the page says.
bad: Prompt the AI: "Change the text on the page to say..." - this embeds text in component code.
context: Content-structure separation is a hard rule.
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Step sequence

Numbered vertical steps for procedural instructions. Each step has a title, content, and an optional tip.

\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: Write the content
content: Author your text and component blocks in a markdown file.
tip: Start with the frontmatter title before writing body content.
</1>
<2>
title: Add to the manifest
content: Register the page in \`src/content/manifest.md\` with its route and type.
</2>
<3>
title: Create the page component
content: Add a \`.tsx\` file in \`src/pages/\` that imports the markdown, parses it, and renders blocks.
</3>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: Write the content
content: Author your text and component blocks in a markdown file.
tip: Start with the frontmatter title before writing body content.
</1>
<2>
title: Add to the manifest
content: Register the page in \`src/content/manifest.md\` with its route and type.
</2>
<3>
title: Create the page component
content: Add a \`.tsx\` file in \`src/pages/\` that imports the markdown, parses it, and renders blocks.
</3>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Accordion

Expandable sections for supplementary content, FAQ, glossaries, or dense reference material that not every reader needs immediately.

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
title: When should I use an accordion?
content:
Use accordions for **supplementary detail** that not every reader needs. If the content is essential to understanding the page, put it in the main flow instead.
</1>
<2>
title: Can I nest markdown inside?
content:
Yes - accordion content supports full markdown including **bold**, *italic*, \`code\`, and [links](https://example.com).
</2>
<3>
title: How many items should I include?
content:
Minimum 2 items. There is no hard maximum, but more than 8 items suggests the content should be split across pages or restructured.
</3>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`accordion
<1>
title: When should I use an accordion?
content:
Use accordions for **supplementary detail** that not every reader needs. If the content is essential to understanding the page, put it in the main flow instead.
</1>
<2>
title: Can I nest markdown inside?
content:
Yes - accordion content supports full markdown including **bold**, *italic*, \`code\`, and [links](https://example.com).
</2>
<3>
title: How many items should I include?
content:
Minimum 2 items. There is no hard maximum, but more than 8 items suggests the content should be split across pages or restructured.
</3>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Flowchart

Visual decision flows with nodes and edges. Flowcharts use YAML syntax because the node-and-edge structure reads more naturally in YAML.

\`\`\`flowchart
nodes:
  - id: start
    label: "New content?"
    type: decision
  - id: scaffold
    label: "Run /scaffold"
    type: action
  - id: manual
    label: "Edit markdown directly"
    type: action
  - id: qa
    label: "Run QA check"
    type: action
  - id: done
    label: "Deploy"
    type: end
edges:
  - from: start
    to: scaffold
    label: "Yes, from scratch"
  - from: start
    to: manual
    label: "No, editing existing"
  - from: scaffold
    to: qa
  - from: manual
    to: qa
  - from: qa
    to: done
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: yaml
content:
\`\`\`flowchart
nodes:
  - id: start
    label: "Question?"
    type: decision
  - id: action1
    label: "Do something"
    type: action
  - id: end
    label: "Done"
    type: end
edges:
  - from: start
    to: action1
    label: "Yes"
  - from: action1
    to: end
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Node types: \`start\`, \`end\`, \`decision\`, \`action\`.

## Highlight

Annotated text with numbered marginalia. Use for key terms, definitions, or passages that benefit from side commentary.

\`\`\`highlight
When we talk about {(1) content-structure separation}, we mean that all text lives in markdown files and all rendering happens in components. The two never mix - {(2) components never contain hardcoded text}.

marginalia:
1: The foundational architecture pattern of every Citadel app
2: This is enforced by the QA checklist and agent rules
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`highlight
When we talk about {(1) content-structure separation}, we mean that all text lives in markdown files and all rendering happens in components. The 2 never mix - {(2) components never contain hardcoded text}.

marginalia:
1: The foundational architecture pattern of every Citadel app
2: This is enforced by the QA checklist and agent rules
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Use \`{(N) text}\` to mark phrases in the body. The \`marginalia:\` line divides body text from annotations. Limit to 2–4 annotations per block.

## Knowledge check

Multi-question assessments with a progress stepper, per-answer feedback, and a results summary. Each question is shown 1 at a time. The learner selects an answer, clicks "Check answer" to see feedback, then advances.

\`\`\`quiz
title: Knowledge check

<1>
question: Which component best fits a list of tasks a learner should complete?
body: The learner needs to work through several action items and track which ones are done.
correct: b
<a>Accordion || Accordions are for reference material. Tasks need tracking.</a>
<b>Checklist || Checklists let readers track their progress through action items.</b>
<c>Step sequence || Step sequences show procedural order but do not track completion.</c>
</1>

<2>
question: When should you use a callout instead of inline text?
correct: a
<a>When the info interrupts the main flow || Callouts surface prerequisites, shortcuts, or risks that break the reading flow.</a>
<b>Whenever you want emphasis || Use bold or italic for emphasis. Callouts are for flow-interrupting information.</b>
</2>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`quiz
title: Knowledge check

<1>
question: Which component fits a task list?
body: The learner needs to track several action items.
correct: b
<a>Accordion || Not quite.</a>
<b>Checklist || Correct!</b>
</1>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Each \`<N>\` tag is a question. Answers use \`<letter>Answer text || Feedback text</letter>\`. Add optional body text with \`((double parens))\`: \`<a>Title ((body text)) || Feedback</a>\`. The title renders semi-bold; the body renders regular weight below it. Feedback and body are both optional. The \`correct:\` field specifies the letter of the right answer. The \`title:\` field is optional (defaults to "Knowledge check"). The progress stepper bar is hidden for single-question quizzes.

## Note card stepper

A card-based journey that presents content 1 card at a time with a progress bar. Each card has a title, body content, and an optional reflection prompt. Use for concept exploration, guided self-assessment, or content the learner should sit with rather than skim.

\`\`\`notecard
<1>
id: card-1
title: Content-structure separation
content: All text lives in markdown files. Components render that text - they never contain it. This means you can rewrite every word in your app without touching a single line of code.
reflection: Where in your current workflow does content get mixed into code? What would it take to separate them?
</1>
<2>
id: card-2
title: The scaffold skill
content: The scaffold converts your source markdown into structured pages with interactive components. It detects patterns - images become TwoUps, action lists become Checklists - so you don't have to author every component manually.
</2>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`notecard
<1>
id: card-1
title: Card title
content: Card body text.
reflection: Optional reflection prompt.
</1>
<2>
id: card-2
title: Second card
content: Body text.
</2>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Each \`<N>\` tag is a card. Required fields: \`id\`, \`title\`, \`content\`. \`reflection\` is optional - when present, it appears below the content in a visually distinct style, inviting the learner to connect the idea to their own experience. The \`journeyTitle\` (rendered above the progress bar) is set in the page component.

## Image

Captioned images with alt text. Use for standalone visuals that do not need the side-by-side TwoUp layout.

\`\`\`image
src: /placeholder.svg
alt: A placeholder demonstrating the image component
caption: Images render at full content width with an optional caption below.
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`image
src: /placeholder.svg
alt: A placeholder demonstrating the image component
caption: Images render at full content width with an optional caption below.
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Video

Direct video file playback for \`.mp4\`, \`.webm\`, \`.ogg\`, or \`.mov\` files. Use when you have a video file hosted directly (not on YouTube or Vimeo).

\`\`\`video
url: https://www.w3schools.com/html/mov_bbb.mp4
title: Sample video
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`video
url: https://www.w3schools.com/html/mov_bbb.mp4
title: Sample video
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Use the \`video\` block for direct file URLs. For platform embeds (YouTube, Vimeo, Loom), use the \`embed\` block instead.

## Embed

Iframe embeds for platform URLs - YouTube, Vimeo, Loom, Google Docs, and any other embeddable URL.

\`\`\`embed
url: https://www.youtube.com/embed/dQw4w9WgXcQ
title: Sample embed
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`embed
url: https://www.youtube.com/embed/dQw4w9WgXcQ
title: Sample embed
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Button group

Rendered buttons from content. Each button specifies a variant (\`default\` or \`outline\`) and text, with an optional \`href\` for navigation.

\`\`\`button-group
<1>
variant: default
text: Primary action
</1>
<2>
variant: outline
text: Secondary action
</2>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`button-group
<1>
variant: default
text: Primary action
</1>
<2>
variant: outline
text: Secondary action
</2>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Only 2 button variants exist: \`default\` (primary) and \`outline\` (secondary). 1 primary CTA per page.

## Custom tokens

Key-value pairs that custom components read via the \`useCustomTokens\` hook. Use this when you build a project-specific component that needs content from the markdown file. See the \`/custom-component\` skill for the full workflow.

\`\`\`custom-tokens
hero-heading: Welcome to the program
hero-subtext: Everything you need to get started
cta-label: Begin learning
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`custom-tokens
hero-heading: Welcome to the program
hero-subtext: Everything you need to get started
cta-label: Begin learning
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Custom tokens render nothing on their own - they are data blocks consumed by custom components. Multiple \`custom-tokens\` blocks on a page are merged.

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Reusable components are template work
content:
If a new interaction should become part of Citadel for every project, use \`skills/component-development.md\` instead of the project-specific custom component path. This is template-maintenance work and requires explicit operator confirmation because it changes core parser, generator, renderer, documentation, and scaffold behavior. The [Component development](/content/component-development) guide explains the full contributor workflow.
\`\`\`


`,i_=`---
title: Extended example components
---

Extended example components turn critique, scoring, matching, grouping, and visual comparison patterns into active practice. Use them when the learner needs to make a judgment before seeing the model reasoning.

## Scenario reveal

Use ScenarioReveal when the learner should inspect a scenario before seeing the critique.

\`\`\`scenario-reveal
scenario:
A rubric says, "The chart should look professional."
critique:
"Professional" is a judgment word. Two reviewers may apply different standards because the criterion does not name visible properties.
improved:
The chart uses one accent color for the highlighted series, neutral gray for other series, and visible axis labels.
keyLesson:
Replace judgment words with verifiable properties.
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`scenario-reveal
scenario:
A rubric says, "The chart should look professional."
critique:
"Professional" is a judgment word. 2 reviewers may apply different standards.
improved:
The chart uses 1 accent color for the highlighted series.
keyLesson:
Replace judgment words with verifiable properties.
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Flag and fix

Use FlagAndFix for audit practice: the learner marks each item as acceptable or needing revision, then compares against the model. For annotation or review tasks, customize the decision labels so the buttons match the learner's actual judgment.

\`\`\`flag-and-fix
instruction: Decide which rubric items need revision.
keyLesson: Verifiable items describe what a reviewer can check directly.
flagLabel: Needs revision
okLabel: Accept item
buttonOrder: flag-ok
<1>
id: a
text: The layout looks clean.
isProblematic: true
reason: "Clean" is subjective without visible criteria.
improved: The layout uses consistent 16px spacing between cards.
</1>
<2>
id: b
text: The page contains exactly three pricing cards.
isProblematic: false
reason: This is directly verifiable.
</2>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`flag-and-fix
instruction: Decide which rubric items need revision.
keyLesson: Verifiable items describe what a reviewer can check directly.
flagLabel: Needs revision
okLabel: Accept item
buttonOrder: flag-ok
<1>
id: a
text: The layout looks clean.
isProblematic: true
reason: "Clean" is subjective without visible criteria.
improved: The layout uses consistent 16px spacing between cards.
</1>
<2>
id: b
text: The page contains exactly 3 pricing cards.
isProblematic: false
reason: This is directly verifiable.
</2>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Good neutral bad table

Use GoodNeutralBadTable for compact comparison across quality bands. For operational tasks, use rows as axes such as label specificity, boundary precision, coverage completeness, and granularity.

\`\`\`good-neutral-bad-table
<1>
topic: Specificity
good: "The hero image is at least 1600px wide and uses a single subject in focus."
neutral: "The hero image is large and clearly shows the subject."
bad: "The hero image looks good."
</1>
<2>
topic: Verifiability
good: "Body text is exactly 16px on desktop, 15px on mobile."
neutral: "Body text is comfortable to read on both desktop and mobile."
bad: "Body text feels readable."
</2>
<3>
topic: Coverage
good: "The completion includes all three requested pricing tiers."
neutral: "The completion includes a pricing section."
bad: "The completion has enough pricing detail."
</3>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`good-neutral-bad-table
<1>
topic: Specificity
good: "The hero image is at least 1600px wide and uses a single subject in focus."
neutral: "The hero image is large and clearly shows the subject."
bad: "The hero image looks good."
</1>
<2>
topic: Verifiability
good: "Body text is exactly 16px on desktop, 15px on mobile."
neutral: "Body text is comfortable to read on both desktop and mobile."
bad: "Body text feels readable."
</2>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Before and after slider

Use BeforeAfterSlider when the difference between 2 visuals is the lesson. This example uses 2 very similar images; the revised version adds only a subtle green alignment cue.

\`\`\`before-after-slider
before: /assets/slider-before.png
after: /assets/slider-after.png
beforeLabel: Original
afterLabel: Revised
alt: Subtle visual comparison with one small alignment cue added
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`before-after-slider
before: /assets/slider-before.png
after: /assets/slider-after.png
beforeLabel: Original
afterLabel: Revised
alt: Subtle visual comparison with 1 small alignment cue added
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Zone highlighter

Use ZoneHighlighter when learners need to find exact phrases inside a passage. You can also use it as a timeline-transcript idiom: put timestamps in the text and make each zone a timestamped cue. Use that documentation pattern before adding a timeline-specific component schema.

\`\`\`zone-highlighter
instruction: Highlight the phrases in the prompt that would create execution ambiguity for two different employees.
text: Make the report look professional. Add a few key visualizations and ensure the executive summary is well-written. Anything that seems off should be cleaned up before submission.
keyLesson: Vague language clusters around three patterns: undefined quantities ("a few"), feeling-based descriptions ("well-written"), and open-ended catch-alls ("anything that seems off").
<1>
phrase: look professional
explanation: "Professional" is a judgment word - different employees apply different standards.
</1>
<2>
phrase: a few key visualizations
explanation: Quantity is undefined and "key" is subjective. Specify a count and a selection rule.
</2>
<3>
phrase: well-written
explanation: No verifiable property - anchor to length, structure, or tone-specific properties.
</3>
<4>
phrase: Anything that seems off
explanation: Open-ended catch-all that two employees would draw the line on differently.
</4>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`zone-highlighter
instruction: Highlight the phrases in the prompt that would create execution ambiguity for 2 different employees.
text: Make the report look professional. Add a few key visualizations and ensure the executive summary is well-written. Anything that seems off should be cleaned up before submission.
keyLesson: Vague language clusters around 3 patterns: undefined quantities, feeling-based descriptions, and open-ended catch-alls.
<1>
phrase: look professional
explanation: "Professional" is a judgment word - different employees apply different standards.
</1>
<2>
phrase: a few key visualizations
explanation: Quantity is undefined and "key" is subjective.
</2>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Required: \`text\` and at least 1 numbered zone with \`phrase\` and \`explanation\`. Optional: \`instruction\`, \`keyLesson\`, \`falseFlagExplanation\`.

## Coverage matcher

Use CoverageMatcher when learners need to connect requirements to submitted outputs. It also fits segmentation-style practice: requirements are the required segments or actions, submitted items are candidate learner outputs, and feedback identifies missing, spurious, and acceptable items.

\`\`\`coverage-matcher
prompt: Build a landing page for a coffee subscription service. Include three tiers, a testimonial section, and a checkout CTA.
keyLesson: Number every distinct requirement in the prompt and confirm each maps to at least one item. Anything in the submission without a prompt anchor is unjustified scope.
<1>
kind: requirement
id: R1
text: Three subscription tiers
coverageNote: Covered by item B with all three tiers visible.
modelConnection: B
</1>
<2>
kind: requirement
id: R2
text: Testimonial section
coverageNote: No item addresses customer testimonials.
modelConnection: null
</2>
<3>
kind: requirement
id: R3
text: Checkout CTA
coverageNote: Covered by item A.
modelConnection: A
</3>
<4>
kind: item
id: A
text: Hero with primary CTA button
note: Maps to checkout CTA.
status: valid
</4>
<5>
kind: item
id: B
text: Three pricing cards in a grid
note: Maps to subscription tiers.
status: valid
</5>
<6>
kind: item
id: C
text: Footer with social links
note: Not part of the prompt - extra scope.
status: spurious
</6>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`coverage-matcher
prompt: Optional prompt block shown above the matcher.
requirementLabel: Requirements
itemLabel: Submitted items
keyLesson: Optional summary shown after the reveal.
<1>
kind: requirement
id: R1
text: What the prompt asks for.
coverageNote: Shown after reveal - what covers this, or why it's a gap.
modelConnection: A
</1>
<2>
kind: item
id: A
text: What was submitted.
note: Shown after reveal - explanation of validity.
status: valid
</2>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Required: at least 1 \`kind: requirement\` item and 1 \`kind: item\` item. Requirement \`modelConnection\` is the matching item ID, or \`null\` for a gap. Item \`status\` is \`valid\`, \`partial\`, or \`spurious\`.

## Grouping drill

Use GroupingDrill when learners need to cluster related items and write a combined answer.

\`\`\`grouping-drill
context: Below are seven rubric criteria written by three different reviewers. The grader needs them consolidated into a smaller number of combined criteria so that overlapping items don't get scored multiple times.
instruction: Select criteria that should be verified together, then write one combined criterion for each group.
keyLesson: Group by what the grader does to verify, not by topic. If three criteria all get checked in one pass through the same UI region, they belong in one combined criterion.
<1>
kind: item
id: i1
text: Hero headline is clear and direct.
group: A
</1>
<2>
kind: item
id: i2
text: Hero subhead explains the value in one sentence.
group: A
</2>
<3>
kind: item
id: i3
text: Hero CTA button is visible above the fold.
group: A
</3>
<4>
kind: item
id: i4
text: Pricing cards use consistent typography.
group: B
</4>
<5>
kind: item
id: i5
text: Pricing cards align to a common baseline grid.
group: B
</5>
<6>
kind: item
id: i6
text: Footer contains required legal links.
group: C
</6>
<7>
kind: item
id: i7
text: Footer copyright year is current.
group: C
</7>
<8>
kind: bucket
id: A
label: Hero block coherence
modelAnswer: The hero block has a clear headline, an explanatory subhead, and a visible CTA above the fold.
</8>
<9>
kind: bucket
id: B
label: Pricing card consistency
modelAnswer: Pricing cards use consistent typography and align to a common baseline grid.
</9>
<10>
kind: bucket
id: C
label: Footer compliance
modelAnswer: The footer contains required legal links with a current copyright year.
</10>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`grouping-drill
context: Optional context block shown above the items.
instruction: Optional task-specific instruction shown above the items.
combinedAnswerLabel: Your combined answer
combinedAnswerPlaceholder: Write 1 combined answer that captures all the items above...
keyLesson: Optional summary shown after the reveal.
<1>
kind: item
id: i1
text: Item text
group: A
</1>
<2>
kind: bucket
id: A
label: Bucket label shown after reveal
modelAnswer: Model combined answer for this bucket.
</2>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

Required: \`kind: item\` entries with \`id\`, \`text\`, and \`group\`, plus matching \`kind: bucket\` entries with \`id\`, \`label\`, and \`modelAnswer\`.

## Rating drill

Use RatingDrill when learners score one or more subjects across multiple metrics.

\`\`\`rating-drill
scale: 7
prompt: Rate the subject on each metric.
keyLesson: Score each metric independently before forming an overall judgment.
<1>
kind: metric
key: quality
label: Quality
description: How strong is the output overall?
</1>
<2>
kind: subject
id: A
label: Completion A
image: /placeholder.svg
expert: quality=5
expertRange: quality=4-6
</2>
<3>
kind: feedback
key: quality
headline: Quality depends on whether the output satisfies the core request.
body: A polished output can still score poorly if it misses the requested work.
</3>
\`\`\`

The syntax:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`rating-drill
scale: 7
prompt: Optional instruction shown above the subjects.
keyLesson: Optional summary shown after the reveal.
<1>
kind: metric
key: metric-key
label: Display label
description: Optional helper text shown before reveal.
inverted: false
naAllowed: false
</1>
<2>
kind: subject
id: A
label: Optional display label
image: /optional/image.png
caption: Optional image caption.
expert: metric-key=5
expertRange: metric-key=4-6
</2>
<3>
kind: feedback
key: metric-key
headline: Short summary
body: Markdown explanation
tip: Optional tip block
example: Optional monospace example
</3>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Scorer

Scorer replaces both score badge and rate-then-reveal. Without axes, it displays a score. With axes, it asks the learner to rate first, then reveals the model breakdown.

\`\`\`scorer
score: 5
max: 7
label: Content quality
showScale: true
size: md
\`\`\`

The syntax for score display:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`scorer
score: 5
max: 7
label: Content quality
showScale: true
size: md
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

The reveal mode uses numbered axes.

\`\`\`scorer
subject:
A short sample the learner should judge.
prompt: How would you rate this on a 1–7 scale?
scale: 7
keyLesson: Commit to a judgment before reading the model reasoning.
<1>
name: Specificity
note: The sample names the topic but not the measurable criteria.
</1>
\`\`\`

The syntax for reveal mode:

\`\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
\`\`\`scorer
subject:
A short sample the learner should judge.
prompt: How would you rate this on a 1–7 scale?
scale: 7
keyLesson: Commit to a judgment before reading the model reasoning.
<1>
name: Specificity
note: The sample names the topic but not the measurable criteria.
</1>
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`
`,X1=`---
title: The content system
---

Every page starts as a markdown file in \`src/content/\`. The file has 2 parts: YAML frontmatter (metadata) and body content (text and components). Understanding this structure is the key to editing any Citadel app.

## File anatomy

\`\`\`highlight
{(1) ---}
{(2) title: Getting started with ^GitHub^}
{(3) description: Set up your development environment.}
{(1) ---}

{(4) ## Prerequisites}

Before you begin, make sure you have Node.js installed.

marginalia:
1: YAML frontmatter delimiters - everything between the dashes is metadata
2: The page title - rendered as the h1 heading, sentence-cased automatically. Proper nouns wrapped in carets keep their original casing.
3: Optional description - rendered below the title in body text style
4: Sub-headings use ## - these create entries in the floating section nav on the right side of the page
\`\`\`

## The manifest

The manifest at \`src/content/manifest.md\` controls sidebar navigation. Each line defines a page - its title, route, and type.

\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
- [Page Title | Sidebar Label](/route) {type}
  - [Child Page](/route/child) {type}
\`\`\`

**Types** determine which frontmatter fields a page uses: \`instructions\` (default), \`quiz\` (adds quiz metadata), \`questions\` (FAQ-style content).

**Pipe syntax** lets you show a shorter label in the sidebar while keeping the full title for the page heading. The part before \`|\` is the title; the part after is the sidebar label.

**Nesting** with 2-space indentation creates parent-child groups. Children are auto-numbered (\`1.\`, \`2.\`) in the sidebar.

\`\`\`example-pair
good: - [Welcome to ^Citadel^ | Welcome](/) {instructions}
- [Getting started with ^GitHub^ | ^GitHub^ setup](/github) {instructions}
  - [Authentication](/github/auth) {instructions}
  - [Repositories](/github/repos) {instructions}
bad: - [Welcome to Citadel](/) {instructions}
- [getting started with github](/github) {instructions}
context: The good version uses pipe syntax for concise sidebar labels, caret markers to preserve proper noun casing, and nesting to group related pages. The bad version loses casing ("github" becomes "Github" after sentence-casing) and puts everything at the top level.
\`\`\`

## Inline formatting rules

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: Sentence casing is automatic
content:
All headings (h1 and h2) and sidebar labels are sentence-cased at render time. You do not need to manually case them - just wrap proper nouns in \`^carets^\` to protect them.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
title: Proper noun markers (^carets^)
content:
Wrap any word that must keep its original casing in \`^caret^\` markers. Without them, \`Getting Started with GitHub\` becomes \`Getting started with github\`. With \`^GitHub^\`, it becomes \`Getting started with GitHub\`.

Use carets in:
- Content file titles (\`title: Getting started with ^GitHub^\`)
- Manifest labels (\`[Getting started with ^GitHub^ | ^GitHub^ setup]\`)
- Any h2 heading in body content
</1>
<2>
title: Step prefixes in titles
content:
Titles that start with \`Step N\` followed by a separator (\` - \`, \` - \`, \`: \`) are preserved as-is before sentence-casing the rest.

Example: \`title: Step 6 - Validate in ^Docker^\` renders as "Step 6 - Validate in Docker"

This lets you number steps in a workflow without the number being stripped by sentence-casing.
</2>
<3>
title: Heading levels
content:
Only two heading levels are allowed:
- \`h1\` - the page title, set via frontmatter \`title:\` field. Never write \`# Heading\` in the body.
- \`h2\` - sub-sections within a page, written as \`## Heading\` in the body. These create entries in the floating section nav.

No h3–h6 headings are used as section-level elements. Use bold text or component titles for finer structure.
</3>
\`\`\`

## Content files in practice

When you want to change what a page says, find its \`.md\` file in \`src/content/\`, edit the frontmatter or body, and save. The app reloads automatically in dev mode. No code changes needed.

When you want to add a new page, create a new \`.md\` file, add a manifest entry, create a minimal page component in \`src/pages/\`, and register a route in \`src/App.tsx\`. The [Component catalog](/content/components) shows how to add interactive elements to your new page.
`,J1=`---
title: Content
---

Citadel enforces a strict separation between content and code. All text lives in markdown files. Components render that text - they never contain it. This architecture means you can rewrite every word in your app without touching a single line of code.

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Edit markdown, not components
content:
The most common mistake new operators make is prompting the AI to add content directly to components. Instead, edit the markdown files in \`src/content/\`. The components read from those files automatically.
\`\`\`

## How it works

Every page in a Citadel app follows the same flow:

1. A **markdown file** in \`src/content/\` holds all text - titles, descriptions, paragraphs, and component data
2. A **page component** in \`src/pages/\` imports and parses that markdown
3. The **ContentBlockRenderer** converts parsed blocks into interactive components

To change what a page says, you edit the markdown. To change how a page looks, you use the design system. To change what components appear, you use fenced code blocks inside the markdown.

Learn the details in the 2 sub-sections: [The content system](/content/system) explains how markdown files, frontmatter, and the manifest work. [Components](/content/components) shows every component type with live examples and authoring syntax.
`,s_=`---
title: Golden Examples
---

Real Lighthouse tasks that passed review. **These are the bar.** Click any task to see its full persona, prompt, per-model failure justifications, and complete rubric.

| # | Description | Task |
|---|---|---|
| Persona 1 | Brand designer in Atlanta, 34, single, no dependents, renter. Works for himself on retainer with a small number of clients. | [Open →](/golden/atif-9f14845b) |
| Persona 2 | Senior software engineer at a publicly-traded tech company in San Francisco, 31, single, renter. California resident. | [Open →](/golden/mark-ce08d55b) |
| Persona 3 | Retired couple in Tampa, both 71, married filing jointly. Two adult kids out of the house. Florida residents. | [Open →](/golden/atif-74b127ce) |
| Persona 4 | Dual-income couple in Denver, both 29, married filing jointly, no kids yet, renters. One person works in construction project management. Other is an RN. | [Open →](/golden/matt-b2b023f3) |
| Persona 5 | Single mother in Phoenix, 45, two teenagers, homeowner. Works a day-job at a tax and advisory firm and runs her own side bookkeeping practice. | [Open →](/golden/phoenix-bookkeeper) |

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: How to read these examples
content:
For each task, ask yourself: where is the **headline finding** hidden? Which **explicit constraints** in the prompt became **negative criteria** in the rubric? Where would a model most plausibly fail, and which **failure axis** would that failure fall under?
\`\`\`
`,Z1=`---
title: Deploy safely
---

Citadel apps are deployed through a controlled pipeline - not through Lovable's publish button. This protects fellows from seeing broken or unreviewed content and ensures every app is properly linked to Handshake's infrastructure.

## The deployment process

\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: Get connected
content: Contact a Fellow Dev engineer (Erik Hinton or Matt Conrad) to link your app to the Learn Apps GitHub repository. Tell them your app name and the intended access level.
tip: Do this early - the first deployment requires infrastructure setup that only Fellow Dev can do.
</1>
<2>
title: Choose an access level
content: Tell the Fellow Dev engineer who should see your app. There are three options: admin only, fellows in a specific project, or any fellow with a login.
tip: You can change the access level later by contacting Fellow Dev again.
</2>
<3>
title: First deployment
content: Fellow Dev handles the initial deployment. They configure the build, connect the domain, and verify the app loads correctly.
</3>
<4>
title: Subsequent deployments
content: After the first deployment, you redeploy your app yourself from the deploy dashboard whenever you make changes.
</4>
\`\`\`

The deploy dashboard lives at:

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
https://deploy.learn.joinhandshake.com/
\`\`\`

## Why not deploy from ^Lovable^?

\`\`\`example-pair
good: Deploy from deploy.learn.joinhandshake.com after Fellow Dev links your app. This goes through the Learn Apps pipeline with proper access controls, domain configuration, and build verification.
bad: Click Lovable's publish button. This deploys to a Lovable-hosted URL with no access controls, no connection to Handshake infrastructure, and no way for fellows to authenticate.
context: Lovable's publish feature is designed for general-purpose web apps. Citadel apps need Handshake authentication, project-based access controls, and the learn.joinhandshake.com domain. The Fellow Dev pipeline provides all three.
\`\`\`

## Pre-deploy checklist

Before requesting a deployment or redeploying from the dashboard, verify your app is ready.

\`\`\`checklist
<1>
id: qa
label: QA check passed
hint: Run the full QA checklist from skills/qa-check.md. Every section must pass.
</1>
<2>
id: dry-run
label: No dry-run artifacts
hint: Check that src/content/dry-run/ is empty or contains only .gitkeep.
</2>
<3>
id: access
label: Access level confirmed
hint: You told Fellow Dev whether the app should be visible to admins only, project fellows, or all fellows.
</3>
<4>
id: content
label: Content reviewed in browser
hint: Open every page and scroll through. Check that images render, components work, and no placeholder TK text remains.
</4>
<5>
id: emojis
label: No emojis in output
hint: Emojis are stripped during scaffolding but can sneak in during manual editing. Check titles and body text.
</5>
\`\`\`

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
title: Admin only
content: Only Handshake admin users can access the app. Use this for internal tools, draft trainings, or apps still in review.
</1>
<2>
title: Fellows in a specific project
content: Only fellows who have been admitted to a particular project can access the app. Use this for project-specific trainings where the content is tailored to a cohort.
</2>
<3>
title: Any fellow with a login
content: Any authenticated fellow can access the app. Use this for general-purpose trainings, onboarding materials, or resources that apply across all projects.
</3>
\`\`\`
`,ek=`---
title: Remix and scaffold
---

Every Citadel project starts the same way: remix the template, then scaffold your content from a source document. In a Citadel remix, scaffolding is enforced as the first operator step before customization, styling, feature work, or content edits. The scaffold converts your external markdown into structured pages with interactive components - no manual page-by-page setup required.

## The remix workflow

\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: Remix the template
content: Open the Citadel template in Lovable and click **Remix**. This creates a copy of the template under your account with all the infrastructure intact - design system, components, parsers, and skills. The next agent interaction should begin scaffold intake, not ordinary app customization.
tip: Name your project something descriptive. The name becomes the repo name.
</1>
<2>
title: Prepare your source document
content: Write or gather your training content in a single markdown file. Use \`#\` headings for major sections - each one becomes a page. Use \`##\` headings for sub-sections within a page.
tip: Google Docs exports work. The scaffold normalizes formatting automatically.
</2>
<3>
title: Run the scaffold
content: In Claude Code or the Lovable chat, hand the agent your source file - upload it, paste the path, or share the URL. The agent detects that scaffolding is needed and starts Guided Scaffold by default: it infers the learner, outcome, training mode, and likely weak spots before it writes files. After you confirm or say "use your defaults," it parses your markdown, detects components, generates content files, and updates the manifest.
tip: To preview output without writing real files, say "scaffold this as a dry run" or run \`/scaffold --dry-run\` explicitly.
</3>
<4>
title: Review and refine
content: Open your app in the browser and walk through every page. Review the agent's editorial suggestions, especially proposed examples, practice moments, or sections marked as needing subject-matter input. Accept the changes that make the training stronger and defer the rest.
</4>
\`\`\`

## Guided Scaffold is the default

Guided Scaffold is the enforced first step after a Citadel remix. If the agent sees a source document, it starts scaffold intake. If no source document is available yet, it should ask for one or help you draft 1 before editing the app.

Guided Scaffold treats your source as the beginning of a training conversation, not just a file to format. Before writing files, the agent proposes a learning arc: who the training is for, what the learner should be able to do, where the source is strong, and where examples, practice, or interactive components could make the experience clearer.

You can choose a faithful scaffold or allow guided enhancements. When the agent proposes new material, it labels the source basis so you can tell the difference between source-derived content, inferred bridges, practice examples, and gaps that need subject-matter input.

## Example: what the conversation looks like

Imagine you give the agent this source excerpt:

\`\`\`code-block
language: markdown
content:
# First mentor meeting

Mentors should greet the learner, set expectations, review the first assignment, and schedule the next touchpoint.
\`\`\`

Before writing files, the agent should respond with something like this:

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
I can scaffold this with guided defaults so we keep momentum.

Inferred defaults:
- Learner: new mentors.
- Learning outcome: mentors can run a first learner touchpoint.
- Training mode: short onboarding path with a checklist and one practice scenario.

Please confirm or revise:
1. Is the learner a new mentor?
2. May I add one practice/example scenario?
3. Should I use these defaults and proceed?

Learning arc:
- Prepare for the first touchpoint.
- Run the conversation.
- Close with next steps.

Source-basis map:
- Source-derived: greet learners, set expectations, review assignment, schedule next touchpoint.
- Practice/example: sample mentor opening script if approved.

Editorial backlog:
- Confirm whether there are required organization-specific talking points.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: The conversation is the point
content:
The scaffold still creates files, routes, and components, but it first helps you decide whether the training needs examples, practice, or subject-matter review.
\`\`\`

## What happens after the scaffold runs

The scaffold doesn't stop when it finishes writing files. It continues through a mandatory sequence automatically:

\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: QA check
content: The agent runs the full QA checklist against every generated file. Violations are reported and fixed before moving on.
</1>
<2>
title: Interactive content review
content: The agent scans every page for content that should be a component but landed as plain text - step lists, comparisons, Q&A patterns. It flags these and asks whether to convert them.
</2>
<3>
title: Rhythmic variety
content: The agent identifies text walls - stretches of prose that exceed the density threshold without a component break. It inserts placeholder callouts and walks you through resolving each one.
</3>
<4>
title: Scaffold health report
content: The agent runs a structural check on every fenced block (empty fields, failed parsers, content fidelity against the source). Issues are reported for your review.
</4>
<5>
title: Pedagogical analysis
content: In Guided Scaffold, the agent reads the project as a whole and identifies missing examples, weak transitions, hidden workflows, repeated ideas, and practice opportunities. Each suggestion includes a source-basis label.
</5>
<6>
title: Persona review
content: The agent reads the full project three times - as a PhD academic, a working professional, and a first-time student - and reports friction points with severity, evidence, suggested fixes, and whether the change is safe to apply automatically.
</6>
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: Don't cancel early
content:
The post-scaffold steps are mandatory, not optional polish. If you close the session after files are written but before the health report runs, your app may have broken component blocks or text walls that the scaffold didn't catch.
\`\`\`

## The scaffold command

\`\`\`code-block
language: bash
content:
/scaffold /path/to/your-source.md
\`\`\`

This starts Guided Scaffold by default.

To preview without writing files:

\`\`\`code-block
language: bash
content:
/scaffold --dry-run /path/to/your-source.md
\`\`\`

If you have already completed the learning-design conversation elsewhere and need the old mechanical conversion path, ask for \`fast scaffold\` or run \`/scaffold --fast\`. Most projects should stay with the guided default.

\`\`\`callout
variant: warning
title: Scaffolding replaces all template content
content:
When you scaffold, this documentation disappears - every page you are reading now gets replaced by your content. That is by design. Commit your work before re-running the scaffold.
\`\`\`

## What makes a good source document

Not all markdown scaffolds equally well. The scaffold's component detection relies on structural signals in your source.

\`\`\`example-pair
good: # Welcome\\n\\nIntroductory paragraph.\\n\\n# Setting Up\\n\\n## Prerequisites\\n\\n- Install Node.js\\n- Clone the repository\\n\\n## Steps\\n\\n1. **Open the terminal**\\n2. **Run npm install**\\n3. **Start the dev server**
bad: Welcome to the course. To set up, install Node.js and clone the repo. Open the terminal, run npm install, then start the dev server. Next we will cover configuration...
context: The structured version gives the scaffold clear section boundaries (# headings), a bulleted list (detectable as a Checklist), and numbered bold steps (detectable as a StepSequence). The flat version produces a single wall of text with no component opportunities.
\`\`\`
`,tk=`---
title: Getting started
---

You have a training document. You want fellows to read it, interact with it, and finish it. The gap between those 2 things is what Citadel closes - it converts your source content into a structured, deployable training app without requiring you to build the app itself.

\`\`\`callout
variant: important
title: Never deploy from Lovable
content:
Lovable has a publish button. Do not use it. All deployments go through the Fellow Dev team and the deploy dashboard. See [Deploy safely](/getting-started/deploy) for the full process.
\`\`\`

## The 2 phases

Every Citadel project follows the same 2-phase arc. Understanding why the phases are separate prevents the most common mistakes.

**Phase 1: Remix and scaffold.** You create a copy of this template, then hand the agent your source document. The scaffold skill converts your markdown into structured pages with interactive components - automatically, without manual page setup. See [Remix and scaffold](/getting-started/remix).

**Phase 2: Deploy safely.** A Fellow Dev engineer connects your app to the Learn Apps infrastructure. This step is required before fellows can access the app - Lovable's publish button bypasses authentication and cannot be used. See [Deploy safely](/getting-started/deploy).

The phases are separate because the first is yours to control (content authoring) and the second requires infrastructure access that only Fellow Dev holds. Trying to skip phase 2, or doing it yourself via Lovable, produces an app that looks deployed but rejects every fellow who tries to open it.
`,l_=`---
title: Tampa Retired Couple
---

**Task ID:** \`74b127ce-c71d-4569-9434-5ccdda49f55b\`

## Persona
\`\`\`callout
variant: note
content:
Retired couple in Tampa, both 71, married filing jointly. Two adult kids out of the house. Florida residents.
\`\`\`

## Prompt
\`\`\`callout
variant: note
content:
Robert and I have been retired down here in Tampa for a while now and feel pretty settled. The thing on my mind lately is the grandkids, Alex and Maya. We put $250 a month into each of their 529s right now and I know we can do more but I don't want to just guess. I want to know exactly what we can increase those contributions to each month based on what's actually coming in and going out, and I want to see the math, not just a ballpark.
The only thing I'd ask is don't tell us to pull more from the IRAs than we already are. Robert takes his distribution and I take mine and that's what we work with. Whatever we can do for the kids has to come out of what's already landing in the checking account. While you're looking through everything, Robert got a Medicare premium notice a few months back that seemed really high and I mentioned it to my daughter and she said it seemed off too but we can't figure out why. I don't need a whole explanation of Medicare, I just want to know if we're paying what we should be paying or if something is wrong. Please don't send us to a financial advisor, we've handled our own money our whole lives.
\`\`\`

## Critical Elements
The 10–15 most critical elements the response must get right.

\`\`\`notecard
<1>
id: ce-1
title: "[Calculation] Total net monthly income landing in the Wells Fargo checking account is $13,564.80: Robert's..."
content:
Total net monthly income landing in the Wells Fargo checking account is $13,564.80: Robert's SS net of Medicare is $2,656.80, Diane's is $1,856.80, Robert's IRA distribution net of withholding is $4,875, Diane's is $2,775 and Diane's Ford pension is $1,400, identical across all 4 months. No additional IRA money is used since the prompt rules that out.
</1>
<2>
id: ce-2
title: "[Flag] Average monthly withdrawals from the Wells Fargo checking account are $6,879.98: January was $6,603.41,..."
content:
Average monthly withdrawals from the Wells Fargo checking account are $6,879.98: January was $6,603.41, February $6,485.08, March $7,136.78, and April $7,294.65. These withdrawals already include the current $500 per month in 529 contributions and the Capital 1 autopay, so no double counting is appropriate.
</2>
<3>
id: ce-3
title: "[Calculation] The monthly surplus available for increased 529 contribution is $6,684.82, confirmed by the checking..."
content:
The monthly surplus available for increased 529 contribution is $6,684.82, confirmed by the checking account growing from $18,200 in January to $44,939.28 at the end of April, a total increase of $26,739.28 over 4 months:  This is the gross surplus before reserving for the 2025 tax underpayment. A model that excludes the current $500 in 529 contributions from outflows and adds it back will overstate the available surplus.
</3>
<4>
id: ce-4
title: "[Calculation] The 2025 federal tax return shows $10, 150.80 owed at filing against a total..."
content:
The 2025 federal tax return shows $10, 150.80 owed at filing against a total liability of $20,350.80 with only $10,200 withheld: This creates a monthly reserve need of $845.90 that a complete answer must account for before recommending how much more to direct toward the 529s. A model that ignores this overstates the available surplus by $845.90 per month.
</4>
<5>
id: ce-5
title: "[Calculation] After reserving for the tax shortfall the true available monthly surplus for additional 529..."
content:
After reserving for the tax shortfall the true available monthly surplus for additional 529 contributions is $6,338.92, or $3,169.46 per grandchild per month: This is the max recommendation based on current cash flow without touching the IRAs. Recommendations above this number are not supported by the data given.
</5>
<6>
id: ce-6
title: "[Calculation] The 2026 annual gift tax exclusion is $19,000 per person per recipient, meaning Robert..."
content:
The 2026 annual gift tax exclusion is $19,000 per person per recipient, meaning Robert and Diane together can give up to $38,000 per grandchild per year without any gift tax filing required: Current contributions of $3,000 per grandchild per year are far under this limit. The monthly ceiling that stays under the combined exclusion is $3,166.67 per grandchild. Any recommendation above $3,166.67 per child per month exceeds $38,000 annually and triggers a gift tax return requirement.
</6>
<7>
id: ce-7
title: "[Headline] The headline finding is that Robert is paying $171.10 per month in IRMAA surcharges..."
content:
The headline finding is that Robert is paying $171.10 per month in IRMAA surcharges on his Medicare premium despite the couple's 2025 AGI of $171,840 being $40,160 below the $212k MFJ first bracket threshold: Robert's IRMAA is based on 2024 income via the 2 year lookback rule, so 2024 income is the key unknown. If 2024 income was also under $212k the surcharge is wrong and can be challenged.
</7>
<8>
id: ce-8
title: "[Calculation] Diane is also paying the same IRMAA surcharge, confirmed by the SSA-1099 showing $4,118.40..."
content:
Diane is also paying the same IRMAA surcharge, confirmed by the SSA-1099 showing $4,118.40 in annual Medicare deductions against a standard Part B premium of $185 per month totaling $2,220 annually: The difference of $1,898.40 per year confirms Diane's IRMAA surcharge of $158.20 per month, identical to Robert's. This is further confirmed by her Social Security gross of $2,200 per month minus her net deposit of $1,856.80 equaling exactly $343.20 withheld per month. Total household IRMAA is $3,951.60 per year.
</8>
<9>
id: ce-9
title: "[Flag] Robert is not subject to Required Minimum Distributions yet because at age 71 he..."
content:
Robert is not subject to Required Minimum Distributions yet because at age 71 he is below the SECURE 2.0 RMD starting age of 73: His current distributions of $65,004 per year are voluntary. His estimated RMD at age 73 based on the current IRA balance of $1,605,248.42 and the IRS Uniform Lifetime Table factor of 26.5 is approximately $60,575.41, meaning his current voluntary distributions already exceed his future required amount.
</9>
<10>
id: ce-10
title: "[Calculation] The checking account balance growth of $6,684.82 per month is entirely from cash, not..."
content:
The checking account balance growth of $6,684.82 per month is entirely from cash, not investment returns, since the checking account earns only $1.20 per month in interest: This confirms the surplus is real and recurring, not a 1 time event and that the couple is over withdrawing from their IRAs relative to their spending needs.
</10>
<11>
id: ce-11
title: "[Flag] T mobile at $65/month, Frontier Communications at $75/month, and AAA at $24/month each appear..."
content:
T mobile at $65/month, Frontier Communications at $75/month, and AAA at $24/month each appear in both the Wells Fargo checking statements and the Capital 1 card every month: These 3 charges total $164/month and $1,968/year in potential duplicate payments that a complete answer should flag.
</11>
\`\`\`
## Failure Justifications
3 frontier models received this prompt. Below are the per-model justifications for why each response passed or failed the rubric.

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
title: "Model 1 · Fail — recommends increasing each grandkid's 529 to $3,592.41 per month, which is $43,108.92"
content:
FAIL
Response 1 recommends increasing each grandkid's 529 to $3,592.41 per month, which is $43,108.92 per kids per year. The 2026 combined annual gift tax exclusion for Robert and Diane together is $38,000 per grandkid. Response 1's recommendation goes over that limit by $5,108.92 per grandkid per year which would mean filing a gift tax return, directly contradicting the prompt's request to avoid creating a tax mess. Response 1 also completely dismisses the Medicare question, starting "you are paying the correct amount and nothing is wrong" without checking whether the IRMAA is justified by their income. Their 2025 AGI of $171,840 is $40,160 under the $212k MFJ IRMAA threshold, and the surcharge amounts on the notice don't match standard tiers for 2026, making this a big factual error that leaves the couple paying $3,951.60 in annual surcharges they might not owe.
</1>
<2>
title: "Model 2 · Fail — correctly reserved $845.90 per month for the tax shortfall and landed on"
content:
FAIL
Response 2 correctly reserved $845.90 per month for the tax shortfall and landed on $3,169.46 per child per month, which is $38,033.52 per grandkid per year. This exceeds the $38k combined annual gift tax exclusion by $33.52 per grandkid per year and makes them have to file a gift tax form that wasn't flagged. Response 2 also correctly identified Robert's IRMAA but only said Diane "appears" to be paying the same surcharge based on her deposit, when the SSA-1099 shows $4,118.40 in annual Medicare deductions for Diane confirming her surcharge of $158.20 per month to the dollar. A response that hedges on a finding that is in the documents is giving the user less than what the data shows.
</2>
<3>
title: "Model 3 · N/A"
content:
N/A
</3>
\`\`\`
## Golden Trajectory
The step-by-step path from prompt to verified answer.

\`\`\`step-sequence
