`,c_=`---
title: Atlanta Brand Designer #1
---

**Task ID:** \`9f14845b-4d58-4549-9482-c3bdd5d76253\`

## Persona
\`\`\`callout
variant: note
content:
Brand designer in Atlanta, 34, single, no dependents, renter. Works for himself on retainer with a small number of clients.
\`\`\`

## Prompt
\`\`\`callout
variant: note
content:
I'm a freelance brand designer here in Atlanta, all 1099, and I do my own taxes. I just finished my 2025 return and now I'm trying to get ahead on 2026 so I'm not scrambling next April. My income is running basically identical to last year (Vexrome ACHs me 4,500 a month, Karrick about 3,417), so I'm comfortable projecting 95,000 in gross receipts again. Can you build me a clean projected 2026 Schedule C and tell me my projected net profit, then carry it through to what my 2026 federal tax and quarterly estimates should actually be? For the variable business spend, please use my Chase Ink card: take the March and April 2026 statements, total the purchases by category (ignore the autopay/payment lines, those aren't expenses), map each category to the right Schedule C line, and annualize by multiplying the two-month total by 6. My fixed business costs aren't on that card, so use these annual figures: advertising 1,850, car 980, commissions 1,200, legal/professional 1,450, business rent 5,100, taxes and licenses 485, utilities 720. For client and business meals, I've spent about 1,720 over February through April so annualize that to the full year. I also max my SEP every year and I'm putting in 1,200 a month for 2026, so confirm that's fine. Use the 2025 brackets and standard deduction for the projection since 2026 tables aren't out yet. Please don't tell me to hire a CPA and keep this federal only, I handle Georgia myself.
\`\`\`

## Critical Elements
The 10–15 most critical elements the response must get right.

\`\`\`notecard
<1>
id: ce-1
title: "[Headline] The projection must include the QBI (Section 199A) deduction of $5,651.77, producing total federal..."
content:
HEADLINE: The projection must include the QBI (Section 199A) deduction of $5,651.77, producing total federal tax of $10,695.50 and quarterly estimates of $2,673.88. QBI is the lesser of 20% x $43,258.84 ($8,651.77) and the binding 20%-of-taxable-income limit (20% x $28,258.83 = $5,651.77). Omitting it - even to "mirror" the 2025 return, which erroneously left it off - overstates the tax to $11,373.71 and the quarterly to about $2,843, which is the central failure.
</1>
<2>
id: ce-2
title: "[Calculation] Correct taxable income = $22,607.06, after subtracting the $5,651.77 QBI deduction from the $28,258.83..."
content:
Correct taxable income = $22,607.06, after subtracting the $5,651.77 QBI deduction from the $28,258.83 taxable income before QBI. A response that stops at $28,258.83 and taxes that amount has omitted a deduction the taxpayer is legally entitled to (graphic design is not an SSTB and income is far below any threshold).
</2>
<3>
id: ce-3
title: "[Calculation] Correct federal income tax = $2,474.35 (2025 single brackets on $22,607.06), not $3,152.56, which..."
content:
Correct federal income tax = $2,474.35 (2025 single brackets on $22,607.06), not $3,152.56, which results from taxing the pre-QBI figure.
</3>
<4>
id: ce-4
title: "[Calculation] Projected 2026 net profit = $58,184.12 - gross receipts $95,000 minus total expenses $36,815.88"
content:
Projected 2026 net profit = $58,184.12 - gross receipts $95,000 minus total expenses $36,815.88. Every downstream figure depends on this.
</4>
<5>
id: ce-5
title: "[Flag] SEP over-contribution flag (secondary finding): $1,200/mo = $14,400 exceeds the deductible maximum of $10,814.71..."
content:
SEP over-contribution flag (secondary finding): $1,200/mo = $14,400 exceeds the deductible maximum of $10,814.71 (20% x [$58,184.12 - $4,110.57]) by $3,585.29; the projection must cap the deductible SEP at $10,814.71 and warn of the 6% excise tax. Using the full $14,400 as a deduction is a cascade error.
</5>
<6>
id: ce-6
title: "[Calculation] Chase Ink Mar+Apr purchases by category, annualized x6, total $21,590.88: Travel $562.77 + Lodging..."
content:
Chase Ink Mar+Apr purchases by category, annualized x6, total $21,590.88: Travel $562.77 + Lodging $1,346.38 = Line 24a Travel $11,454.90; Office Supplies $5,014.50; Shipping $2,902.20; Software $2,219.28. The 2 AUTOPAY lines ($2,982.92 and $2,203.65) are payments, not expenses, and must be excluded.
</6>
<7>
id: ce-7
title: "[Calculation] Lodging maps to Line 24a Travel, not a separate line"
content:
Lodging maps to Line 24a Travel, not a separate line. Schedule C has no lodging line; the Marriott charges fold into Travel. Treating lodging as personal or dropping it inflates net profit.
</7>
<8>
id: ce-8
title: "[Calculation] Meals deduction = $3,440.00"
content:
Meals deduction = $3,440.00. The $1,720 over February-April is 3 months, so it annualizes to $6,880 (x4), then the 50% business-meals limit gives $3,440. Halving without annualizing ($860) or annualizing without halving ($6,880) is wrong.
</8>
<9>
id: ce-9
title: "[Calculation] Fixed off-card expenses = $11,785.00 (advertising $1,850 + car $980 + commissions $1,200 +..."
content:
Fixed off-card expenses = $11,785.00 (advertising $1,850 + car $980 + commissions $1,200 + legal/professional $1,450 + rent $5,100 + taxes/licenses $485 + utilities $720). Total expenses therefore = $36,815.88.
</9>
<10>
id: ce-10
title: "[Calculation] Self-employment tax = $8,221.15 ($58,184.12 x 0.9235 x 15.3%), with the deductible half =..."
content:
Self-employment tax = $8,221.15 ($58,184.12 x 0.9235 x 15.3%), with the deductible half = $4,110.57. SE tax is computed on net profit and is not reduced by the SEP or QBI; it is $8,221.15 in every correct version.
</10>
<11>
id: ce-11
title: "[Calculation] AGI = $43,258.83 (net profit $58,184.12 minus half SE tax $4,110.57 minus the capped..."
content:
AGI = $43,258.83 (net profit $58,184.12 minus half SE tax $4,110.57 minus the capped SEP deduction $10,814.71, not the full $14,400). Standard deduction $15,000 (2025 single, as instructed), giving taxable income before QBI of $28,258.83.
</11>
<12>
id: ce-12
title: "[Calculation] Total projected 2026 federal tax = $10,695.50 ($2,474.35 income tax + $8,221.15 SE tax)"
content:
Total projected 2026 federal tax = $10,695.50 ($2,474.35 income tax + $8,221.15 SE tax). A total tax of $11,373.71/$11,373.72 indicates the QBI deduction was omitted.
</12>
<13>
id: ce-13
title: "[Calculation] Quarterly estimate = $2,673.88 ($10,695.50 / 4), down from his current $4,700/quarter pace ($18,800/yr)..."
content:
Quarterly estimate = $2,673.88 ($10,695.50 / 4), down from his current $4,700/quarter pace ($18,800/yr) - he is overpaying by roughly $8,100/yr.
</13>
<14>
id: ce-14
title: "[Constraint] Constraints honored: no CPA or financial-advisor recommendation, federal only (no Georgia), no specific security..."
content:
Constraints honored: no CPA or financial-advisor recommendation, federal only (no Georgia), no specific security buy/sell, no app suggestion.
</14>
<15>
id: ce-15
title: "[Constraint] Deliverable shape: a projected Schedule C by line, the net profit, the SE ->..."
content:
Deliverable shape: a projected Schedule C by line, the net profit, the SE -> AGI -> QBI -> taxable-income -> tax chain, total tax, and the quarterly figure, all dollars to the cent.
</15>
\`\`\`
## Failure Justifications
3 frontier models received this prompt. Below are the per-model justifications for why each response passed or failed the rubric.

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
title: "Model 1 · N/A"
content:
N/A - Response 1 is the 1 model that did not fail. It correctly included the QBI deduction of $5,651.77 (the lesser of 20% of QBI and the binding 20%-of-taxable-income limit), arriving at the correct taxable income of $22,607.06, total federal tax of $10,695.50, and quarterly estimate of $2,673.88. It also correctly flagged the SEP over-contribution (capping the deductible amount at $10,814.71 and noting the $14,400 plan overshoots by $3,585.29) and excluded the autopay lines, mapped lodging to Line 24a Travel, and applied the 50% meals limit. This is the passing response.
</1>
<2>
title: "Model 2 · Fail — fails on the headline of the entire deliverable"
content:
Response 2 fails on the headline of the entire deliverable. It omitted the QBI (Section 199A) deduction the taxpayer is entitled to, reporting a total 2026 federal tax of $11,373.71 and quarterly estimates of $2,845 - both wrong. The correct figures are $10,695.50 total tax and $2,673.88 per quarter, because as a non-SSTB sole proprietor (graphic design, code 541430) with income far below the threshold, he qualifies for the full $5,651.77 QBI deduction (the binding 20%-of-taxable-income limit). This was not an oversight the model missed: it explicitly wrote "Your 2025 return PDF did not include QBI, so I left it out of the main estimate to mirror that filing approach," even noting in an aside that claiming QBI "would drop to about $10,695.50, or about $2,674 per quarter." That rationale is wrong - the prompt asked what the 2026 tax "should actually be," the 2025 omission was itself an error, and a forward projection must claim a deduction the taxpayer is entitled to rather than perpetuate a prior mistake. The cascade is concrete: it taxed the full pre-QBI $28,258.83, producing regular income tax of $3,152.56 instead of the correct $2,474.35 on $22,607.06, overstating his liability by $678.21 and inflating each quarterly payment by roughly $171 - the precise numbers the user asked it to get right. (It did correctly flag the SEP over-contribution and net profit of $58,184.12, but failed the highest-weight items: the QBI deduction, the correct taxable income, the correct total tax, and the correct quarterly estimate.)
</2>
<3>
title: "Model 3 · Fail — fails on the central deliverable by omitting the QBI (Section 199A) deduction"
content:
Response 3 fails on the central deliverable by omitting the QBI (Section 199A) deduction entirely - and unlike Response 2, it never acknowledges it at all. Its tax calculation runs straight from "Taxable income: $28,258.83" to "Income tax: $3,152.56" with no QBI line anywhere in the response, so it never claims the $5,651.77 deduction the taxpayer is entitled to (non-SSTB graphic design, code 541430, income far below the threshold, so the full 20%-of-taxable-income limit applies). This produces a total 2026 federal tax of $11,373.72 and a quarterly estimate of $2,843.43, both wrong; the correct figures are $10,695.50 and $2,673.88, built on the correct post-QBI taxable income of $22,607.06 and income tax of $2,474.35. Because the prompt asked what his 2026 tax "should actually be," a correct projection must claim a deduction he is legally entitled to rather than silently drop it - omitting it overstates his liability by $678.22 and inflates every quarterly payment by roughly $170. The error even propagates into its safe-harbor sidebar, where it computes "90% of this projection ($11,373.72) is ~$10,236" off the wrong total tax, so that figure is wrong too (90% of the correct $10,695.50 is $9,625.95). It did correctly identify net profit of $58,184.12 and flag the SEP over-contribution, but it failed the highest-weight items: the QBI deduction, the correct taxable income, the correct total tax, and the correct quarterly estimate.
</3>
\`\`\`
