`,u_=`---
title: ^San Francisco^ Engineer #1
---

**Task ID:** \`ce08d55b-2a1b-46f6-afd1-86bbaef5d7dd\`

## Persona
\`\`\`callout
variant: note
content:
Senior software engineer at a publicly-traded tech company in San Francisco, 31, single, renter. California resident.
\`\`\`

## Prompt
\`\`\`callout
variant: note
content:
so I just filed my 2025 taxes and got totally blindsided, I ended up owing $22,795 in April on top of everything that was already getting withheld from my paychecks all year. That really stung and I do not want a surprise like that again. I'm a software engineer here in San Francisco, single, no dependents, and I max out my 401k every year. A decent chunk of my pay is RSUs that vest every quarter and I sell them as soon as they vest. I have a feeling the RSUs are part of why I keep coming up short, but honestly I don't really understand the mechanics of it.
What I'm trying to figure out is this: looking at my 2026 pay stubs so far and comparing them to what last year actually looked like, am I on track to owe again for 2026, and if so roughly how much should I be setting aside now so I'm not caught off guard in April? My federal withholding looks like it's running about $5,000 a month right now. I'd really like an actual dollar figure I can plan around, not an "it depends" answer, and I want to understand why this keeps happening to me so I can actually stop it instead of just bracing for it every spring.
A couple of things please. Do not tell me to go hire a CPA or to buy some tax software, I am trying to actually get my own arms around these numbers myself. And please do not give me a vague range, I want you to commit to a number. Lay it out for me in these sections: "What Happened in 2025," "My 2026 Outlook," and "What I Should Do," and use whole dollar amounts throughout.
\`\`\`

## Critical Elements
The 10–15 most critical elements the response must get right.

\`\`\`notecard
<1>
id: ce-1
title: "[Calculation] Priya's 2025 total tax was $85,295.95 against only $62,500.00 of federal withholding, producing the..."
content:
Priya's 2025 total tax was $85,295.95 against only $62,500.00 of federal withholding, producing the $22,795.95 balance due she paid in April (1040 lines 24, 25a, 34). This is the verifiable starting point and any answer must reproduce these 3 figures correctly.
</1>
<2>
id: ce-2
title: "[Calculation] Her 2026 federal withholding pace is exactly $5,000.00 per month per paystub, which annualizes..."
content:
Her 2026 federal withholding pace is exactly $5,000.00 per month per paystub, which annualizes to $60,000.00 for the year. The response must recognize this is actually $2,500.00 LOWER than her 2025 withholding, so the problem is widening, not closing.
</2>
