title: Denver Couple #1
---

**Task ID:** \`b2b023f3-6b8e-4e0b-bb67-ca0c490e4aa9\`

## Persona
\`\`\`callout
variant: note
content:
Dual-income couple in Denver, both 29, married filing jointly, no kids yet, renters. Alex works in construction project management. Jamie is an RN.
\`\`\`

## Prompt
\`\`\`callout
variant: note
content:
ok I need help settling something because Alex and I keep going back and forth on it. we're both 29, no kids, renting in Denver, I'm an RN and he does construction project management, and between us there's the Chase Sapphire and his Capital One Venture. I went to cancel stuff last week and now neither of us can agree on what we're actually paying for versus what's just getting billed twice for no reason.

&nbsp;

so go through both cards for March and April, just the actual subscriptions and memberships, streaming music gyms that kind of thing. please don't drag in the phone bill or car insurance or internet or water, those aren't subscriptions and I don't want them inflating the number, and don't treat a one time Home Depot or REI run as recurring.

&nbsp;

here's the thing I actually need. I don't want three different totals and a lecture, I want one number: what are we going to actually pay next month, assuming we cancel every charge that's genuinely the same service billed to both cards but we KEEP anything that's legitimately two separate things. so you have to actually decide on each one. like if the same gym is hitting both cards that's one membership billed twice and it goes, but if it's two real memberships for two people that stays and it's not a duplicate. I need you to commit to a call on each, not just hand me a maybe. tell me the one monthly number we'll land on, what that is per year, and exactly which charges you killed to get there and which you kept and why.

&nbsp;

and then just the short version of what's redundant. no budgeting app, no spreadsheet, I never stick with those, and don't tell me to call an advisor, it's subscriptions. just give me the number and your reasoning on the close calls.
\`\`\`

## Critical Elements
The 10–15 most critical elements the response must get right.

\`\`\`notecard
<1>
id: ce-1
title: "[Flag] The number they wanted is $304.95 a month"
content:
The number they wanted is $304.95 a month. $3,659.40 a year. That's after you cancel every real duplicate and keep one of each. All 3 models missed it - 2 said $463.95, 1 said $394.95. So this is the 1 that matters.
</1>
<2>
id: ce-2
title: "[Calculation] Orangetheory"
content:
Orangetheory. $159 on the Chase and $159 on the Venture, same months, same merchant. That's 1 membership getting billed to 2 cards, and it's the biggest single thing in the whole file. Cancel 1. The models that kept both because "2 people probably each go to OTF" just talked themselves out of the answer. There's nothing in the data that says 2 people. There's a charge that shows up twice.
</2>
<3>
id: ce-3
title: "[Calculation] Netflix, same deal"
content:
Netflix, same deal. $22.99 twice, both months. Kill 1, it's $22.99 back.
</3>
