title:
description:
---

<div class="relative rounded-2xl px-8 py-10 mb-6 overflow-hidden shadow-sm" style="background: linear-gradient(120deg, #00D3D8 0%, #D3FB52 60%, #8CCC1F 100%);">
  
  <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-semibold text-slate-900 leading-[1.1] tracking-tight">Welcome to Project Lighthouse</h1>
  
</div>

<div class="rounded-xl border border-sky-200 bg-sky-50 px-5 py-4 mb-4">

Project Lighthouse tests how frontier models handle real personal-finance questions.

Each task assigns you a **persona** (their background, goals, and a folder of 10 to 20 pre-loaded synthetic financial documents) and a **category**, like budgeting or tax planning. You write a complex prompt in that persona's voice, then grade three model rollouts against a rubric you author.

<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-1.5 mt-3 text-xs">
  <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white border border-sky-300 text-sky-900 font-semibold rounded">Prompt</span>
  <span class="text-muted-foreground">→</span>
  <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white border border-sky-300 text-sky-900 font-semibold rounded">Critical Elements</span>
  <span class="text-muted-foreground">→</span>
  <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white border border-sky-300 text-sky-900 font-semibold rounded">Failure Justifications</span>
  <span class="text-muted-foreground">→</span>
  <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white border border-sky-300 text-sky-900 font-semibold rounded">Golden Trajectory</span>
  <span class="text-muted-foreground">→</span>
  <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-white border border-sky-300 text-sky-900 font-semibold rounded">Rubric</span>
</div>

</div>

<div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 px-5 py-4 mb-4">

## What's Next?

1. **Read the Project Lighthouse Playbook**.
   - Check out the FAQ section at the end - we are updating it regularly.
2. **Ask your questions on Maven in Slack** (AI assistant for common questions).
   - See attached PDF for instructions.
3. **For remaining task-specific questions** → #lighthouse-tasking or #lighthouse-reviewers.
   - Moderators create [threads](https://slack.com/help/articles/115000769927-Use-threads-to-organize-discussions) for you to reply to - you are not able to create posts yourself.
   - Only reviewers are part of the reviewers channel.
4. **All other questions** (including questions around your account, pay, or access) → fellow-support@joinhandshake.com.

Please do not DM Handshake AI Project Team members.

</div>

<div class="rounded-xl border border-rose-300 px-6 py-5 mb-4" style="background-color: #fee2e2;">

## No-^AI^ rule

<div class="rounded-lg border border-rose-300 px-4 py-3 mb-4" style="background-color: #fecaca;">
  <div class="font-semibold text-rose-950 mb-1">This is the one rule that gets you offboarded</div>
  <p class="text-sm text-rose-950/90 m-0">Do not use any LLM, AI assistant, or AI generation tool to author any part of a task. This includes ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot, character.ai, or anything else. The prompt, the failure justifications, and the rubric must all be your own writing.</p>
</div>

**What you can and cannot use AI for**

<div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-rose-200 bg-white mb-4">
  <table class="w-full text-sm m-0">
    <thead style="background-color: #fecaca;">
      <tr>
        <th class="text-left px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Topic</th>
        <th class="text-left px-3 py-2 font-semibold text-emerald-800">Good</th>
        <th class="text-left px-3 py-2 font-semibold text-amber-800">Neutral</th>
        <th class="text-left px-3 py-2 font-semibold text-rose-800">Bad</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr class="border-t border-rose-100">
        <td class="px-3 py-2 align-top font-medium">Lookups</td>
        <td class="px-3 py-2 align-top">Use AI to look up a tax rule, a formula, or a definition you then re-verify.</td>
        <td class="px-3 py-2 align-top">Use AI to remind you of a calculation method you already know.</td>
        <td class="px-3 py-2 align-top">Use AI to compute the answer for your task.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

**Why the rule exists**

The value of Project Lighthouse is human judgment about what real people ask and what a good answer looks like. If you launder the work through an AI, you produce data that trains the next model on the previous model's mistakes. That is the failure mode the project exists to avoid.

</div>

<div class="rounded-xl border border-sky-200 bg-sky-50 px-5 py-4 mb-4">

## Live sessions

Drop-in Zoom War Room / Office Hours - click to join at the scheduled time.


- **Tasker War Room / Office Hours with Denny** — Daily at 10:30 AM PT — [Join the Zoom →](https://joinhandshake.zoom.us/j/93408305389)
- **Tasker War Room / Office Hours with Denny** — Daily at 2:00 PM PT — [Join the Zoom →](https://joinhandshake.zoom.us/j/93408305389)

</div>

<div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 px-5 py-4 mb-4">

## Refer a fellow

Know someone who would be strong on Lighthouse? Send them the [Handshake AI fellows interest form](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSd9DwAV1a3rXCKKU3PDqtm-hX06Gbj20VnWdG-Fb5TAmMz2sw/viewform). Referral bonuses are paid when your referral onboards and starts tasking.

</div>

`,f_=`---
title: The Essentials
---

Key things every fellow needs to know from day one.

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
id: critical-elements
title: How do I get to 10-15 critical elements, and what if my prompt cannot support that many?
content:
Critical elements do not need to be as long or complex as the manual example. They are the 10 to 15 answer facts a correct response must get right: the headline conclusion, the key numbers, the constraints, the required comparisons, and the traps the model must avoid.

The test is whether a reviewer can point to the response and say "this element is present and correct" or "missing or wrong."

If you cannot reach 10, do not pad - that is a signal the prompt is too narrow or underspecified. Strengthen the prompt by adding grounded complexity from the files (more constraints, more calculations, more close calls), not by splitting one fact into artificial fragments.
</1>
<2>
id: pass-fail
title: How do I determine PASS or FAIL, and what if a response is essentially blank?
content:
You judge each response against the prompt and the critical elements you identified, not against a finished rubric.

As you read a rollout, ask whether it gets the headline conclusion right, hits the key numbers and constraints, respects user requirements, and avoids the traps the prompt sets up. If it does, it passes; if it misses or contradicts critical elements, it fails. The response does not need to literally say "Failure" - write down which elements were missed as you go, and those notes become the failure justification.

Edge case for blank or near-blank responses: if the platform failed to generate any output, treat it as a platform issue and report it rather than scoring it as a model failure. If the model produced a minimal but real answer, score what is there - it will usually fail, but the justification should distinguish weak model output from a platform error.
</2>
<3>
id: referrals
title: Is there a process for referring strong candidates to the project?
content:
Know someone who would be strong on Lighthouse? Send them the Handshake AI fellows interest form.

Referral bonuses are paid when your referral onboards and starts tasking.
linkText: Handshake AI fellows interest form
linkHref: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSd9DwAV1a3rXCKKU3PDqtm-hX06Gbj20VnWdG-Fb5TAmMz2sw/viewform
</3>
<4>
id: cpt-opt-eligibility
title: What are CPT/OPT eligibility requirements?
content:
F-1 students may be eligible to participate in Handshake AI through either CPT (Curricular Practical Training) or OPT (Optional Practical Training).

CPT/OPT supervision isn't available for every project, especially ones that aren't domain-specific.

To learn more, check out the help center article below.
linkText: Fellowship work authorization eligibility
linkHref: https://ai-support.joinhandshake.com/articles/32245223383703
</4>
<5>
id: onboarding-deadline
title: When is the onboarding deadline?
content:
We recommend reaching production - after completing assessments and initial onboarding - within about 2 days of your initial outreach email.

This is a guideline, not a strict deadline, so don't stress if you go slightly beyond it. Moving quickly helps you stay active on the project.

To learn more, check out the help center article below.
linkText: Onboarding timing guidance
linkHref: https://ai-support.joinhandshake.com/articles/32265242654871
</5>
<6>
id: multiple-projects
title: Can I be in more than one project?
content:
Yes. You're welcome to submit interest in more than one project, and if you're invited, you can work on multiple at the same time.

Keep in mind some projects have a minimum weekly hour requirement. If you can't meet it, you may be removed for inactivity.
</6>
<7>
id: interest-form-timeline
title: I submitted an interest form for another project - when will they get back to me?
content:
Our team reviews applications on a rolling basis and matches fellows based on project needs, qualifications, experience, and area of expertise. Timelines vary by project.

If your background matches an active or upcoming project, a team member will contact you with next steps.

Submitting an application doesn't guarantee placement.

To learn more, check out the help center article below.
linkText: Project interest next steps
linkHref: https://ai-support.joinhandshake.com/articles/32244169079703
</7>
<8>
id: tax-details-setup
title: How do I set up tax details?
content:
Completing the correct form is required to remain eligible for payments.

We can't provide personalized tax advice - for questions about your tax situation, please consult a licensed tax professional.

To learn more, check out the help center article below.
linkText: Filling out tax forms (W-8 or W-9)
linkHref: https://ai-support.joinhandshake.com/articles/37675393830551
</8>
<9>
id: vpn-usage
title: Can I use a VPN?
content:
No. Please don't use a VPN or any tool that masks your location when accessing Handshake AI. This helps us reduce fraud and keep the network secure for everyone.

If you're traveling or will be abroad, contact Support first. They can review your situation and set up approved tasking access where it's allowed.
</9>
\`\`\`
`,p_=`---
title: Getting Paid
subtitle: Payment schedules, methods, and what to expect.
---

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
id: what-paid
title: What work will I be paid for?
content:
Project Lighthouse is pay-per-task, and only tasks that are reviewed and meet project standards are paid out. You still must track your time in Handshake AI for every task you work on, but this is not the primary way of tracking work and processing payments for this Project.

Meetings are generally not paid unless explicitly stated by your project leads.

Projects may occasionally offer incentives for onboarding activities or high-quality work. For more information, please reference guidance shared by your project leads.
</1>
<2>
id: how-paid
title: How will I be paid (setting up payments)?
content: Generally, every fellow will use Stripe, but some older fellows have access to Deel. If you don't know what Deel is, please ignore it. Each individual project has a unique pay rate and is listed on the contract related to that project.
linkText: Set Up Stripe
linkHref: https://ai-support.joinhandshake.com/articles/36935929970711
</2>
<3>
id: when-pay
title: When should I expect my pay to arrive?
content: Handshake AI processes payouts every Wednesday for the prior week's work. Banks may take a few additional days to post the funds to your account.
linkText: Pay Processing Tracker
linkHref: https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vbfwmvoFPCcecjI-m-EHMwAxujiN2tlmO81uDWBEBQM/edit?tab=t.0
</3>
<4>
id: max-hours
title: What is the maximum number of hours I can work each week?
content: You can work up to 40 hours per week. On rare occasions, fellows producing high-quality work may be approved to exceed this cap.

If you need to work beyond 40 hours in a given week, get approval from your project lead before billing the additional hours.
</4>
<5>
id: min-hours
title: What is the minimum hourly requirement per week?
content:
There usually isn't a minimum hourly requirement, but we encourage fellows to aim for at least 20 hours per week to maintain quality and task output.

Many fellows contribute 15-20 hours per week. Some projects do have a minimum - check your project instructions to confirm.
</5>
<6>
id: not-received
title: What should I do if I haven't received a payment?
content:
Before reaching out, make sure you're tracking time in Handshake AI for every task completed, and remember that banks may take a few additional days to post funds.

If you still haven't received a payment notification, please wait until Friday at 12:00pm PST before reaching out. If it's past 12:00pm PST on Friday and you still haven't been paid, please visit our [Help Center](https://ai-support.joinhandshake.com/), or select "Support" in the upper right hand corner of your [Handshake AI dashboard](https://ai.joinhandshake.com/fellow/projects).
</6>
<7>
id: dispute
title: What if I would like to dispute the amount of pay?
content: Please wait until the funds have been deposited into your account - payments can be disbursed in multiple parts (e.g. hourly earnings and incentives paid separately). If you don't see all expected payments by 12:00pm PST on Friday, please submit a dispute form.
linkText: Submit a Dispute Form
linkHref: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfu4Se-oUxcjWk82Ro7gGbzVy5HX4lIdczadNtAQgZGewBFxQ/viewform
  </7>
\`\`\`
