`,g_=`---
title: Task Access
---

How to find, unlock, and manage your assigned tasks.

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
id: platform-glitch
title: Any platform glitch (failed rollouts, stuck loops, blank generators, missing buttons, disappeared tasks). What do I do?
content:
Save your work to a separate doc, refresh the browser once, and reopen the task. Most transient errors clear on refresh.

If it survives a clean refresh, post the task ID in the #lighthouse-tasking Slack channel with the exact error text and a one-line description of what is broken. Wait for confirmation before claiming more work, and do not restart from scratch or reclaim a disappeared task - that can destroy recoverable state on the backend.

Rough timing for model rollouts: about an hour with no progress is stuck, two hours is worth reporting. Do not include sandbox instance IDs in public writeups unless support asks.
</1>
<2>
id: blocked-file-browser
title: Can I download files from the environment if the file browser is blocked?
content:
No. Do not try to bypass a blocked file browser - use only the access the task provides.

If a blocked file prevents you from completing the task, report it in the #lighthouse-tasking Slack channel with the task ID.
</2>
<3>
id: task-deadline
title: How long do I have to finish a task, and what if no tasks are available?
content:
Once you claim a task you have 48 hours to finish it. You do not need to do it in one sitting - use Save to come back later, but do not claim more than you can finish inside the window.

Task availability depends on the queue, not your account, so there is no fixed wait time and no published deadline for claiming your first task. Supply fluctuates through the day - if you see "no tasks available," check back in a few hours before escalating.

If you see "no tasks available" across a full day, or you previously had a task in progress that is now gone and the queue is also empty, post in the #lighthouse-tasking Slack channel with your account email (and any missing task ID) so the team can confirm your account is wired up and check for stuck state on the backend.
</3>
<4>
id: reviewer-status
title: Reviewer status questions - checkmarks, review timing, and "resubmit your task" emails.
content:
A checkmark on a completed task means submitted, not approved. Approval shows separately once a reviewer finishes the review, and timing depends on reviewer capacity and task volume.

Resubmit emails are not sent in error and not a judgment on you - they fire when a reviewer flags something on the submitted version. Before redoing work, reply to the email or post the task ID in Slack and ask which specific changes are still outstanding. Do not resubmit blindly - get the reviewer's actual diff first.

Check the Task Tracker. It is a live-updated sheet that shows the current status of any task by its ID number, so you can usually answer "where is my task?" yourself in a few seconds.
linkText: Task Tracker (live status by task ID)
linkHref: https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1EI7pgz9nFVw3O0dFov4Ise-p9UFYo78Q61XTv-TNZNs/edit?gid=616481621#gid=616481621
</4>
<5>
id: restart-paste
title: I restarted a glitched task and pasted my own prior text back in. Will that get flagged?
content:
Pasting your own previously written work into a restarted task is fine - it is not a plagiarism or AI-use issue.

If you want a paper trail, post the task ID in the #lighthouse-tasking Slack channel and note that you restarted due to a platform bug and reused your own prior text. That protects you if any automated flag fires later.
</5>
<6>
id: navigate-dashboard
title: How do I navigate the project dashboard?
content:
Your dashboard has two main sections:

- **Available tasks** - unclaimed tasks open for you to pick up
- **My tasks** - tasks you've started or already submitted

Use the dropdown filters to find what you need:

- **Stage** - filter by where a task is in the process (e.g., In progress, Needs fixing)
- **Sort by** - order tasks from newest to oldest, or vice versa
- **Priority** - filter by urgency
- **Task type** - filter by task role (e.g., attempter or reviewer)

For a full walkthrough, check out the help center article below.
linkText: Annotation platform overview
linkHref: https://ai-support.joinhandshake.com/articles/32262318892439
</6>
<7>
id: quality-checks
title: What are quality checks, and how do I resolve them?
content:
Quality checks flag answers that may be incomplete or incorrect. You'll find them in the top-right corner of your task, next to the Skip and Exit buttons - look for the checklist icon (a page with a checkmark and an X).

To resolve a quality check:

1. Click the icon to open the quality checks panel.
2. Click into each flagged item. If there's a Reasoning dropdown, open it to see the explanation.
3. Fix the issue, or disagree with the check if you believe your answer is correct.

To disagree: click the strikethrough circle icon in the top-right corner of that quality check and enter your reasoning.

Resolve or respond to every quality check before continuing - unresolved checks will block your task.
</7>
<8>
id: needs-fixing
title: I got my task back for review. How do I claim it and adjust the comments?
content:
Tasks that have been reviewed will show up in your My tasks section as **Needs Fixing**.

The comments will appear in the quality checks section of your task. Most of the time, these will be marked as major or minor errors. Be sure to acknowledge and fix every mistake and comment, or the task may block you.
</8>
<9>
id: unknown-holding
title: What is the unknown/holding stage?
content:
This means a project lead is taking a closer look at your task. No action is needed from you - just continue working on other tasks as normal.

If this task is blocking you from moving forward, reach out to your project lead on Slack.
</9>
<10>
id: task-load-time
title: Why is my task taking a long time to load?
content:
Some tasks generate a rubric or answer in real time, which can take a few minutes. If the platform tells you to wait:

- Do not refresh the page or exit the task.
- Leave it running and check back after the expected wait time.

If it's still not loaded after twice the expected time, contact your project lead on Slack.
</10>
<11>
id: multiple-tasks
title: Can I work on multiple tasks at once?
content: Focus on one task at a time unless your project lead instructs otherwise. Quality matters more than quantity.
</11>
<12>
id: edit-previous-steps
title: How do I edit my previous steps within a task?
content:
If you want to change your answer to a previous step, use the pencil icon near the bottom-right corner of the answer.

The screen will then change and allow you to edit the answer in the box at the bottom of your screen. Press the submit button - the black box with an arrow pointing up - to resubmit.
</12>
<13>
id: review-time
title: How long does it take for a task to be reviewed?
content:
Most tasks are reviewed within 48 hours, though timing varies with submission volume.

Many reviewers are not active on weekends, so we recommend waiting until Monday before following up. If you'd like faster feedback, reach out in Slack or office hours for a live review.
</13>
<14>
id: llm-use
title: Can I use an LLM (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot, etc.) to help me with any part of this task?
content:
Generally, no. You are strictly prohibited from using Large Language Models or other artificial intelligence-assisted tools (collectively, "LLMs") to generate work product for Handshake AI, unless otherwise expressly authorized in writing by Handshake.

To learn more, check out the help center article below.
linkText: LLM policy
linkHref: https://ai-support.joinhandshake.com/articles/37148280550295
</14>
<15>
id: failed-to-submit
title: What should I do if I keep getting "Failed to submit task/block"?
content:
This error can happen for a few reasons. Try these steps in order:

1. **Check the task requirements.** Make sure all required fields are filled in, all boxes are checked, and any task IDs are in the correct format.
2. **Wait and try again.** This is sometimes a temporary platform or rate limit issue, which can happen when partner AI lab models are overloaded. Refresh the page, wait 1-2 hours, then try submitting again.
3. **Check your browser.** Restart your browser and make sure you're using Google Chrome. You can also try an incognito window or clear your cache and cookies.
4. **Check your device.** Restart your computer. Don't use a mobile device or Chromebook.

Still stuck? Flag it in Slack to the project team and let them know you've tried all the steps above.
</15>
<16>
id: dollar-sign-latex
title: Why does my text look oddly formatted after I type a "$"?
content:
That's a LaTeX rendering bug. The editor reads dollar signs as math delimiters, so everything between two "$" characters gets re-typeset into italic, spaced-out math text.

Click the "T" icon in the upper right corner of the editor to turn the rendering off. Your text snaps back to plain characters and the dollar amounts read normally again.

Check this any time you write prompts, justifications, or rubric criteria with currency in them - the underlying text is fine, but reviewers see what the renderer shows.
</16>
\`\`\`
