`,ak=`---
title: Milestones
---

Track learner progress through your training by registering milestones - markers that fire when someone visits a page or clicks an element.

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Progress signals, not surveillance
content:
Use milestones to understand where the training flow succeeds or breaks down. The useful question is whether the content supports completion, not whether an individual clicked everything.
\`\`\`

## Why milestones matter

Without progress data, you have no way to know whether learners finish your training or abandon it halfway through. Milestones give you that visibility. Each milestone is a signal - "this person reached this point" - that rolls up into completion and engagement metrics in the training admin. Think of milestones as checkpoints, not surveillance. The goal is to understand where learners get stuck so you can improve the content, not to monitor individuals.

\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: Page visit
content: Fires automatically when a learner navigates to a specific page. No learner interaction required beyond opening the page. Use this for measuring reach - did people get to the conclusion? Did they open the optional deep-dive?
</1>
<2>
title: Button or link click
content: Fires when a learner clicks a specific element. Use this for measuring engagement - did they click the external resource link? Did they submit the reflection quiz?
</2>
\`\`\`

## Create milestones in the admin

Open your training in the admin panel at \`ai.joinhandshake.com/admin/trainings/TRAINING_ID\`. Each milestone you create there gets a unique ID. Copy that ID - you will need it when wiring milestones into your app.

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: Start simple
content:
The easiest completion signal is a single page-visit milestone on your last page. Add checkpoint milestones only when you need more granular progress data.
\`\`\`

## Wire milestones with the skill

Run \`/milestone\` in the Citadel agent. The skill walks you through 3 decisions:

\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: Provide the milestone ID
content: Paste the ID from the training admin. The skill validates that it is not already registered in the manifest.
</1>
<2>
title: Choose the trigger type
content: Select **visit-page** for automatic page-load tracking, or **click** for element-interaction tracking.
</2>
<3>
title: Identify the target
content: For visit-page, name or describe the page ("the conclusion page" or \`/conclusion\`). For click, name or describe the element ("the Submit button on the quiz page" or the element's \`id\`). The skill resolves your description to the correct route or element, then wires the handler into the page component.
</3>
\`\`\`

## What the skill generates

Behind the scenes, the skill updates the manifest, validates routes and element IDs, and adds the tracking code to the correct page. First, it registers the milestone in the manifest frontmatter of \`src/content/manifest.md\`:

\`\`\`code-block
language: yaml
content:
milestones:
  - id: completed-training
    action: visit-page
    route: /conclusion
  - id: clicked-resource-link
    action: click
    buttonId: external-resource
\`\`\`

Second, it adds the tracking code to the target page component. For page visits, that is a single hook call:

\`\`\`code-block
language: tsx
content:
import { useMilestonePageView } from "@/hooks/useMilestone";

// Inside the component, before the return:
useMilestonePageView("completed-training");
\`\`\`

For clicks, it attaches a handler to the target element:

\`\`\`code-block
language: tsx
content:
import { trackClick } from "@/lib/milestones";

<Button id="external-resource" onClick={trackClick("clicked-resource-link")}>
  View resource
</Button>
\`\`\`

## Choosing a milestone strategy

How many milestones you register depends on what you need to learn about your learners.

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
title: Completion only
content: Register one visit-page milestone on your final page. If a learner reaches the last page, they completed the training. This is the simplest setup and covers the most common reporting need.
</1>
<2>
title: Checkpoint progress
content: Register visit-page milestones on several pages throughout the training - typically at the end of each major section. This tells you not just whether someone finished, but how far they got before dropping off. Three to five checkpoints across a ten-page training is usually enough.
</2>
<3>
title: Engagement depth
content: Add click milestones on key interactive elements: a quiz submission button, an external resource link, a reflection checklist. These measure whether learners are engaging with the content, not just scrolling past it. Use sparingly - tracking every click creates noise without insight.
</3>
\`\`\`

## Validation rules

The milestone system enforces several rules to prevent misconfiguration. The QA check verifies these before every commit:

\`\`\`checklist
<1>
id: unique-ids
label: Each milestone ID is unique across the manifest
</1>
<2>
id: valid-routes
label: Every visit-page route matches an existing page in the manifest navigation
</2>
<3>
id: single-button-id
label: Every click button ID appears as an element \`id\` exactly once in exactly one page
</3>
<4>
id: registered-handler
label: Every registered milestone has a corresponding handler in the page component code
</4>
<5>
id: no-orphans
label: No orphaned handlers remain after manifest changes
</5>
\`\`\`
`,w_=`---
title: No-^AI^ Rule
---

\`\`\`callout
variant: warning
title: This is the one rule that gets you offboarded
content:
Do not use any LLM, AI assistant, or AI generation tool to author any part of a task. This includes ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot, character.ai, or anything else. The prompt, the failure justifications, and the rubric must all be your own writing.
\`\`\`




## What you can and cannot use AI for

\`\`\`good-neutral-bad-table
<1>
topic: Lookups
good: "Use AI to look up a tax rule, a formula, or a definition you then re-verify."
neutral: "Use AI to remind you of a calculation method you already know."
bad: "Use AI to compute the answer for your task."
</1>
\`\`\`

## Why the rule exists

The value of Project Lighthouse is human judgment about what real people ask and what a good answer looks like. If you launder the work through an AI, you produce data that trains the next model on the previous model's mistakes. That is the failure mode the project exists to avoid.
`,ok=`---
title: "404"
message: "Oops! Page not found"
linkText: "Return to Home"
linkHref: "/"
---

# Not Found Page

This page is displayed when a user navigates to a route that doesn't exist.
`,ik=`---
title: Step 0 - Understand Personas
---

All personas live in a shared Google Drive folder. Find your assigned persona, open the subfolder, and download the files you need.

\`\`\`embed
url: https://drive.google.com/embeddedfolderview?id=11t6waSaiXijvUTkVXXw-VMbVjzfeJsWb#grid
title: Project Lighthouse Personas
\`\`\`

If the embed does not load, [open the folder directly in Google Drive](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/11t6waSaiXijvUTkVXXw-VMbVjzfeJsWb?usp=sharing).

## Context notes

Each persona has a short context note describing their situation and the documents they provide. Read the note for your assigned persona before opening the documents - it can tell you what they think is going on and what they are unsure about.

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
id: persona-6-veronica-geller
title: Persona 6 - Veronica Geller (2025 tax year)
content: |
  **Background:** Spent the first half of 2025 in New York working for Bedford & Vassar Media, then took a role with Pemberton Global and relocated to London in mid-June on a USD-payroll international assignment. So two employers in 2025 and two W-2s, filing a US 1040 as a single US citizen covering worldwide income.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Two 2025 W-2s (Bedford & Vassar Media; Pemberton Global)
  - 2025 Form 1040 (my draft)
  - 2024 Form 1040 (as filed) + my 2025 estimated-tax / safe-harbor worksheet
  - A 2026 monthly paystub from Pemberton (London payroll, USD)
  - May 2026 Barclays US checking statement + transactions CSV
  - Fidelity brokerage statement (current holdings, incl. a REIT fund)
  - 2025 Fidelity Consolidated 1099 (INT / DIV / B)
  - Pemberton Stock Plan Supplemental (adjusted RSU + ESPP basis)
  - 2025 Form 5498-SA (HSA)
  - A half-finished tax tracker workbook (tax_tracker_DRAFT_2025.xlsx)

  **What to look for:**
  The attached tax tracker workbook was self-started and the numbers may be off - the draft shows a balance due that looks high ($13,687.90). Watch for errors in the draft that inflate the liability, and confirm what the correct 2025 bottom line should be (amount owed or refund) before filing.

  **Goal:** a correct 2025 bottom line (amount owed or refund) and a short list of corrections to the draft.
</1>
<2>
id: persona-7
title: Persona 7 - Nadia Okafor (2025 tax year)
content: |
  **Background:** A single, high-earning Boston tech worker who changed jobs mid-2025 (Brightmoor Jan-Jun, then Halcyon Jul-Dec), so two W-2s with RSUs, ESPP, 401(k), and an HSA. Filing a 2025 US Form 1040. Her draft comes out to approx. $25,685 owed, which she suspects is far too high given how much was withheld across two jobs.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Two 2025 W-2s (Brightmoor; Halcyon)
  - 2024 Form 1040 + 2025 estimated-tax worksheet
  - Fidelity Consolidated 1099 (INT / DIV / B)
  - Fidelity Stock Plan Supplemental (adjusted RSU + ESPP basis)
  - Fidelity brokerage statements (2025 Q4, 2026 Q1)
  - HealthEquity HSA forms (5498-SA / 1099-SA)
  - Halcyon paystubs and Harborstone checking statements + CSVs (Mar, Apr 2026)
  - A half-finished tax tracker (okafor_tax_tracker_DRAFT_2025.xlsx)

  **What to look for:** The draft overstates the tax. Two employers each withheld near the Social Security cap, so combined SS tax (approx. $20,813) far exceeds the 2025 max of $10,918 - the approx. $9,895 excess is a refundable credit (Schedule 3) she missed. And the 1099-B reports broker basis on the RSU/ESPP sales that omits approx. $30,500 of comp already taxed on her W-2; using the Stock Plan Supplemental's adjusted basis cuts the gain sharply. Also honor the wash sales, exclude muni interest, and apply qualified-dividend rates. In the other direction: combined 401(k) deferrals ($25,000) exceed the $23,500 limit, so the $1,500 excess is added back, and additional Medicare tax should be reconciled (Form 8959).

  **Goal:** the correct 2025 bottom line (likely a refund, not approx. $25,685), with the RSU/ESPP basis and excess-SS credit handled right, plus her true 2026 monthly surplus (excluding transfers to savings, brokerage, and HSA).
</2>
<3>
id: persona-8-diane-okafor
title: Persona 8 - Diane R. Okafor (Los Angeles, CA - Tax year 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Husband Mark passed away in March 2025 after a long illness, and a few months later received a devastating breast cancer diagnosis. Large medical bills and a life insurance payout further complicate the picture. The 2025 tax return is self-prepared and unreviewed. Daughter Nia, 15, lives in the home in California.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 W-2 + payroll register
  - Draft 1040 packet (with attempted schedules)
  - 2025 consolidated 1099
  - Estate basis letter + date-of-death valuation + life-insurance summary
  - HSA tax forms (contributions and distributions)
  - Medical expense summary
  - Brokerage statement
  - 1099-R
  - Mortgage 1098 + amortization
  - Excerpt of the 2024 return
  - A recent paystub
  - 3-month bank statement
  - A half-finished tax tracker

  **What to look for:** Whether the 2025 federal return was prepared correctly and what the actual bottom line should be (refund or balance due, and how much), including filing status for 2025 (the draft uses "Single" following the loss of a spouse).
  Other potential areas of concern:
  - Stock activity and losses: inherited CRL stock sold with a 1099-B basis that disagrees with the estate letter, NVTK sold at a loss and repurchased weeks later, and a capital loss carryover from 2024.
  - A $15,000 retirement-account withdrawal used for treatment, and any early-withdrawal penalty.
  - HSA distributions for medical expenses alongside a $1,000 personal HSA contribution.
</3>
<4>
id: persona-9-rikita-remi
title: Persona 9 - Rikita Remi
content: |
  **Background:** Age 41, single, living in Denver, CO. Recently laid off from Pinnacle Cloud Systems on April in a RIF, with no new job lined up yet, and money decisions need to be made before filing or spending. 2025 taxes and income is messy, with two jobs (Helix Data Corp January-June, then Pinnacle July-December) producing two W-2s, and a large share of Pinnacle pay delivered as RSUs, with a lump sum severance and an old 401(k) of roughly $128,000 being considered as bridge cash. A rough self-prepared spreadsheet estimates about $102,000 after the 20% withholding, for combined bridge cash near $133,000.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Two 2025 W-2s (Helix, Pinnacle)
  - 2025 Form 1040
  - 2025 consolidated 1099
  - Stock plan supplemental
  - HSA Form 5498-SA
  - January and final April 2026 paystubs
  - March bank statement + transactions CSV
  - Brokerage statement
  - Summit Visa statement
  - Greenline 401(k) statement
  - Severance summary
  - A half-finished tracker spreadsheet

  **What to look for:** Whether the 2025 federal return reflects the correct bottom line (refund or balance due, and how much), and what the true after-tax cost of cashing out the Greenline 401(k) would be versus rolling it over. Other potential areas of concern:
  - Possible excess Social Security tax and possible excess 401(k) deferrals across the two 2025 employers.
  - RSU withholding at a flat 22% supplemental rate, which may under-withhold relative to actual marginal rate.
  - PNCL stock sale with a 1099-B basis that may understate cost, and an ABCD position sold at a loss and repurchased within weeks (potential wash sale).
  - Brokerage 1099 items with mixed tax treatment: muni-bond interest, REIT dividends, and foreign tax paid.
  - HSA contributions split between employer payroll and personal deposits.
  - A potential early-withdrawal penalty and additional tax on a full 401(k) cash-out beyond the 20% withheld, versus a direct rollover.
  - Concentration risk from a brokerage account heavy in PNCL (former employer), recurring subscription spending, and a Summit Visa balance being paid at the minimum.
</4>
<5>
id: persona-10-castellanos-family
title: Persona 10 - Castellanos family college-funding decision (June 2026)
content: |
  **Household:** Marco and Renata Castellanos (married, filing jointly), Westminster, CO. They have wages of $133,640.00 plus investment income from a taxable Granite Peak Brokerage account (interest, dividends, capital gains, and some foreign-source dividends). They self-prepared a 2025 tax-return DRAFT (attached) but have NOT had it reviewed and suspect it contains mistakes. One child: Diego, admitted as a first-year student to Thornfield University for fall 2026.

  **The situation:**
  Diego got into Thornfield (an Ivy-tier school). The 2026-27 cost of attendance is $90,500.00. He received a partial aid package: $48,000.00 in gift aid. After gift aid the family still faces a net cost of $42,500.00 for year one. The award letter also lists federal loans of $5,500.00 and $2,800.00 of work-study, and projects costs for all four years.

  **What the family has to work with:**
  - A CollegeInvest 529 account (owner Marco, beneficiary Diego) - see the multi-page 529 statement, including its tax treatment and a non-qualified-withdrawal illustration.
  - The federal loans and work-study in the award letter (with a 4-year projection).
  - About $3,000.00 Diego saved from summer work (he has a 2025 W-2).
  - Six months of checking activity (Jan-Jun 2026) showing the full monthly cash flow and a list of recurring subscriptions.
  - A taxable brokerage account generating investment income (see the truthful consolidated 1099; use it to check the self-prepared 2025 Form 1040 DRAFT, which may mis-handle several items).
  - A Sapphire Visa carrying a purchase balance and a 0% promotional balance that expires in September 2026.
  - They expect to claim the American Opportunity Tax Credit on their 2026 return.

  **The questions they want answered:**
  1. What is our REAL year-one out-of-pocket cost, treating loans as debt and work-study as earned?
  2. How do we pay tuition so we actually qualify for the AOTC, given the 529 also pays tuition AND our MAGI is in the credit's phase-out range?
  3. Will the 529 last all four years at the current draw - and if not, what changes?
  4. With un-withheld investment income, are we set up to owe a penalty? Did we handle the muni interest, wash sale, capital-gain rates, and the foreign tax credit correctly?
  5. Where can we trim recurring spending, and how should we pay down the credit card before the 0% promo expires?
</5>
<6>
id: persona-11
title: Persona 11 - Margaret Ostrander (2025 tax year)
content: |
  **Background:** 71-year-old widow in Dubuque, Iowa, filing on her own for the first time after her husband Harold (who handled the finances) passed two years ago. Retired teacher living on retirement income. Filing a 2025 US Form 1040 as a single Iowa resident.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 1099-R from IPERS (teacher's pension)
  - 1099-R from Vanguard (IRA; $1,000/month withdrawal)
  - Form RRB-1099 (green form) - SSEB portion of Harold's railroad survivor annuity
  - Form RRB-1099-R - NSSEB/pension portion of the same annuity
  - 1099-INT from the bank (CDs)
  - Bank checking statement
  - A half-finished tax tracker spreadsheet Harold started (numbers may be off)

  **What to look for:** The two railroad forms are the tricky part. The green RRB-1099 (SSEB) is taxed like Social Security; the RRB-1099-R (NSSEB) is taxed like a pension. Watch for them being double-counted or fully taxed when they shouldn't be, and don't trust the figures in Harold's draft.

  **Goal:** a correct 2025 federal bottom line (owed or refund), proper treatment of the railroad annuity, whether an Iowa return is required, and a withholding recommendation - plus a short list of fixes to the draft.
</6>
<7>
id: persona-12
title: Persona 12 - Maple Okonkwo (2025 tax year)
content: |
  **Background:** A single filer trying to sort out her own taxes for the first time, working off a self-started draft 1040 she suspects is wrong. Income is wages plus investment income; she has a taxable brokerage account, did a backdoor Roth, carries student loans, and wants help untangling the investment forms and cleaning up her finances.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Fidelity 1099-R - $7,043 in boxes 1 and 2a, box 2b checked (a $7,000 nondeductible Traditional IRA contribution converted to Roth in 2025; no Form 8606 ever filed)
  - Fidelity Consolidated 1099 (INT/DIV/B) for the taxable account - tax-exempt interest, qualified vs ordinary dividends, section 199A dividends, foreign tax paid, a wash sale (code W), and a VXUS sale with basis not reported
  - 1098-E from MOHELA ($612 student loan interest)
  - Huntington checking statement (recurring subscription charges to total up)
  - Her draft 2025 Form 1040

  **What to look for:** The backdoor Roth is the big one - her draft taxed the full $7,043 as income, but almost none should be taxable once Form 8606 is filed. The brokerage draft is also off: VXUS basis was left at 0 (inflating the gain), plus questions on the wash sale, tax-exempt interest, dividend types, 199A, and the foreign tax credit. Don't trust the draft's numbers.

  **Goal:** corrected capital gains, taxable income, total tax, and refund/owe; the right Form 8606 treatment of the conversion; her total monthly subscription bleed so she can cancel; and a plain explanation of marginal vs effective tax rate. (She also flagged that her $4,200 checking balance is her whole safety net - no emergency fund yet.)
</7>
<8>
id: persona-13
title: Persona 13 - Curtis & Renata Vandermolen (2025 tax year)
content: |
  **Background:** A married couple in Fort Wayne, Indiana filing a 2025 US Form 1040 jointly, two kids (Mia, 13; Owen, 10). Curtis is a union pipefitter at Meridian Mechanical; Renata drives a school bus part-time. Two W-2s, a small brokerage account Renata opened, and a family HSA Curtis funds pre-tax through payroll plus $4,000 of their own money this year. He does the return by hand off a tracker spreadsheet and figures they're getting a bit under $400 back; he wants a second set of eyes before mailing.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Two 2025 W-2s (Meridian Mechanical - Curtis; the school district - Renata)
  - 2025 Form 1040 (his draft, married filing jointly)
  - Fidelity Consolidated 1099 (INT / DIV / B) for Renata's account - a muni bond fund, a dividend fund, one stock sold
  - 2025 HSA forms from EvergreenHSA (1099-SA distributions + 5498-SA contributions)
  - A recent pay stub and last month's bank statement
  - A half-finished tax tracker (tax_tracker_DRAFT_2025.xlsx)

  **What to look for:** The HSA is the trap, and the draft understates the tax. Curtis assumes everything he puts in is deductible and everything he takes out is tax-free. But the $4,000 he added himself stacks on top of the pre-tax payroll contributions and pushes total contributions past the $8,550 family limit (excess contribution - 6% excise, Form 5329), so the full $4,000 deduction is too high. And he treats the HSA card like checking: the part spent on groceries and gas isn't a qualified medical expense, so it's taxable plus a 20% penalty (Form 8889). The brokerage 1099 figures, by contrast, look correct - don't get distracted there.

  **Goal:** confirm the correct 2025 bottom line (the approx. $400 refund is likely wrong), handle the HSA properly on Form 8889 (and 5329 if needed), and give a short, plain-language list of fixes plus what to change going forward.
</8>
<9>
id: persona-14
title: Persona 14 - Earl Castellano (2025 tax year)
content: |
  **Background:** A 57-year-old regional sales manager at Keystone in Scranton, Pennsylvania, married to Lorraine and filing a 2025 US Form 1040 jointly. His stepson Dylan (15) lives with them as a dependent; his own son Marcus is grown and on his own. After a rough year of medical bills (approx. $13,000 out of pocket), Earl pulled $24,000 from his IRA before age 59 1/2. He did his own paper draft and a tracker spreadsheet, figures he owes about $220, and wants it checked before filing.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 W-2 (Keystone)
  - 2025 IRA 1099-R (the $24,000 early withdrawal; some tax was withheld)
  - Brokerage Consolidated 1099 (INT / DIV / B) - includes a muni bond fund
  - HSA form (contributions made both through payroll and directly by Earl)
  - 2025 medical bills (approx. $13,000 unreimbursed)
  - A recent pay stub and bank statements
  - His draft 2025 Form 1040 and tracker spreadsheet

  **What to look for:** The draft overstates the tax, mostly around the early withdrawal. Earl applied the 10% penalty to the full $24,000, but the medical-expense exception reduces the amount subject to the penalty (unreimbursed medical above 7.5% of AGI, Form 5329) - and that exception applies even though he takes the standard deduction. He also lumped tax-exempt muni interest in with taxable interest, didn't apply qualified-dividend/capital-gain rates, and left his HSA off entirely, missing the direct-contribution deduction (Form 8889), which is also available with the standard deduction. He likely doesn't owe - he's probably due a refund.

  **Goal:** the correct 2025 federal bottom line (owed or refund) with the early-withdrawal penalty figured correctly, a short plain-language list of what he left on the table, and an estimate of his real 2026 monthly spending (excluding transfers to savings/investing) to help him pay down the $9,000 HELOC.
  </9>
<10>
id: persona-15
title: Persona 15 - Zuri Bankole (2025 tax year)
content: |
  **Background:** A 26-year-old gig worker in Atlanta filing a 2025 US Form 1040 as a single filer. Income from rideshare driving (Vanta), freelance photography (Snaplii + Venmo), and weekend bartending (the only W-2). Two Schedule C businesses, no quarterly estimates paid. His draft shows a scary balance due, but he just added up every form without subtracting anything.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - W-2 from Ponce City Taproom (small wages)
  - 1099-K from Vanta - $31,200 gross (before fees); 14,000 on-trip miles
  - 1099-NEC from Vanta - $1,400 (bonuses)
  - 1099-K from Snaplii - $14,500 (they take 15%)
  - 1099-K from Venmo/PayPal - $7,600, with a goods-and-services vs. friends-and-family split
  - Camera kit invoice - $14,000, March 2025, 100% business
  - Mileage log, a pay stub, bank statements, and his draft return

  **What to look for:** The draft hugely overstates the tax - he summed the gross 1099s and deducted nothing. Platform fees (Vanta, Snaplii) are deductible; the friends-and-family part of the Venmo $7,600 (roommate rent, a ticket repayment) isn't income. He also missed the mileage deduction (14,000 + 1,500 business miles) and the $14,000 camera (Section 179). In the other direction, don't forget self-employment tax and a likely underpayment penalty (Form 2210) for skipping estimates.

  **Goal:** the correct 2025 federal number done right (Schedule C, SE tax, mileage and equipment deductions), which 1099 amounts are real income, and whether an underpayment penalty applies.
  </10>
<11>
id: persona-16
title: Persona 16 - Miles Morales (2025 tax year)
content: |
  **Background:** Widower W-2 employee (Pacific Meridian Health Systems) in Los Angeles with a 15-year-old dependent, Nia. His husband, Marcus, died in March 2025, leaving inherited securities and a life-insurance payout, and Miles took an early 401(k) distribution. His self-prepared draft shows approx. $25,320 owed, which he suspects is wrong.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - W-2
  - 2025 Form 1040 draft + 2024 Form 1040 (final joint return)
  - Fidelis Consolidated 1099 (INT / DIV / B) + 2025 brokerage statement
  - 1099-R (Vanguard - $15,000 early 401(k) distribution, code 1)
  - Form 1098 (mortgage interest) + 2025 medical expense summary
  - HSA 5498-SA / 1099-SA
  - Estate inheritance basis letter
  - May 2026 paystub, Q2 2026 checking statement + CSV, tax_tracker_DRAFT_2025.xlsx

  **What to look for:**
  - **Filing status.** He filed Single. Because his spouse died during 2025, he is treated as married for the full year and should file Married Filing Jointly (the year-of-death return). Head of Household is not available, and Qualifying Surviving Spouse only applies in 2026-2027.
  - **Itemize.** His itemized deductions far exceed the MFJ standard deduction ($31,500), so the standard deduction on the draft is wrong.
  - **Excluded "income."** He added $54,000 that isn't income: the $50,000 life-insurance death benefit is excluded under §101(a) (only the $900 settlement interest is taxable, reported with interest), and the $4,000 HSA distribution was entirely qualified medical, so $0 is taxable.
  - **Missed HSA deduction.** He omitted the $1,000 direct (post-tax) HSA contribution, an above-the-line deduction.
  - **Inherited stock basis.** He used broker basis on the inherited CRL shares. They receive a full community-property step-up to date-of-death FMV ($95/share) and are always long-term - so the real CRL result is a approx. $1,200 long-term gain, not a $30,400 short-term gain.
  - **Wash sale.** The $2,000 loss on NVTK is disallowed - he repurchased the shares 14 days after selling (1099-B box 1g shows the $2,000 adjustment, $0 allowed).
  - **Capital loss carryover.** The 2024 return carries a $5,000 long-term capital loss into 2025, usable on this final joint return. Netting CRL (+$1,200), the disallowed NVTK loss ($0), and the carryover (-$5,000) gives a net capital loss limited to -$3,000 on line 7, with approx. $800 carrying to 2026.
  - **Medical deduction.** Deductible medical is $26,000, not $30,000 - the $4,000 paid from the HSA can't also be deducted. After the 7.5%-of-AGI floor, the deduction is approx. $16,549.
  - **Early-withdrawal penalty.** The $15,000 distribution is taxable, but the 10% penalty is eliminated, not just reduced: unreimbursed medical exceeding 7.5% of AGI (approx. $16,549) is greater than the full distribution (Form 5329 medical exception).
  - **NIIT.** Recomputes to $0 - MAGI (approx. $126,000) is far below the $250,000 MFJ threshold.

  **Goal:** the correct 2025 bottom line is a refund of roughly $6,500-$7,200 (not approx. $25,320 owed), filing MFJ, itemizing, excluding the life-insurance and HSA amounts, applying the stepped-up long-term basis, the wash-sale disallowance, and the prior-year loss carryover, with the early-withdrawal penalty and NIIT both at $0.
</11>
<12>
id: persona-17
title: Persona 17 - Priyanka Raghunath (2025 tax year)
content: |
  **Background:** A single H-1B visa holder in Seattle filing a 2025 US Form 1040. Priyanka, 29, is a data scientist earning a $142,000 base salary plus restricted stock units (RSUs) that vest annually; she shares an apartment, has no debt, and sends approx. $1,500/month to her parents in India. She keeps a deliberately oversized cash cushion in case her visa situation forces her to leave the country on 60 days' notice. From before she moved, she still holds two Indian bank accounts - an NRE and an NRO account - whose combined balance crossed $10,000 this year. She generated a draft return with self-service tax software (importing her broker forms but hand-keying her India interest and never carefully reviewing the equity-comp side), and she doesn't trust it. She wants a second set of eyes before filing, and she's especially anxious about a foreign-account filing ("FBAR") a friend mentioned that she may have been unknowingly missing.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 W-2 (employer)
  - 2025 Form 1040 (her software-generated draft, single filer)
  - Consolidated 1099 (INT / DIV / B) imported from her broker
  - Stock-plan supplemental statement (RSU vesting / cost-basis detail)
  - 2025 HSA form
  - India account statements (NRE and NRO accounts)
  - Recent pay stubs, plus bank and brokerage statements
  - A half-finished tax tracker spreadsheet

  **What to look for:** The foreign accounts and the equity comp are the traps. On the accounts, the question isn't whether she owes more tax but which informational filings the India accounts trigger - FinCEN Form 114 (FBAR) is filed separately with Treasury once aggregate foreign balances exceed $10,000, and Form 8938 (FATCA) may also apply depending on whether she clears the higher 1040 thresholds; she's at risk of both under-filing out of ignorance and over-filing out of fear, so the answer must say clearly what she must file and what she need not. On the equity comp, RSU income is already in her W-2 wages, so the trap is double-counting or, more likely, an incorrect (often $0 or too-low) cost basis on the broker 1099-B for shares sold to cover taxes - which would overstate her capital gain and her tax. The India interest she typed by hand needs checking against the statements, and the $1,500/month to her parents is a personal gift with no deduction (and likely no Form 709 issue). Don't assume the imported broker figures are correct just because they imported.

  **Goal:** Confirm the correct 2025 bottom line and whether the draft over- or understates the tax, fix the RSU cost-basis and India-interest figures on the return, give a precise list of which foreign-account filings (FBAR / Form 8938) are required and which are not, and provide a short, plain-language list of fixes plus what to change going forward.
</12>
<13>
id: persona-18
title: Persona 18 - Diego Fuentes-Maro (2025 tax year)
content: |
  **Background:** A single 24-year-old in Phoenix filing a 2025 US Form 1040. Diego is a diesel-mechanic apprentice at Desert Ridge Diesel and takes two night classes a semester at Phoenix College (tuition paid in cash at the cashier's window). He lives with his parents and two younger siblings, gives his mom $450/month toward rent, and has approx. $1,800 in savings - that's everything. The shop pays him $38,000 on a 1099 and calls him a contractor, but every fact points the other way: he uses the shop's tools, lifts, and diagnostic computers; the foreman assigns his bay and jobs and checks his work; he works a fixed Mon-Fri 7:00-3:30 schedule set by them, for no other shops; and he never invoices - the same amount just arrives every two weeks. He had no tax withheld all year, so whatever he owes is due in one lump. Free tax software pushed him onto Schedule C + Schedule SE and returned a bill far larger than he can cover, which alarmed him. He wants a second set of eyes before filing.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 Form 1099-NEC from Desert Ridge Diesel ($38,000)
  - 2025 Form 1040 (software draft, single filer, with Schedule C and Schedule SE)
  - Form 1098-T tuition statement from Phoenix College
  - Secured Visa credit-card statements (opened ~6 months ago, $700 deposit, autopay set to minimum)
  - Bank/savings statements and any pay records
  - A half-finished tracker of his money

  **What to look for:** The worker-classification question is the crux, and the software's Schedule C / SE treatment likely overstates the tax. Every factor - the shop's tools, the foreman's control over what and how he works, the fixed schedule, the steady non-invoiced pay, no other clients - points to employee status, not an independent contractor. If he's truly a misclassified employee, he can file Form SS-8 to ask the IRS to determine his status and Form 8919 to pay only the employee half of FICA (~7.65%) instead of the full ~15.3% self-employment tax that Schedule SE imposes - a material difference on $38,000. On the tuition, the out-of-pocket amounts on the 1098-T should be checked for an education credit (the American Opportunity Credit for an undergraduate, or the Lifetime Learning Credit), which the draft may be omitting entirely. Note that as a likely dependent of his parents, the education credit may belong on their return, not his - that needs confirming before claiming it. Don't assume the software's classification and lump-sum number are correct just because they came out of the program.

  **Goal:** Confirm the correct 2025 bottom line and whether the draft over- or understates the tax, determine whether Form SS-8 / Form 8919 apply and rework the self-employment tax accordingly, sort out who can claim the education credit and for how much, and give a short, plain-language list of fixes plus what to change going forward - including practical notes on the lump-sum he owes (e.g. IRS payment-plan options), the secured-card autopay habit, and building a cushion.
</13>
<14>
id: persona-19
title: Persona 19 - Daniel Reyes (mid-2026 offer decision)
content: |
  **Background:** A single 34-year-old senior product manager with no dependents, currently in Austin, TX (no state income tax), making a mid-2026 decision before the completed 2025 tax year. Daniel has two offers in hand: stay at Lumengrid in Austin on a retention counteroffer (base $182,000 + 15% bonus + a $240,000 RSU grant + $25,000 retention cash) versus move to Veridian Cloud in New York City (base $205,000 + 15% bonus + a $360,000 RSU grant with a 1-year cliff + $40,000 signing + $30,000 relocation). On paper the NYC offer looks like much more money, but he'll only move for a genuine after-tax gain once NY taxes and rent are counted - and leaving would forfeit approx. $104,300 of unvested Lumengrid equity. He built a comparison worksheet he doesn't trust, does his own analysis with no paid advisor, and wants a second set of eyes before deciding.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Two offer letters (Lumengrid retention and Veridian Cloud NYC, multi-page)
  - Two RSU vesting schedules (Lumengrid and Veridian; note Veridian's 1-year cliff)
  - Lumengrid paystubs (mid- and year-end 2025) and 2025 W-2
  - Equity account, brokerage, and 401(k) statements
  - Current Austin lease and the recruiter's NYC relocation estimate
  - His comparison worksheet (comp_comparison_worksheet.xlsx)
  - Two bank account statements (checking and savings)

  **What to look for:** The NYC offer's bigger gross is deceptive, and the worksheet understates the cost of moving badly enough to flip the answer. New York charges a state income tax and a separate NYC city income tax (approx. $11,280/yr) on top of federal - and the worksheet omits the city tax entirely, the single largest error. It also treats one-time money as recurring: the $40,000 signing and $30,000 relocation are one-time and shouldn't be annualized into the ongoing comparison. The move forfeits approx. $104,300 of unvested Lumengrid equity, and Veridian's 1-year cliff means none of the new grant vests in year one. The recruiter's relocation estimate is optimistic and should be treated as a distractor, not a reliable number. Austin's zero state/city income tax and lower rent do more work than the base-salary gap suggests - don't anchor on gross pay.

  **Goal:** Deliver an apples-to-apples, after-tax-plus-rent comparison of what Daniel would actually keep each year under each offer - including NY state and NYC city tax, one-time bonuses counted as one-time, the forfeited Lumengrid equity, and the NYC vesting cliff - and a short, plain-language list of where his worksheet goes wrong plus what to weigh going forward. (Ground truth: Austin wins by approx. $21,753/yr after tax and rent; the move doesn't make financial sense on money alone.)
</14>
<15>
id: persona-20
title: Persona 20 - Harold and Junko Klein (MFJ, retirees, 2025 return)
content: |
  **Background:** A married couple filing jointly in Scottsdale, AZ for tax year 2025, preparing their own return by hand with no paid preparer. Harold (73, first RMD year) and Junko are both retired. In 2025 Harold took $25,000 in cash from his traditional IRA and had Vanguard send another $10,000 directly to the Sonoran Desert Conservancy as a charitable gift - $35,000 distributed in total. Their Medicare premiums jumped this year and they don't understand why, since their current income isn't high. They expect to need $30-40k next year for a kitchen remodel and aren't sure whether to pull it from the IRA or the brokerage account. On the draft 1040, Harold entered the full $35,000 as taxable, which felt too high. He wants a second set of eyes, walked through in plain language, before filing.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 Form 1040 (their hand-prepared draft, married filing jointly)
  - 1099-R from Vanguard (IRA distribution; box 2a likely shows the full $35,000)
  - SSA-1099s (Social Security) and any pension 1099-R
  - Consolidated 1099 for the joint brokerage account
  - Multi-page IRA and brokerage statements (including the 12/31/2024 year-end IRA statement)
  - IRMAA determination letter from Medicare/SSA
  - QCD confirmation letter from the Sonoran Desert Conservancy
  - Bank account statements

  **What to look for:** The RMD, the charitable gift, and the Medicare surcharge all interact, and the draft overstates the tax. First, the $10,000 sent straight to the charity is a qualified charitable distribution (QCD): it counts toward satisfying the RMD but is excluded from taxable income - so entering the full $35,000 as taxable is the main error. Only the $25,000 cash withdrawal is taxable; report the gross $35,000 but subtract the QCD so taxable income is $25,000 (write "QCD" on the line). Second, check whether the $35,000 total actually met the required amount - the RMD is computed off the 12/31/2024 IRA balance (the prior-year statement), not a current figure. Third, the Medicare premium jump is IRMAA, a surcharge based on income from two years earlier; it should fall once the high-income year ages out of the lookback. Don't be distracted by "tax-free" municipal bond dividends, which are still added back into the MAGI that drives both IRMAA and Social Security taxability.

  **Goal:** Confirm whether the $35,000 satisfied the RMD (and that no penalty applies), correct the taxable IRA amount on the draft 1040 down to $25,000 via the QCD, explain why Medicare premiums rose and whether they'll come back down, recommend the most tax-efficient source for next year's $30-40k remodel withdrawal, and give a short, plain-language list of fixes plus what to watch going forward.
 </15>
<16>
id: persona-25
title: Persona 23 - Jolene Hartwell (Hartwell Family Farm, 2025 return)
content: |
  **Background:** A married couple filing jointly outside Paducah, KY for tax year 2025, preparing their own return by hand with no paid preparer. Jolene and Dale Hartwell run a 90-acre family farm - corn and soybeans with some hay and a small cow-calf herd - and Dale also drives a school bus for the county. Their son Caleb is a full-time student at Murray State. Jolene pulled the records together over the winter and took a first pass at the return herself; that draft is in the folder along with the source documents behind it. She wants a second set of eyes before filing.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Draft Form 1040 packet Jolene started (Form 1040 + Schedules 1, 2, SE and worksheets)
  - Schedule F and the farm depreciation schedule (Form 4562)
  - Fuel purchase and tax records for the year (clear diesel, gasoline, dyed diesel)
  - Dale's year-end payroll record and W-2 from the school district
  - Farm operating-account bank statement and the transaction export (CSV)
  - Statements for the operating line of credit, the equipment loan, and the home mortgage
  - Jolene's old 401(k) statement from a former job

  **Goal:** Confirm the correct 2025 federal income tax for the Hartwell household - the correct total tax and the balance due or refund - worked out from the source documents in the folder, and give a short, plain-language list of any fixes to the draft plus what to watch going forward.
</16>
<17>
id: persona-25-ngozi
title: Persona 25 - Ngozi Balogun (single mom, night-shift nurse, refund hold)
content: |
  **Background:** A single mother in Newark, NJ working night shifts as a nurse and raising Zara (7) and Kofi (4) on her own as of June 2026. The kids' father helps occasionally but isn't reliable. Daycare costs exceed her rent. Her spring tax refund was held up and she received an IRS letter about it. She's pulled her paperwork together to resolve the refund issue and get a clear picture of her finances.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - IRS letter about the held refund
  - Pay statements from her nursing job showing base pay plus night and weekend differentials
  - Emergency fund savings account statements
  - Credit card statements (balance has come down significantly from its peak)
  - Car loan and student loan statements
  - Workplace retirement plan statement (contributing enough to get the employer match)
  - Personal spreadsheet tracking accounts, monthly budget, and debts

  **Goal:** Resolve the IRS refund hold so she actually receives the money she's counting on, keep paying down the credit card until it's gone, continue growing the emergency fund, and stay on top of the kids' costs against her monthly budget.
</17>
<18>
id: persona-26-holloway
title: Persona 26 - Adrian Holloway (foreign trust beneficiary, 2025 return)
content: |
  **Background:** Adrian M. Holloway, head of the Holloway household in Greenwich, CT, preparing for a first meeting with a new accountant for tax year 2025. Most of his income comes from his grandfather's trust - the Ashworth Family Settlement, established in Jersey (Channel Islands) - which pays him $10,000/month ($120,000/year). The trust is worth roughly $2.1M on paper but the principal is locked up until he turns 45. He also does light independent consulting through Brightwater Partners. The grandfather's lawyers used to "handle the trust paperwork" with no explanation; now that they're out of the picture, Adrian has realized the trust is a foreign one and suspects the U.S. reporting side has been neglected. Nothing was set up with the IRS during the year. Despite the paper wealth, cash is tight: approx. $3,000 in checking, approx. $22,000 across three credit cards, a large mortgage, and a Range Rover lease.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Trust formation documents for the Ashworth Family Settlement (Jersey)
  - Trustee's annual accounts
  - Investment manager's statements and tax voucher
  - Foreign-account paperwork
  - Brightwater Partners consulting records
  - Mortgage year-end statement
  - Bank statement and full-year transaction export
  - Three credit card statements
  - Half-finished personal spreadsheet ("tax_tracker_DRAFT") - not reliable, do not lean on his numbers

  **Goal:** Give Adrian a clear, plain-language picture of where the Holloway household actually stands for 2025 - what's reportable to the IRS (including the foreign trust and foreign-account obligations he's likely missed), what the correct federal tax position looks like, and a short list of fixes and things to watch going forward before he sits down with the new accountant.
</18>
<19>
id: persona-27-vega-holloway
title: Persona 27 - Marisol Vega-Holloway (head of household, multi-gen support, 2025 return)
content: |
  **Background:** Marisol Vega-Holloway, 49, unmarried, principal at Sandia Vista Public Schools in Albuquerque, NM, preparing her 2025 return. Three dependents live in her household: Mateo (15, in high school, home all year), Camila (19, full-time at UNM with Marisol paying tuition and a Parent PLUS loan), and her mother Dolores (78), who moved in and lived there the whole year. Marisol and her siblings Esteban and Lucia all chip in to support Dolores; none individually covers more than half, but together they cover well over half, and the siblings signed a Form 2120 multiple-support agreement so Marisol claims Dolores this year. Marisol has always filed Single and isn't sure that's right. She took a rough draft pass in a spreadsheet but suspects mistakes - the refund looked low. Whatever the refund turns out to be is going straight onto the Parent PLUS balance.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - W-2 and pay statements (income and withholding)
  - Bank statements and 1099-INT (cash flow and interest earned)
  - Mortgage 1098 (interest and property tax)
  - UNM 1098-T for Camila's tuition
  - 1098-E for Parent PLUS loan interest
  - Dolores's medical billing statement (approx. $6,000 paid out of pocket)
  - Multiple-support declaration packet: Form 2120, signed sibling waivers, Dolores's SSA-1099 and 1099-R, and the family support log
  - Draft tax spreadsheet (tax_tracker_DRAFT, still has yellow cells to fill in - not reliable)

  **Goal:** Get the 2025 return right - confirm the correct filing status (likely Head of Household), validate that the Form 2120 multiple-support agreement properly lets Marisol claim Dolores and whether Social Security counts against the dependent income test, resolve whether the Parent PLUS interest is deductible by Marisol as the borrower, decide whether the approx. $6,000 in Dolores's medical bills makes itemizing better than the standard deduction, and land on the correct refund or balance due so it can be applied to the PLUS loan.
</19>
<20>
id: persona-28-brennan
title: Persona 28 - Declan and Saoirse Brennan (MFJ, 1099-C cancellation of debt, 2025 return)
content: |
  **Background:** Declan and Saoirse Brennan in Boston, MA, filing jointly for 2025 and claiming their son Finn (turned 2 in August) as a dependent. Declan, 36, is a pharmaceutical sales rep at Stoneridge covering Greater Boston; base plus monthly commission came to about $130,000. Saoirse stayed home with Finn and had no income. They've filed a federal extension and are putting the return together now. 2025 was Declan's first real push to dig out of debt he ran up in his twenties and early thirties. He enrolled with a debt-resolution company in January, made monthly payments into a dedicated settlement account, and in late October the company settled his maxed-out Meridian Trust Visa for a lump sum less than the full balance; the rest was wiped out. In early 2026 a Form 1099-C arrived for the canceled balance and Declan is rattled - worried the IRS will treat it as income and create a tax bill he can't pay on money he never received. Other facts: one older car owned outright (2015 Honda Accord, approx. $5,000), ordinary household furnishings, no investments outside the 401(k), no business, no other income beyond wages and a little savings interest.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Year-end pay package and W-2 from Stoneridge
  - Meridian Trust settlement letter, 1099-C, and account history
  - Harbor Point bank statements (checking and savings) and full-year transaction export
  - Declan's 401(k) statement
  - Condo mortgage statement with Form 1098
  - October appraisal from the attempted refinance (refi did not go through)
  - Harbor Point Visa credit card statement (the card they are still paying down)
  - Car lease paperwork

  **Goal:** Land on the correct 2025 federal result for the Brennans - what they actually owe or get back - and handle the 1099-C correctly, including whether an insolvency exclusion (Form 982) applies given their assets and liabilities at the moment just before the cancellation, so Declan walks away knowing exactly where they stand.
</20>
<21>
id: persona-29-tran
title: Persona 29 - Mai-Linh Tran (family-owned S-corp / closely held business, foreign owners)
content: |
  **Background:** Mai-Linh Tran, 31, runs the family business - Jade Lotus Nail Spa, Inc., three locations in San Jose, CA. Her parents started and built the company; they semi-retired and moved back to Vietnam a couple of years ago. They still own the majority of the company between them and Mai-Linh owns the rest. She runs the day-to-day on the ground - staff, customers, books - and works with an outside accountant and payroll service. She also keeps her own household in San Jose and supports her grandmother, who lives with her. The company pays Mai-Linh a salary. It also pays her parents: they own the buildings the salons operate in (so there are related-party leases) and years ago put money into the business that is still being paid back (a related-party loan). The company also makes owner distributions from time to time. Mai-Linh is not a tax person and wants a clear, accurate picture of both sides.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Jade Lotus Nail Spa, Inc. business tax return and year-end financial statements
  - The information return the business files for its foreign owners, plus related-party records (building leases with the parents, the shareholder loan from the parents, etc.)
  - Business bank account activity for the year
  - Mai-Linh's personal federal and California returns, with her W-2 and 1099s
  - Mai-Linh's recent paystubs and her personal checking and savings statements
  - Statement for the business loan Mai-Linh personally co-signed

  **Goal:** Give Mai-Linh a clear, accurate read on where the business stands and where she stands personally for the year - confirming the entity-level return and the foreign-owner reporting are right, that the related-party leases and shareholder loan are being handled correctly, and that her personal return properly reflects her wages, owner share, and any items flowing through from the business.
</21>
<22>
id: persona-30-okonkwo-bauer
title: Persona 30 - Niamh and Stefan Okonkwo-Bauer (MFJ, household employer, 2025 return)
content: |
  **Background:** Niamh A. Okonkwo-Bauer and her husband Stefan, married filing jointly in Washington, DC, finishing their 2025 federal return. Two W-2 incomes - Niamh at the U.S. Department of Justice, Stefan at Potomac General Health System. Two children in private school. They employ a full-time nanny on the books (household employer). They own their DC condo (mortgage and property taxes), hold a taxable brokerage account and retirement accounts, and are still paying down law-school and graduate-school loans. Good combined income but money is tight by the end of every month. They prepared a draft themselves and thought they were getting a small refund, but want to verify before filing.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Both W-2s and year-end pay records
  - Household payroll file for the nanny (Schedule H territory)
  - Brokerage statement and consolidated 1099
  - Mortgage statement and property-tax records (Form 1098)
  - Student loan statements (Forms 1098-E)
  - Bank statement and full-year transaction export
  - Half-finished tax-tracker spreadsheet (self-prepared draft)

  **Goal:** Verify whether the self-prepared draft actually captures everything the Okonkwo-Bauers owe for 2025 - especially the household-employer (nanny) taxes on Schedule H, brokerage and investment income, mortgage and SALT items, and student-loan interest - and land on the real bottom line, refund or balance due, before they file.
</22>
<23>
id: persona-31-reyes-bloom
title: Persona 31 - Octavia Reyes-Bloom (self-employed musician, foreign royalty withholding, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Octavia Reyes-Bloom, 30, songwriter and session musician based in Nashville, TN, working for herself. Income comes from two main streams: songwriting royalties (streams and performances worldwide) and session/studio work for hire. Income is uneven year to year; 2025 was her strongest year so far and the first year her music earned a meaningful amount overseas. Several foreign markets withheld tax on her royalties before the money reached her, itemized by territory on her royalty statement. She rents a live/work loft in East Nashville and uses roughly 40% of the space (a back room and a converted closet) as her studio and work area. She runs music income and most work-related spending through her Pinnacle business checking account; day-to-day personal spending goes on her Summit One credit card, which has carried a balance from leaner years. She pays her own health insurance at $385/month, made four quarterly estimated payments to the IRS during the year, contributes monthly to a Roth IRA at Larkspur, and is paying down a Sweetwater gear-financing plan. She has not prepared any tax forms yet - these are the source records.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Annual royalty statement (with per-territory foreign tax withheld)
  - Session-work statement
  - Pinnacle business checking statements and full-year transaction exports
  - Summit One credit card statement
  - Larkspur Roth IRA statement
  - Sweetwater gear-financing statement

  **Goal:** Build Octavia's 2025 self-employed return from the source records - correct Schedule C net income across royalties and session work, self-employment tax, the self-employed health insurance deduction, the home-office allocation for the ~40% live/work space, the foreign tax credit for royalties withheld abroad, proper treatment of the Roth IRA contribution and the gear-financing interest, credit for the four quarterly estimated payments - and land on the correct refund or balance due.
</23>
<24>
id: persona-32-volkov-hayes
title: Persona 32 - Dmitri Volkov-Hayes (crypto losses, first-year 1099-DA, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Dmitri Volkov-Hayes, 28, marketing manager in Miami, FL, earning $72K and renting with his partner. He rode a crypto value increase to a approx. $90K on-paper peak in 2025 and then watched it collapse to approx. $3K in current value. He's also has approx. $11K in cash split between checking and savings, a credit card balance around $6K bleeding him at ~25% APR, and roughly $18K in remaining student loans. His 2025 taxes are a tangle: many trades across an exchange plus an old self-custody wallet he'd basically forgotten about. This is also the first year the crypto-exchange issued him a Form 1099-DA, which reports tens of thousands of dollars in gross proceeds with no cost basis - a number that scared him into procrastinating on filing. He believes "I lost money overall, so I can't possibly owe anything," and the 1099-DA forces him to confront the difference between economic loss and taxable gain/loss per lot. He also has a small brokerage account he's too nervous to add to after the crypto experience.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Pay documents (W-2 and paystubs)
  - Exchange tax package including Form 1099-DA (gross proceeds, no basis reported)
  - Self-custody wallet transaction export (old, partially forgotten)
  - Checking and savings account statements
  - Credit card statement (approx. $6K balance, ~25% APR)
  - Student loan statement (approx. $18K remaining)
  - Small brokerage account statement
  - Half-finished self-prepared crypto/tax tracking spreadsheet

  **Goal:** Determine Dmitri's actual 2025 federal bottom line - refund or balance due - by correctly reconciling the 1099-DA gross proceeds against cost basis from both the exchange history and the self-custody wallet, computing realized short- and long-term capital gains and losses per lot, applying the $3,000 capital loss limit against ordinary income with the rest carried forward, and separating that from the paper-only decline in what he still holds. Then translate the tax answer into a realistic dig-out plan against the 25% credit card debt, the student loans, and the cash he has on hand.
</24>
<25>
id: persona-33-pendergast
title: Persona 33 - Gus Pendergast (pre-retirement, Form 8888 refund split, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Gus Pendergast, 63, structural engineer in Spokane, WA, three years from retiring at his firm. Married to Marlene, a retired teacher living on her pension plus Social Security. House is paid off, no debt. Gus's 2025 income is a complex spread: W-2 wages from the firm, side consulting reported on Schedule C with quarterly estimated payments, a limited-partner stake in Palouse Engineering reported on a K-1, a small rental on Lake Coeur d'Alene in Idaho, brokerage sales from repositioning his portfolio, a required minimum distribution from an IRA he inherited from his brother, and the final redemption of old paper Series I savings bonds. After 40 years of saving, he's learning how to spend down. He wants his federal refund sequenced via Form 8888 in a specific priority order: (1) buy the maximum in paper Series I savings bonds, (2) route as much of the remainder as allowed into his Roth IRA, (3) send anything still left to the joint checking account ending 3318 at Inland Cascade Bank. He doesn't realize that the Form 8888 paper-I-bond purchase option has ended and that his MAGI likely blocks a direct Roth contribution - both assumptions need to be surfaced and corrected in the plan.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - W-2 and full-year pay stubs from the engineering firm
  - Northwest Securities consolidated 1099 and year-end brokerage statement
  - Treasury 1099-INT for redeemed paper I-bonds
  - Coeur d'Alene rental package (income, expenses, depreciation schedule)
  - Palouse Engineering K-1 (limited partner)
  - Consulting/Schedule C records with quarterly estimated-tax payment confirmations
  - Retirement income forms: Marlene's pension 1099-R, inherited IRA 1099-R (RMD), Social Security SSA-1099
  - Inland Cascade Bank full-year statements and transaction exports
  - Self-prepared tracker spreadsheet (draft, unverified)

  **Goal:** Compute Gus and Marlene's correct 2025 federal bottom line across the full income stack - W-2, Schedule C with SE tax, K-1, Schedule E rental, brokerage capital gains, inherited-IRA RMD, taxable I-bond interest, pension, and Social Security - net of estimated payments and withholding, and produce the exact refund amount. Then translate that refund into a Form 8888 split that respects current rules: flag that paper I-bond purchases via refund are no longer available, test whether MAGI allows any direct Roth contribution (and if not, surface the workaround options), and land on a three-bucket allocation that adds precisely to the refund with the checking account ending 3318 as the residual.
 </25>
<26>
id: persona-34-adjei
title: Persona 34 - Kwame Adjei (two W-2s, first-year IB analyst, bonus-to-loans plan, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Kwame Adjei, 25, single, based in Chicago. Just finished his first full year of work: started 2025 in an entry-level finance role at Northbridge (Jan-May), then landed a first-year investment-banking analyst seat at Halstead Crane (Jun-Dec) at a $110k base plus a $25k signing bonus and a $79k year-end bonus. He rents a room in a three-bedroom with two roommates to keep fixed costs down. He's carrying roughly $165k in student loans across multiple tranches (one at 9%) and a high-APR credit card balance. Two W-2s in the same year threw off his withholding math - he assumed his paychecks covered his taxes and expected a small refund, but it looks like he owes. He planned to throw the entire year-end bonus at the 9% loan tranche and now needs to know how much of that bonus actually survives the tax bill. "I make six figures and I'm the poorest person I know, on paper."

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - W-2 from Northbridge (Jan-May)
  - W-2 from Halstead Crane (Jun-Dec), including signing bonus and year-end bonus
  - Final pay stubs from both employers
  - Student loan statements across all tranches (with the 9% tranche called out)
  - Credit card statement (high-APR balance)
  - Checking and savings account statements
  - Roommate rent-split records

  **Goal:** Compute Kwame's correct 2025 federal bottom line - refund or balance due - accounting for both W-2s stacked into one filer's brackets, Social Security wage-base over-withholding across two employers (excess SS credit), and the supplemental withholding rate applied to the signing and year-end bonuses versus his actual marginal rate. Then translate the tax answer into an allocation plan: exact dollars from the year-end bonus that remain after covering the balance due, and a defended recommendation on whether to send that residual to the 9% student loan tranche or attack the high-APR credit card first.
</26>
<27>
id: persona-35-ferncliffe
title: Persona 35 - Eudora Ferncliffe (phantom OID interest, empty-nesters, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Eudora Ferncliffe, 50, a university administrator in Madison, WI earning $104,000. Married to Bram, a part-time consultant whose practice has quietly grown; newly empty-nested. Auto-pilot savers who've never optimized anything: approx. $720k in retirement accounts, approx. $45k in cash, and approx. $60k left on a $390k home. They've always itemized "because we own a house" without rechecking whether it still beats the standard deduction, and Bram never made quarterly estimated payments on his consulting income. Their 2025 wake-up call is a Form 1099-OID from the brokerage: Treasury STRIPS and a zero-coupon CD Eudora bought a few years ago because they looked "safe" are now taxing them annually on phantom interest they won't actually receive until maturity - income on the form, nothing in the account. Refrain: "We've always just... saved a bit and hoped. It seems fine?"

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Eudora's year-end pay package (W-2 and final pay stub)
  - Brokerage tax forms including the Form 1099-OID for the Treasury STRIPS and zero-coupon CD
  - Bram's consulting records (income and expenses; no quarterly estimated payments made)
  - Mortgage statement (Form 1098) on the $390k home with approx. $60k remaining
  - Bank and credit card statements
  - Retirement account year-end statement (approx. $720k)
  - Bram's half-finished tax tracking spreadsheet

  **Goal:** Compute the Ferncliffes' correct 2025 federal bottom line - refund or balance due - correctly picking up the accrued OID from the Treasury STRIPS and zero-coupon CD as current-year taxable interest, running Bram's consulting income through Schedule C with self-employment tax and surfacing the missed quarterly estimated payments (and any underpayment penalty exposure), and explicitly comparing itemized deductions (mortgage interest, SALT capped at $10k, charitable) against the current standard deduction to confirm which is actually better rather than defaulting to itemizing out of habit. Then translate the answer into a plain-English "where we stand" summary for two empty-nesters who have never optimized anything.
</27>
<28>
id: persona-36-branch-sterling
title: Persona 36 - Austin Branch & Tyler Sterling (two-member LLC vs household, 2025 + 2026 YTD)
content: |
  **Background:** Austin Branch and Tyler Sterling, spouses in Columbia, MD, trying to cleanly separate household finances from a new home-based business. Sterling Branch Heraldry & Flagworks LLC (trade name Sterling Branch Flagworks) was formed in January 2025 as a 50/50 two-member Maryland LLC selling custom plaques, display pieces, and custom wood decor. Tyler is the resident agent and maker/operator while Austin is a 50% member and handles bookkeeping/admin. The business runs on Revolut business banking with 2025 and 2026 YTD operating records, invoices, handwritten logs, sales-tax records, and a cash-flow/pipeline workbook. Austin also has separate personal W-2/1099/Deel income; Tyler receives non-taxable VA disability benefits. The household mixes personal Wells Fargo/Revolut checking, Capital One cards, Robinhood, an Arrived real-estate holding, student loans, and home-office/coworking spending that must not be silently swept into the LLC.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Maryland LLC formation pack: articles of organization, operating agreement, member consent, EIN letter, sales/use tax registration, Howard County home occupation permit, trader license, and Form 1 support
  - 2025 and 2026 YTD business records: invoices, checking registers, card activity, expense ledgers, mileage and property records, handwritten logbooks
  - Revolut business bank statements (2025 through June 2026)
  - Cash-flow and pipeline workbook with actuals through June 28, 2026 and forward-looking quote assumptions
  - Austin's personal income records: W-2, 1099s, Deel/HireArt-style contractor statements
  - Personal banking and credit: Wells Fargo and Revolut personal statements, Capital One card statements
  - Investment and other accounts: Robinhood accounts, Arrived real-estate statement
  - Student loan schedule
  - Tyler's VA disability benefit letter (non-taxable)

  **Possible goals:** Produce (a) business/household finance review for 2025 and 2026 YTD that (a) treats the LLC as a 50/50 two-member Maryland LLC - not Tyler's sole proprietorship and not Austin's personal contractor activity; (b) keeps Austin's W-2/1099/Deel income and Tyler's non-taxable VA disability out of LLC gross receipts; (c) reconciles LLC gross receipts, taxable sales, non-taxable commission income, sales tax collected, and processor fees across the formation pack and operating records, and separates sales tax collected from actual revenue; (d) explains the high June 28, 2026 Revolut cash balance despite two $12,000 owner draws, and correctly classifies the $24,000 in equal Tyler/Austin June distributions as owner draws, not deductible expenses; (e) avoids double-counting card activity (bank card-payment transfers vs underlying card charges) and avoids treating 2026 gross deposits as revenue when they include sales tax; (f) flags Q2 2026 sales tax payable/reserve as an open liability through the June 28 cutoff; (g) treats the post-June 28 pipeline workbook as planning assumptions, not booked revenue, and stress-tests the forecast for base/conservative scenarios, expected receipts, timing risk, and whether a single large quote is distorting the ending-cash figure; (h) treats home occupation, rent/household, and coworking records as raising allocation questions rather than proving deductible amounts; and (i) uses the formation/license records to assemble a Maryland SDAT, sales/use tax, Howard County home occupation, and trader license compliance checklist, citing source documents for every tax or legal conclusion.
</28>
<29>
id: persona-37-idowu
title: Persona 37 - Persephone Idowu (Gen-Z investing native, creator Schedule C + QBI, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Persephone Idowu, "the Gen-Z investing native," 22, is a Brooklyn social-media coordinator (approx. $52k W-2 at Brightwell) living with three roommates. Her monetized-creator side hustle - brand campaigns booked through her agency Lumen plus a Stripe-powered digital-products shop - has grown from free product and occasional $50 payments into a real Schedule C business, and 2025 is the first year none of that income had tax withheld along the way. She's a disciplined saver: brokerage contributions every paycheck, cash cushion, "fun" fractional shares, and student loans she never lets snowball. The signature tax hook is Form 8995 / the §199A QBI deduction on her creator profit - the write-down her parents' generation never had. "My parents learned money from their parents; I learned it from my For You page, and now I'm learning the parts the finfluencers skip."

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Brightwell W-2 and year-end earnings statement (day job)
  - Lumen agency 1099-NEC and year-end earnings statement (brand campaigns)
  - Stripe year-end payments processor report (digital-products shop)
  - Fidelity investment and tax package (brokerage, fractional shares, 1099s)
  - Student loan year-end statement
  - SoFi checking statement and full transaction history
  - Chase year-end summary
  - Prior-year federal and state tax returns
  - Her own creator-income tracker spreadsheet

  **Goal:** Compute Persephone's correct 2025 federal bottom line - refund or balance due - by running the Brightwell W-2 alongside a proper Schedule C for the combined Lumen 1099-NEC and Stripe digital-products income (netted against legitimate creator business expenses), computing self-employment tax on the net creator profit, and claiming the §199A QBI deduction on Form 8995 against that qualified business income. Reconcile 1099-NEC and Stripe totals against her own tracker and bank deposits to catch anything missed, pick up brokerage 1099 activity and student loan interest correctly, and then answer the forward-looking question: given no withholding on the creator side, should she start sending quarterly estimated payments in 2026, and roughly how much per quarter to stay penalty-safe.
</29>
<30>
id: persona-38-carrasco
title: Persona 38 - Delphine M. Carrasco (newly single realtor, innocent-spouse relief, divorce settlement, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Delphine M. Carrasco, 54, self-employed Sacramento real-estate agent, divorced last spring after about 25 years of marriage to Victor - now filing her own taxes for the first time ("For 25 years I never saw a tax return. Now I'm the CFO of my own life."). Two grown kids out of the house. About $152,000 in 2025 commissions, kept the Sutterville Road house (approx. $210,000 owed on a approx. $300,000 home), moved most of a $180,000 divorce settlement into a brokerage account and savings with a little in checking, started a fledgling solo 401(k), and carries approx. $14,000 of divorce-attorney fees on a high-APR credit card. Signature issue: the IRS is pursuing both spouses on their 2023 joint return for income from Victor's contracting business that was left off the return she signed without seeing - she has started Form 8857 (innocent-spouse relief) to separate his mess from her fresh start.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Draft 2025 Form 1040 (her first solo attempt) and her partial tracker spreadsheet
  - 2025 real-estate commission records / 1099s (approx. $152,000 gross)
  - IRS notice on the 2023 joint return + her started Form 8857 (innocent-spouse relief)
  - Copy of the 2023 joint return as filed
  - Divorce decree / settlement summary ($180,000 to Delphine)
  - Brokerage statement (bulk of the settlement)
  - Savings and checking statements
  - Mortgage statement / Form 1098 on the Sutterville Road house (approx. $210,000 balance on approx. $300,000 value)
  - Credit-card statement showing the approx. $14,000 divorce-attorney balance and APR
  - Solo 401(k) statement and contribution records

  **Goal:** Compute Delphine's correct 2025 federal bottom line as a single filer with all income now flowing through her real-estate Schedule C - self-employment tax on net commissions, solo 401(k) deduction sized correctly against SE income, and any home-office / auto / MLS-type business expenses picked up. Give a realistic read on her exposure on the 2023 joint bill and on how strong her Form 8857 innocent-spouse case actually is. Confirm the tax treatment of the $180,000 settlement and where she stands on the house (basis, equity, mortgage-interest deductibility). Produce a defended payoff decision on the approx. $14,000 high-APR attorney balance using settlement cash versus keeping it invested. Close with an honest cash-flow, net-worth, and retirement-readiness read now that the solo 401(k) is open.
</30>
<31>
id: persona-39-whitfield
title: Persona 39 - Coraline M. Whitfield (laid-off PM, severance + RSUs, QSBS on failed startup, runway math, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Coraline M. Whitfield, 38, San Mateo product manager laid off in April 2026 from Vireo Systems (where she earned $165,000) and job-hunting since. Her husband Miles, a physical therapist, earns $70,000 and is now the only steady paycheck. They are raising a 4-year-old, Juniper, in a $750,000 condo bought at peak income with $120,000 down and a approx. $628,000 mortgage - "we bought the condo for the salary I don't have anymore." They hold $240,000 in retirement, $40,000 in vested Vireo RSUs, and a $55,000 emergency fund draining approx. $4,000 a month. Coraline sold some Vireo shares in December for cash and trimmed a couple of fund positions during the year. Signature issue: her old startup Meridian Robotics - where she exercised ISOs in 2022 and had mentally written the shares off - was acquired, producing a surprise Form 1099-CAP for 2025. The return (on extension) now has to reconcile ISO/AMT basis from 2022, RSU cost basis, and potential wash-sale adjustments, all while she decides how long they can keep the condo before listing it and whether to chip away at the Summit credit-card balance.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Coraline's W-2 from Vireo Systems (partial-year 2025, through the April 2026 layoff covers Jan-Dec 2025 wages)
  - Miles's W-2 (approx. $70,000, physical therapist)
  - Form 1099-CAP from the Meridian Robotics acquisition
  - 2022 ISO exercise records for Meridian (Form 3921, exercise-price and FMV worksheets, prior AMT return)
  - Vireo RSU vesting and sale records + broker 1099-B (December share sale, cost-basis detail)
  - Brokerage 1099s covering the fund-position sales during the year (possible wash-sale flags)
  - Form 1098 mortgage-interest statement on the San Mateo condo (approx. $628,000 balance, $750,000 value)
  - Retirement account statements (approx. $240,000 combined)
  - Emergency-fund savings statements (approx. $55,000, approx. $4,000/month drawdown)
  - Summit credit-card statements (balance and APR)
  - Juniper's daycare invoices / dependent-care records
  - Coraline's own spreadsheet reconciling the sales (totals that keep coming out different)
  - Extension confirmation for the 2025 return

  **Goal:** Compute the Whitfields' correct 2025 federal bottom line as married-filing-jointly on extension - properly basing the Meridian 1099-CAP proceeds against the 2022 ISO exercise (regular-tax basis vs AMT basis, prior-year AMT credit recovery on Form 8801), reconciling Vireo RSU sales at the correct vested-FMV basis, and clearing any wash-sale adjustments from the fund trades. Pick up mortgage interest, dependent-care benefits, and retirement-contribution treatment correctly. Then answer the forward-looking questions honestly: how many months the $55,000 emergency fund actually lasts at the current approx. $4,000/month burn on Miles's salary alone, at what point listing the condo beats draining the cushion further, and whether paying down the Summit balance now beats holding cash - given Coraline is still unemployed and 2026 withholding will look nothing like 2025.
</31>

<32>
id: persona-40-nkemelu
title: Persona 40 - Bartholomew "Bart" Nkemelu (early-retired NJ corrections officer, PSO $3K health exclusion, pension bridge to SS, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Bartholomew "Bart" Nkemelu, 50, a New Jersey state corrections officer who retired June 30, 2025 after 25 years of service (final salary approx. $88,000). He files jointly with his wife Adaeze, who worked part-time in retail all year, and they have two teens (16 and 14) at home in Hamilton Township. 2025 is a split year: about six months of W-2 paychecks from the State, then monthly PFRS pension payments starting in July. Signature document is the Form 1099-R covering the pension, with the Public Safety Officer $3,000 health-premium exclusion available as he bridges from age-50 pension eligibility to Social Security. Balance sheet: approx. $1.4M present-value pension, $40,000 in a 457(b) deferred-comp plan, approx. $120,000 remaining on a $260,000 home, approx. $9,000 on a credit card, and approx. $16,000 on a car loan. His worry in his own words: "My pension's my whole plan. I just hope the math holds for 30 more years."

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Bart's 2025 W-2 from the State of New Jersey (January-June)
  - Adaeze's 2025 W-2 (part-time retail, full year)
  - Form 1099-R package for Bart's PFRS pension (July-December), including PSO health-premium exclusion detail
  - 457(b) deferred-comp year-end statement (approx. $40,000 balance)
  - Bank checking statements and full-year transaction export
  - Credit card statements (approx. $9,000 balance)
  - Car loan statement (approx. $16,000 balance)
  - Form 1098 mortgage-interest statement on the Hamilton Township home (approx. $120,000 balance, approx. $260,000 value)
  - Bart's own rough spreadsheet attempting the 2025 numbers (incomplete, unverified)

  **Goal:** Compute the Nkemelus' correct 2025 federal bottom line and correct New Jersey bottom line as married-filing-jointly, and the combined household result. Handle the split-year income correctly: six months of State W-2 wages plus six months of PFRS pension on the 1099-R, applying the Public Safety Officer $3,000 health-insurance premium exclusion where it qualifies and reflecting New Jersey's separate treatment of pension income (pension exclusion eligibility, age/income thresholds). Confirm no early-withdrawal penalty applies to the age-50 public-safety pension, pick up mortgage interest and any dependent-related credits for the two teens, and verify Adaeze's retail W-2 withholding lines up. Then sanity-check Bart's rough spreadsheet - flag what's wrong or missing. Close with a defended answer on whether to use household cash to pay down the approx. $9,000 credit-card balance now, given the fixed pension income, the car loan, and the long bridge to Social Security.
</32>
<33>
id: persona-41-bramble
title: Persona 41 - Ignatius "Iggy" Bramble (IT day job + Etsy/reselling side hustles, Form 3115 method change, messy books cleanup, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Ignatius "Iggy" Bramble, 32, a single renter in Portland, OR who earns $68,000 in IT support at his day job while running three side hustles: an Etsy shop for handmade goods, a sneaker-reselling operation, and a half-built app. His money is scattered across PayPal, Venmo, and Etsy (for the side businesses), a small brokerage (approx. $9,000 total), a checking account, approx. $6,000 of on-hand inventory, and approx. $12,000 of debt across three credit cards he's used to finance stock. For years he confused gross revenue with profit and never reconciled anything - so this year he finally set up real books and filed Form 3115 to switch his side businesses to an accounting method that properly accounts for inventory. Core question in his own words: after fees, refunds, and cost of goods, is any of this actually making money?

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - W-2 and year's earnings statements from his IT day job (approx. $68,000)
  - Checking account statement and full-year transaction export
  - Year-end activity reports from PayPal, Etsy, and Venmo (side-business gross flows, fees, refunds)
  - Brokerage year-end statement (approx. $9,000 balance)
  - Three credit-card statements (approx. $12,000 combined balance, used to finance inventory)
  - Inventory records (approx. $6,000 on hand: Etsy handmade materials and sneaker stock)
  - Form 3115 filed for the accounting-method change (cash to accrual/inventory method for the side businesses)
  - Iggy's own partial spreadsheet trying to pull it all together

  **Goal:** Compute Iggy's correct 2025 federal bottom line as a single filer with W-2 wages plus three side businesses on Schedule C - separating each hustle (Etsy handmade, sneaker reselling, app-in-progress) so gross revenue, platform fees, refunds, and cost of goods sold are handled correctly under the new inventory-based accounting method, and reflecting the Form 3115 §481(a) adjustment for the method change. Confirm which of the three activities is actually profitable versus a hobby/loss, pick up self-employment tax and the QBI deduction where they apply, and treat the app build correctly (start-up costs vs current deduction, no revenue yet). Then give an honest read on whether the credit-card debt is being serviced by real business profit or subsidized by the day job, and a defended answer on whether to pay down the approx. $12,000 in card balances with brokerage cash before stocking more inventory.
</33>
<34>
id: persona-42-grewe
title: Persona 42 - Florian Grewe (Vermont librarian, lives on half his income, donated a collectible 1987 Buick Grand National to public radio on Form 1098-C, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Florian Grewe, 41, a single-by-choice librarian in Burlington, VT earning $58,000. He rents a small apartment, bikes everywhere, has never carried debt, and lives on roughly half of what he earns - the rest quietly stacks up. Over the years that habit has built approx. $190,000 in a taxable brokerage plus approx. $20,000 cash, alongside a separate 403(b) through the library system. The one outlier on his balance sheet is a 1987 Buick Grand National he bought from his father for $500 decades ago that turned out to be wildly collectible. He never drove it. In September 2025 he donated it to the public radio station he's supported for years; they sold it at auction in October and issued him Form 1098-C for his records. His creed, in his own words: "I don't need a bigger paycheck. I need fewer wants."

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Florian's 2025 W-2 from the Burlington public library (approx. $58,000)
  - 403(b) year-end statement (library system plan)
  - Taxable brokerage year-end statement and 1099-B (approx. $190,000 balance)
  - Checking and savings statements (approx. $20,000 cash) plus full-year transaction export
  - Form 1098-C from the public radio charity for the donated 1987 Buick Grand National (auction gross proceeds, sale date, vehicle VIN/description)
  - Original bill of sale from his father showing $500 purchase price (basis documentation, held decades)
  - Rent receipts and renter's-insurance statement
  - Prior-year return for reference

  **Goal:** Compute Florian's correct 2025 federal bottom line and correct Vermont bottom line as a single filer. Handle the vehicle donation on Form 1098-C correctly - deduction is generally limited to the charity's gross auction proceeds (not appraised fair market value), require Form 8283 given the amount, and confirm whether itemizing beats the standard deduction given his otherwise minimal deductions (renter, no mortgage, no state-tax cap issue at this income). Pick up 403(b) contributions, any brokerage dividends/interest and capital-gain distributions on the 1099-B, and confirm no wash-sale or unusual basis issues on the taxable account. Then answer the forward-looking questions honestly: given his ~50% savings rate, whether the current taxable/403(b) mix is the right vehicle for the next dollar (Roth IRA space, 403(b) headroom), and whether keeping approx. $20,000 in cash is over- or under-sized for someone with no debt, no dependents, and stable public-sector employment.
</34>


<35>
id: persona-43-ghebremariam
title: Persona 43 - Amanuel T. Ghebremariam (Detroit GM engineer, MFJ with two kids, three foreign bank accounts and 1/3 inherited home in Asmara, FATCA/Form 8938 and FBAR, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Amanuel T. Ghebremariam, 42, naturalized Eritrean-American GM engineer in Detroit earning $153,000, MFJ with stay-at-home wife Selam and two US-born kids, Danait (9) and Nahom (6). Home on Rutherford St. (approx. $190K value, approx. $130K Rocket Mortgage), approx. $260K retirement (GM 401(k) + Fidelity rollover IRA), small Schwab brokerage, approx. $20K in Comerica checking, Chase card paid in full. Overseas is the whole point: three personal foreign accounts - Commercial Bank of Ethiopia (birr), Emirates NBD Dubai (dirham), Commercial Bank of Eritrea (nakfa) - totaling approx. $121K; approx. $2,000/month wired to his mother and two siblings in Eritrea; and a 1/3 inherited interest (with his two brothers) in the family home in Asmara where his mother lives rent-free. Signature trap: FATCA/Form 8938 on the return, separate FinCEN 114 (FBAR), and correct treatment of the inherited foreign real property.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 W-2 from GM (approx. $153,000)
  - Fidelity year-end statements: GM 401(k) and rollover IRA (approx. $260K combined)
  - Schwab taxable brokerage year-end statement and 1099
  - Comerica checking statements and full-year transaction export, including the monthly approx. $2,000 wires to Eritrea
  - Chase credit-card statements
  - Rocket Mortgage Form 1098 for the Rutherford St. home
  - Year-end statements for the three foreign accounts, balances and interest in local currency
  - Estate paperwork showing the 1/3 inherited interest in the Asmara home with date-of-death valuation
  - Amanuel's partial spreadsheet (unfinished, especially overseas)
  - Prior-year return for reference

  **Goal:** Compute the Ghebremariams' correct 2025 federal bottom line (MFJ, two qualifying children) and handle the international pieces properly. Convert each foreign account balance and interest to USD, report the interest, and file both reports: Form 8938 attached to the 1040 (MFJ living-in-US thresholds: $100K end-of-year / $150K any-time) and standalone FinCEN 114 FBAR (aggregate >$10K at any point - clearly triggered). Treat the 1/3 inherited Asmara interest correctly: inheritance not US-taxable income, basis stepped up to date-of-death FMV, no rental income since his mother lives there rent-free, and directly-held foreign real property is generally not an 8938 "specified foreign financial asset" unless held through an entity. Confirm the approx. $2,000/month remittances are non-deductible personal support; flag annual gift-tax exclusion math per recipient and whether any Form 3520 reporting is triggered. Pick up mortgage interest, CTC for both kids, and 401(k)/IRA treatment. Sanity-check Amanuel's spreadsheet, flag overseas gaps, and close with a defended view on whether the US retirement / US cash / overseas split is the right shape for a single-earner household supporting family on two continents.
</35>


<36>
id: persona-44-backstrom
title: Persona 44 - Linnea M. Bäckström (Minneapolis consulting actuary, single/no dependents, mother's 1099-LTC and Form 8853 Section C, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Linnea M. Bäckström, 47, single and childfree by choice, consulting actuary (FSA, MAAA) at Sightline Actuarial Group in Minneapolis earning $145,000. Loring Park condo owned outright, approx. $980K across Schwab taxable and retirement, approx. $50K cash, no debt. Thinks in probabilities; models her portfolio to age 100. In early March her mother Astrid entered memory care at Birchwood Terrace. Linnea is policyholder and payee on Astrid's tax-qualified per-diem long-term care policy through Mutual of Omaha, receives the 1099-LTC benefits, and pays the facility - pulling her onto Form 8853 Section C to reconcile per-diem benefits against the daily limit and Astrid's qualifying LTC expenses. The return also stacks inherited stepped-up-basis stock sold this year, a wash sale, a prior-year capital loss carryover, §199A REIT dividends, a foreign tax credit from an international fund, and NIIT exposure. She made one January estimated payment she suspects was too small. The blind spot she cannot model is who will show up for her - sharpened by being the one who shows up for her mother. Signature document: Form 8853; filing status single, under 65.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 W-2 from Sightline Actuarial Group (approx. $145,000)
  - Schwab taxable 1099 composite and year-end supplement: heavy rebalancing, inherited stepped-up-basis stock sale, wash sale, §199A REIT dividends, foreign tax paid via international fund, prior-year capital loss carryover reference
  - Schwab retirement year-end statements (traditional IRA, Roth IRA, employer plan as applicable)
  - Form 1099-LTC from Mutual of Omaha, tax-qualified and per-diem boxes checked, paid to Linnea as policyholder on Astrid's policy
  - Birchwood Terrace memory care billing for Astrid, March through year-end, itemizing room, care level, and separately stated medical services
  - Astrid's LTC policy declarations page (tax-qualified; Linnea as policyholder/payee)
  - Physician certification / plan of care establishing Astrid as chronically ill under §7702B
  - Hennepin County property tax statement for the condo (no mortgage interest)
  - Donor-advised fund contribution confirmation
  - Record of the January 2025 federal estimated tax payment
  - Linnea's half-finished spreadsheet draft (balance-due number she doesn't trust)
  - Prior-year federal return for carryover and reference

  **Goal:** Compute Linnea's correct 2025 federal bottom line (single, under 65, no dependents) and defend every piece rather than accepting the draft. Handle Form 8853 Section C for the per-diem LTC benefits: confirm tax-qualified status and Astrid's chronically ill certification, apply the 2025 per-diem exclusion against gross benefits, offset against Astrid's qualifying LTC expenses at Birchwood Terrace, and determine how much (if any) of the 1099-LTC benefit is taxable to Linnea as payee - noting Astrid is not Linnea's dependent and the medical expenses are Astrid's, not Linnea's Schedule A. Work Schedule D / Form 8949 cleanly: stepped-up basis on the inherited stock as of date of death, wash-sale disallowance and basis adjustment, prior-year capital loss carryover, reconciled to the 1099-B and supplement. Pick up §199A REIT dividends via Form 8995, foreign tax credit on Form 1116 (or de minimis election if under threshold), and NIIT on Form 8960. Confirm standard deduction beats itemizing (no mortgage interest, SALT capped, DAF is the main charitable item). Then answer honestly: what she actually owes or is refunded once everything is correct (and whether her January estimate plus withholding cleared safe-harbor), and a short read on the checking-cash pile-up versus recurring subscriptions - separating true monthly spend from Schwab transfers and the double-billed grocery charge that later reversed. Close in the unsugar-coated framing she asked for: the tax and cash questions are spreadsheet problems and answerable now; the "who shows up for me" question is not, and naming that boundary is part of the answer.
</36>


<37>
id: persona-45-osei-brandt
title: Persona 45 - The Osei-Brandt family (Raleigh NC MFJ, Form 8839 adoption credit, 2025)
content: |
  **Background:** Nathaniel Osei-Brandt, 34, civil servant with Wake County (approx. $73,400), and Claire Osei-Brandt, 33, RN at WakeMed (approx. $76,800 with shift differentials), married filing jointly in Raleigh, NC. Two kids under four: Lena (biological, age 3) and Theo (domestically adopted, finalized 2025, age 2). Both in full-time daycare at Little Acorns (approx. $26,400/year combined); Claire elected the full $5,000 dependent-care FSA. Home on Poplar Ridge Rd. (approx. $310K value, approx. $248K Truist mortgage), combined student loan debt (approx. $41K, both IDR), minivan note (approx. $18K), approx. $9K in Ally HYSA, plus Nathaniel's NC Pension + 457(b) and Claire's 403(b). Signature situation: Form 8839 adoption credit for Theo interacting with Form 2441 dependent care and CTC/ACTC. Recurring line: "we make decent money and hand most of it to daycare."

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Nathaniel's W-2 (Wake County; NC Pension pickup and 457(b) in Box 12)
  - Claire's W-2 (WakeMed; 403(b) in Box 12; $5,000 dependent-care FSA in Box 10)
  - Little Acorns year-end statement with provider name, address, and EIN for Form 2441
  - Adoption agency invoices and receipts: agency fees, home study, attorney, court costs, travel, spanning 2024-2025
  - Final decree of adoption for Theo (Wake County District Court, 2025)
  - Truist Form 1098 (mortgage interest, escrowed property taxes)
  - Wake County property tax statement
  - 1098-E student loan interest statements for both
  - Honda Financial year-end statement (context only, not deductible)
  - Ally HYSA 1099-INT
  - Small Fidelity taxable brokerage 1099 (dividends only)
  - Prior-year (2024) federal and NC returns
  - Claire's rough reconciliation spreadsheet

  **Goal:** Compute the correct 2025 federal (MFJ, two qualifying children under 6) and NC bottom lines and defend the stack of family credits. Handle Form 8839 cleanly: confirm Theo is an eligible child, aggregate qualified adoption expenses across 2024-2025, apply the 2025 per-child maximum, run the MAGI phase-out (comfortably under), and carry forward any unused nonrefundable credit. Work Form 2441 correctly: expenses capped at $6,000 for two-plus kids, reduced dollar-for-dollar by the $5,000 FSA exclusion already out of Claire's wages, leaving $1,000 eligible at their AGI's percentage. Confirm CTC/ACTC for both kids (Theo must have SSN by the return due date) and get the ordering right between the nonrefundable 8839 credit and CTC/ACTC/Saver's - some reduces tax now, some carries forward. Pick up mortgage interest and property taxes, student loan interest above-the-line (with MFJ phase-out check), retirement contributions, and small 1099-INT/DIV. Compare itemized vs standard given SALT cap. Then answer the real question behind Claire's spreadsheet: how much of the adoption credit actually lands this year versus carries forward up to five years, whether the FSA election was sized right, and whether the current savings mix is the right shape for two earners with two kids under four - or whether the daycare years are a stretch to survive rather than optimize.
</37>

<38>
id: persona-46-aturupane
title: Persona 46 - Tobias "Toby" R. Aturupane (Gainesville FL college freshman, first credit card and concentrated UTMA)
content: |
  **Background:** Tobias "Toby" R. Aturupane, 18, first-year student at the University of Florida in Gainesville, learning to manage money for the first time. His first credit card carries a $342.60 balance on a $500 limit (68.5% utilization, 26.99% APR) and he is paying the minimum. He spends about $700/month, including recurring Spotify, iCloud, and Chegg charges, a duplicate Spotify subscription billed to the card, and a dining charge that was double-billed then reversed. A grandparent-funded UTMA holds about $45,000, heavily concentrated in a single fund with large unrealized gains. He also has approx. $1,400 in checking, approx. $300 in savings, and a $5,500 federal subsidized loan disbursed across two terms.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - First credit card statement: $342.60 balance, $500 limit, 26.99% APR, minimum payment due
  - Checking account statement (approx. $1,400) with monthly spending detail
  - Starter savings statement (approx. $300)
  - Subscription export covering Spotify, iCloud, and Chegg, including the duplicate Spotify charge
  - Dining charge detail showing the double-bill and its reversal
  - UTMA custodial account statement: approx. $45,000, holdings by fund, cost basis, unrealized gains
  - Federal subsidized loan disbursement records ($5,500 across fall and spring terms)
  - UF student account statement: tuition, fees, scholarships applied, room and board
  - Toby's own budget notes and what he thinks he can afford each month

  **Goal:** Give Toby a correct read across three fronts and say which one matters first. Credit: compute what the minimum payment actually costs him in months and dollars at 26.99%, explain what 68.5% utilization does to a brand-new credit file, and state the payoff target that gets utilization under control. Budgeting: reconcile the approx. $700/month of spending against real cash flow, cancel the duplicate Spotify, net out the reversed dining charge so it is not counted twice, and identify which recurring charges survive scrutiny. Investing: assess the approx. $45,000 UTMA - name the concentration risk in a single fund, the tax consequence of the large unrealized gains if anything is sold, and whether rebalancing is worth triggering. Close with the ordering he needs: pay the card down first, do not touch the UTMA to do it, and keep the $5,500 loan and thin savings in view rather than treating the custodial account as spending money.
</38>

<39>
id: persona-47-strand-owens
title: Persona 47 - Magdalena R. Strand-Owens (Overland Park KS, goal-based planning after returning to work)
content: |
  **Background:** Magdalena R. Strand-Owens, 45, of Overland Park, KS, returned to paid work in 2025 earning $48,000 after years at home. She files jointly with her husband Gregory (approx. $135,000). Three kids at home, a paid-off house, and no other debt. The household holds approx. $410,000 in retirement savings, almost all of it in Gregory's name, plus a joint taxable brokerage account of roughly $128,000. With her income back, the couple wants to know whether their saving rate and account mix keep them on track for their goals, and how much of her own retirement she is rebuilding after the years she was out of the workforce.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Magdalena's 2025 pay statements from the dental practice ($48,000; any retirement plan availability noted)
  - Gregory's 2025 pay statements (approx. $135,000; 401(k) deferral rate and employer match)
  - Gregory's 401(k) and rollover IRA year-end statements (bulk of the approx. $410,000)
  - Magdalena's retirement accounts, if any, from before she left work
  - Joint taxable brokerage year-end statement: approx. $128,000, holdings by position, cost basis, allocation
  - Household budget and monthly cash flow worksheet
  - Bank checking and savings statements
  - Johnson County property tax statement (house paid off, no mortgage)
  - The couple's written goals: retirement timing, college for three kids, and target lifestyle

  **Goal:** Run a goal-based plan and a retail portfolio review for a household reassessing itself after a return to work. Establish the real numbers first: combined income, current savings rate as a percentage of gross, and net worth including the paid-off house. Then test the goals - is the current pace enough for the retirement age and lifestyle they name, and where do three kids' college costs sit against it? Address the account mix directly: Gregory has approx. $410,000 and Magdalena has almost nothing in her own name, so say what she should be contributing to now that she has earned income, in what order across her workplace plan, an IRA in her own name, and the joint brokerage, and why an account in her own name matters beyond the math. Review the approx. $128,000 brokerage account for allocation and diversification: what it holds, whether it is concentrated, how it fits alongside the retirement accounts as one household portfolio, and what rebalancing would cost in taxes given cost basis. Close with the trajectory: what their savings and net worth look like on the current path versus a corrected one, and the two or three changes with the most leverage.
</39>

<40>
id: persona-48-ojo
title: Persona 48 - Winifred A. Ojo (Kansas City claims adjuster, snowball in avalanche clothing)
content: |
  **Background:** Winifred A. Ojo, 34, insurance claims adjuster in Kansas City, single, renting, earning $64,000. She is attacking approx. $48,000 of debt across two credit cards, a personal loan, and student loans, while holding a $3,000 emergency fund steady rather than raiding it. She calls herself a "debt-avalanche warrior" and the label is aspirational: both her tracker and her actual payments run a snowball, with every extra dollar going to the smallest balance (the $3,480 Summit card) instead of the highest rate (the 27.99% Meridian card). The credit decision at the center of her file is whether to keep chasing quick wins by balance or reorder the payoff by interest rate.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 pay statements from the insurance carrier ($64,000; any 401(k) deferrals noted)
  - Summit credit card statements: $3,480 balance, APR, interest charged, minimum payment
  - Meridian credit card statements: balance, 27.99% APR, interest charged, minimum payment
  - Personal loan amortization schedule and year-end statement (balance, APR, interest paid)
  - Student loan servicer year-end statement: balances by loan, rates, repayment plan
  - Her debt payoff tracker spreadsheet, labeled "avalanche," with full payment history
  - Checking and savings statements showing the $3,000 emergency fund and monthly cash flow
  - Rent ledger and recurring subscription charges
  - Her own notes on the payoff order she believes she is following

  **Goal:** Settle the payoff-order question with numbers, not labels. Start by reconciling the tracker's "avalanche" label against her actual payment history and showing plainly that extra payments have gone to the $3,480 Summit card, the smallest balance, while the 27.99% Meridian card keeps compounding. Then compute both paths across all four debts using the real balances and APRs: total interest paid and payoff date under her current snowball versus a true avalanche ordered by rate, and the dollar and month difference between them. Name the tradeoff honestly - what the psychological win of clearing Summit is worth against the cost of leaving Meridian at the top of the rate stack - and give a recommendation rather than a menu. Confirm the $3,000 emergency fund should stay intact instead of being thrown at the debt, and say what it protects her from. Check whether a balance transfer or consolidation would beat reordering, and under what conditions it would not. Close with the concrete next month: which minimums, where the extra dollar goes, and what her payoff date becomes once the order is corrected.
</40>
<41>
id: persona-49-achebe-stern
title: Persona 49 - Rosalind Achebe-Stern (career changer to nursing, single income and a draining cushion)
content: |
  **Background:** Rosalind Achebe-Stern, 51, left a $130,000 marketing job to train as a nurse and has no income until she graduates around December 2027. Her husband Daniel, 52, is the sole earner at approx. $95,000. They cover the gap from a savings cushion of roughly $62,000 while carrying a mortgage, a credit card at 24.99% APR with an $8,400 balance, and a $25,000 nursing-school loan. Their son's college costs are covered by a 529 plan. Checking looks flat month to month, which hides the real problem: savings is quietly draining.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Daniel's 2025 and 2026 pay statements (approx. $95,000; 401(k) deferrals and benefit deductions)
  - Rosalind's final pay statement and severance or payout detail from the $130,000 marketing role
  - Joint checking statements covering at least six months of transfers and spending
  - Savings account statements showing the roughly $62,000 cushion and every transfer out
  - Credit card statements: $8,400 balance, 24.99% APR, interest charged, minimum payment
  - Nursing school loan documents: $25,000 balance, rate, in-school deferral terms, repayment start
  - Mortgage statement: balance, rate, escrowed taxes and insurance, monthly payment
  - Nursing program cost of attendance: tuition, fees, clinical costs, remaining terms through Dec 2027
  - 529 plan statement and the son's college billing schedule
  - Household budget as they currently understand it

  **Goal:** Tell this household the truth about its burn rate and what it can survive. First, compute the real monthly burn: total outflow against Daniel's take-home only, and expose the transfers from savings that make checking look flat while the cushion shrinks. Then answer the runway question - at the current burn, how many months does roughly $62,000 last, and does it reach graduation in December 2027 or fall short, and by how much. Third, settle the debt order: the 24.99% card at $8,400 is the expensive problem, the $25,000 school loan is likely deferred and cheaper, and the mortgage stays as is - say which dollar goes where and why paying the card down beats holding extra cash idle, while naming the minimum cushion they must keep. Then find the gap-closers: which expenses are actually cuttable, whether any earned income during school is realistic, and what happens to the plan if it is not. Leave the 529 for the son's college alone and say so explicitly rather than treating it as reserve. Close with the decision they need: the burn number, the month the cushion runs out on the current path, and the two or three changes that move that date past graduation.
</41>
<42>
id: persona-50-kahale
title: Persona 50 - Bodhi Kahale (Honolulu values investor, concentrated ESG fund and a co-op second home)
content: |
  **Background:** Bodhi Kahale, 29, marine biologist at a Honolulu nonprofit earning $58,000, partnered, renting on Oahu, no kids. He is a committed values investor: roughly $40,000 across ESG and sustainable funds, approx. $12,000 sitting in a brokerage cash sweep, and $22,000 of student loans. He will not hold anything that fails his ethical screen even when the screen costs him return. He is also a tenant-stockholder in a small housing co-op on Moloka'i, owning one unit he uses as a second home and never rents out. His largest ESG fund has grown to roughly half the portfolio, so he wants to trim it to cut concentration and seed a $32,000 house-fund reserve.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 and 2026 pay statements from the nonprofit ($58,000; any 403(b) or 401(k) deferrals and match)
  - Brokerage year-end statement: ESG and sustainable fund holdings, share lots, cost basis, unrealized gains
  - Brokerage cash sweep detail (approx. $12,000) and its current yield
  - Fund prospectuses or fact sheets for the ESG holdings, including expense ratios and screening methodology
  - Student loan servicer statement: $22,000 balance, rates by loan, repayment plan
  - Moloka'i co-op documents: stock certificate, proprietary lease, monthly maintenance assessments, co-op share of mortgage and property tax
  - Checking and savings statements with monthly cash flow
  - Oahu rent ledger
  - His written ethical screen and his notes on the $32,000 house-fund target

  **Goal:** Answer the trim question with real numbers and respect the screen rather than arguing with it. Start with the portfolio as it stands: what fraction of the roughly $40,000 sits in the single largest ESG fund, what the concentration exposes him to, and how the approx. $12,000 cash sweep counts toward the $32,000 house fund. Then price the trim: using actual share lots and cost basis, compute the capital gain from selling down to a defensible weight, the tax on it at his income including any Hawaii state tax, and the net proceeds after tax - then say how close that lands him to $32,000 and what still has to come from monthly saving. Name the reinvestment path that keeps the screen intact: what he moves the proceeds into, how it lowers concentration without violating his criteria, and what the screen costs in expense ratio or expected return so he chooses with eyes open. Address the Moloka'i co-op honestly as a second home he never rents: the maintenance and assessment drag on cash flow, what tenant-stockholder ownership means for deductibility, and whether it belongs in the plan at this income. Confirm where the $22,000 student loan sits against a taxable-gain-funded reserve. Close with the sequence: how much to trim, when, and what his next twelve months of contributions have to look like to fund the house reserve on time.
</42>
<43>
id: persona-51-calloway
title: Persona 51 - Nadia and Theo Calloway (Salem teachers, cash-flow surplus and a doubled dental charge)
content: |
  **Background:** Nadia Calloway, 38, and Theo Calloway, 40, are married public school teachers in Salem, Oregon, filing jointly, earning approx. $118,400 combined. Both paychecks land in a single Summit River Bank checking account, and they move $600 a month into a high-yield savings account. They carry a small mortgage, an auto loan, two student loans, a 403(b), and a handful of recurring subscriptions. Month to month the account looks fine, but they have never pinned down what they actually clear, Nadia's budget tracker is mostly blank with one hand-filled summary nobody has checked, a November dental charge appears to have hit twice, and the subscription list has grown without anyone auditing it.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - December 2025 pay statements for both teachers (approx. $118,400 combined gross; 403(b) deferrals, Section 125 medical, withholding, net deposit), plus both 2025 W-2s and the 2025 Form 1040
  - Summit River Bank checking statement for Q4 2025, plus the same activity as a transaction-level CSV with dates, merchant categories, amounts, running balance, and reference IDs
  - High-yield savings statement for Q4 2025 showing the $600 monthly transfers, interest credited, and APY, plus the 2025 Form 1099-INT
  - Mortgage statement: balance, rate, escrow balance, monthly payment, interest and principal split
  - Student loan servicer statement for both loans: balances, rates, scheduled payments
  - 403(b) statement: beginning and ending balance, quarterly payroll deferrals, market change
  - Budget tracker spreadsheet (DRAFT) with blank Income, Fixed Expenses, Subscriptions, and Variable Spending tabs and a hand-filled DRAFT Summary flagged as needing checking
  - The November dental charges and the recurring subscription charges as they appear in the checking activity - there is no auto loan statement and no dental provider statement or explanation of benefits in this folder

  **Goal:** Give this household a cash-flow number it can trust. First, compute the true monthly surplus: take-home against all fixed and variable outflow for October, November, and December, with the $600 transfer treated as saving rather than spending, showing all three months so one heavy month is not read as the norm, and saying what a three-month window is worth versus twelve. Second, check the DRAFT Summary against the statements: reproduce the take-home, spending tally, and leftover figure from source and name the exact dollars misclassified. Third, resolve the November dental charge: line up the two debits and the entry that follows by amount, date, reference ID, and channel, say whether the household is still out any money and what November actually cost, name the next step if a recovery is owed, and say which explanations - duplicate, pre-authorization plus settlement, or two visits - the bank record cannot settle without the provider statement. Fourth, audit the subscriptions: list each with its annual cost, separate the cuts the documents support from those needing a usage answer, and recommend specific cuts with the dollars they free. Then rank where the freed surplus goes - student loans and mortgage by rate, the 403(b), or savings - and why, flagging that the auto loan's APR and any 403(b) match cannot be determined here and naming what would settle each. Close with a concrete next month: the surplus number and its range, the corrected dental amount, the subscriptions to cancel, and where each freed dollar lands.
</43>
<44>
id: persona-52-drummond
title: Persona 52 - Percival Drummond (Omaha risk analyst, seven life insurance policies never reviewed together)
content: |
  **Background:** Percival Drummond, 47, senior risk analyst in Omaha earning $98,000, married filing jointly with two children and a part-time spouse earning approx. $18,000. He holds $380,000 across retirement accounts, owes $130,000 on a home worth approx. $280,000, and keeps roughly $60,000 in cash. He prices risk all day at work, yet for twenty years he has quietly bought it away in his own life. A rotating cast of commissioned agents sold him seven life insurance policies, each on its own autopay, none of them ever reviewed side by side. He wants to know what all of it costs him in a year and whether he is over insured, before he sits down with a planner.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 and 2026 pay statements for Percival ($98,000; 401(k) deferrals, employer match, benefit deductions) and the spouse (approx. $18,000)
  - All seven life insurance policies: type (term, whole, universal, variable), face amount, premium and billing frequency, issue date, cash value, surrender charges, riders
  - Employer group life certificate and any supplemental coverage already included in benefits
  - Annual statements for any permanent policies: cash value, dividends, internal cost of insurance, loans against the policy
  - Checking and card statements showing every insurance autopay across twelve months
  - Retirement account statements totaling approx. $380,000, with contribution rates
  - Mortgage statement: $130,000 balance, rate, escrow, monthly payment; plus a home value estimate near $280,000
  - Cash and savings statements totaling roughly $60,000
  - Household budget and monthly fixed obligations
  - Disability coverage documents, if any

  **Goal:** Put all seven policies on one page and tell him the truth about the total. Start with the annual cost: sum every premium across the twelve months of statements, convert mixed billing frequencies to an annual figure, and state what percentage of gross household income and of monthly cash flow the insurance stack consumes. Then compute the coverage he actually needs: income replacement for the spouse and two children, the $130,000 mortgage payoff, education costs, and final expenses, offset by the $380,000 in retirement assets, roughly $60,000 cash, employer group life, and the spouse's earnings - and give a defensible need number rather than a rule of thumb. Compare need against total in-force face amount and say plainly whether he is over insured and by how much. Then rank the policies: which to keep and why, which are redundant, and for any permanent policy show cash value against surrender charges and internal cost so a lapse-versus-surrender-versus-1035-exchange decision is grounded in numbers, and warn where cancelling a policy loses an underwriting advantage he could not re-buy at 47. Name the gap he may have missed while over-buying life coverage: disability insurance on the primary earner. Close with the agenda for the planner meeting: annual insurance spend, need number, the specific policies to drop or restructure, the dollars that frees, and where those dollars should go.
</44>
<45>
id: persona-53-solvberg
title: Persona 53 - Beatrix Solvberg (Minneapolis program manager, a Swedish inheritance blurring her real spending)
content: |
  **Background:** Beatrix Solvberg, 32, single nonprofit program manager in Minneapolis earning $61,000, roughly $1,702.53 net per semimonthly paycheck, renting, carrying approx. $16,500 in MOHELA student loans with a normally modest checking account. This spring she inherited from her grandmother in Sweden: a house valued at 3.8 million SEK, roughly $360,000, plus approx. 950,000 SEK, roughly $90,000, held in cash at Handelsbanken. The inheritance triggers a Form 3520 filing, which is context here rather than a tax bill. In July she wired $40,000 into her US checking and then parked it: $30,000 into savings and $8,000 into a new Vanguard brokerage account, leaving her July statement full of one-off money movements. Having gone from budgeting groceries to managing a house abroad, she has lost her handle on ordinary finances and wants a clean read.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2026 pay statements (approx. $1,702.53 net semimonthly; retirement deferrals and benefit deductions)
  - July checking statement showing the $40,000 inbound wire and every transfer out
  - Savings account statement covering the $30,000 transfer
  - Vanguard brokerage statements: the $8,000 opening funding and holdings
  - MOHELA statement: approx. $16,500 balance, rates by loan, repayment plan, monthly payment
  - Minneapolis lease and rent ledger
  - Card statements for July with recurring subscription charges, refunds, and reversals
  - Handelsbanken statements for the inherited cash (approx. 950,000 SEK) and the wire documentation
  - Swedish estate and property documents for the inherited house (3.8 million SEK) and any carrying costs
  - Form 3520 filing notes or preparer correspondence

  **Goal:** Separate her real life from her inheritance and hand her three trustworthy numbers. Start by classifying every July line item: ordinary spending, inheritance transfers, refunds, and reversals - and exclude the $40,000 wire, the $30,000 savings move, and the $8,000 brokerage funding from spending entirely so a one-time windfall does not masquerade as cash flow. Then compute true monthly spending from what remains, netting refunds and reversals against the original charges instead of double-counting either side, and flag any charge that is one-off so a single July anomaly does not set her baseline. Second, compute her savings rate against take-home only: what she saves from the approx. $3,405 monthly net, stated separately from inherited money, because a rate inflated by the wire tells her nothing about her habits. Third, list every recurring subscription with its monthly and annual cost, mark overlaps and anything unused, and name specific cuts with the dollars they free. Treat the Form 3520 as a filing obligation, not a tax - say so plainly so she stops worrying about a bill that is not coming. Then address the house abroad as an ongoing cost center rather than a windfall: carrying costs, currency exposure, and the fact that decisions about keeping or selling belong in a separate conversation. Close with the clean read she asked for: monthly spending, savings rate, subscriptions to cancel, and whether her MOHELA loans deserve any of the parked cash.
</45>
<46>
id: persona-54-feldman
title: Persona 54 - Marcus and Rachel Feldman (Silver Spring, corrected basis on a busy brokerage year)
content: |
  **Background:** Marcus and Rachel Feldman file jointly in Silver Spring, Maryland, raising two children, including an adult disabled son supported by a funded special needs trust. Marcus is a software architect and Rachel a part-time therapist, with roughly $200,000 of combined wages. Alongside retirement accounts and a Maryland ABLE account, they hold a personal taxable brokerage account that had a busy year: they trimmed a concentrated single-stock position, harvested losses, and received dividends, a spinoff, and return of capital distributions. The 1099-B basis the broker reported does not tell the whole story. They want to know what their taxable capital gain and tax actually are once basis is corrected, whether their withholding and estimated payments cover it, and whether they are still too concentrated or holding the wrong assets in the wrong accounts.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2025 and 2026 pay statements for Marcus and Rachel (roughly $200,000 combined; 401(k) deferrals, benefit deductions, federal and Maryland withholding)
  - Taxable brokerage statements and 1099-B with realized lot detail, including covered and noncovered lots and any wash-sale adjustments
  - Trade confirmations for the single-stock trim and every tax-loss-harvest sale, with acquisition dates and cost per lot
  - 1099-DIV showing qualified and ordinary dividends and any return of capital (nondividend) distributions
  - Spinoff corporate-action notice with the basis allocation ratio between parent and spun-off shares
  - Prior-year return and any estimated tax payment records or vouchers
  - Retirement account statements: balances, contributions, holdings by account
  - Maryland ABLE account statement: balance, contributions, allowable uses
  - Special needs trust documents and trust account statements, plus any trust tax filing
  - Checking and card statements covering twelve months of household spending and recurring subscriptions

  **Goal:** Correct the basis first, then tell them the real number. Rebuild each sold lot: apply the return of capital distributions as basis reductions, allocate basis across the spinoff using the corporate-action ratio, and check every harvested loss against wash-sale rules including replacement purchases in other accounts. Say explicitly where the broker's reported basis was wrong and by how much, because noncovered lots and return of capital are exactly where 1099-B figures go stale. Then compute the actual taxable gain split into short-term and long-term, net it against harvested losses, apply the correct rates including qualified dividend treatment and any net investment income tax, and add Maryland state tax to reach one total. Compare that to withholding plus estimated payments and state whether they owe, are covered, or face an underpayment penalty - with the exact catch-up payment and date if one is needed. Then judge the portfolio: what percentage the trimmed position still represents and whether the concentration risk is resolved, and whether asset location is right - interest-bearing and high-turnover holdings inside retirement accounts, tax-efficient holdings in taxable, with the ABLE account used for what only it can do. Note that the special needs trust is a separate taxpayer whose decisions do not belong in this personal return. Close with a clean read on the household: true monthly spending, recurring subscriptions with annual costs and specific cuts, and where the freed dollars and any remaining tax liability land.
</46>
<47>
id: persona-55-mbatha
title: Persona 55 - Saffron Mbatha (Brooklyn lawyer, runway math against a concentrated position)
content: |
  **Background:** Saffron, 41, single, is a burned-out senior associate at a Brooklyn law firm with about $1.4M across her investment and retirement accounts and roughly $80,000 of cash in checking. She wants to know if she can afford to quit for lower-paid legal-aid work. After gifting a block of appreciated stock to a donor-advised fund this year, her taxable brokerage sits near $1.0M, but one holding, Aurelix Technologies, is about 43 percent of it and carries a big unrealized gain, so trimming it is not free. She saves hard, sweeping cash into the brokerage monthly, but subscriptions and an odd double charge blur her true burn rate. Her line: "I have the money to walk away. I don't have the nerve."

  **Documents:**
  - 2025 W-2 plus year-to-date earnings summary (wages, 401(k) deferral, Social Security and Medicare including the 0.9 percent surtax, NY State and City withholding)
  - 2025 final semimonthly pay stub with the full 24-period YTD register (shows the Social Security cap crossing mid-year)
  - Taxable brokerage statement (H2 2025): positions totaling approx. $1.0M with Aurelix at approx. 43 percent, cost basis and unrealized gain per position, the charitable stock delivery, and a tax-year income summary
  - Consolidated 1099 (INT and DIV): interest, ordinary and qualified dividends, capital-gain distributions (no 1099-B, no sales)
  - Retirement statement: Traditional IRA and 401(k) balances, contributions, holdings
  - Checking statement and matching CSV (H2 2025): payroll, co-op maintenance, share-loan payment, utilities, subscriptions, monthly sweeps, and a reversed duplicate; ends near $80,000
  - Form 1098 (co-op share-loan interest) and the co-op Section 216 letter (her share of building real estate taxes)
  - Form 8283 and the donor-advised-fund package documenting the stock gift
  - 2024 filed tax return (context and safe harbor)
  - A half-finished tax-tracker spreadsheet whose bottom line she does not trust

  **Goal:** Give her a runway number she can act on. (1) Find her true burn rate from the checking and card activity, counting the brokerage sweeps as saving and netting out the reversed charge, reported as a monthly range not just an average. (2) Compute runway: how long the approx. $80,000 cash lasts at that burn, how much longer the taxable brokerage extends it after tax on a sale, and what is truly liquid versus not (co-op equity and retirement are not). (3) Price the Aurelix trim: long-term capital-gains tax plus NIIT plus NY State and City tax on the gain, net proceeds, and what a drop in Aurelix would cost her with no paycheck. (4) Reconcile her distrusted draft against the real 2025 tax picture (30 percent charitable cap and carryforward, NIIT, additional Medicare, preferential dividend rate). Close with the decision as arithmetic, not nerve: the burn, the runway in months, the tax cost of derisking, and the cushion to build before she resigns.
</47>
<48>
id: persona-56-mansour
title: Persona 56 - Yusuf Mansour (Dearborn engineer, halal home purchase against a hidden concentration)
content: |
  **Background:** Yusuf Mansour, 33, is a mechanical engineer in Dearborn, Michigan, married with two young kids and strong ties to family back home. He follows halal finance strictly: no interest-based debt, Sharia-compliant funds only, and approx. $50,000 parked in non-interest cash that serves as both his safety buffer and a drag on returns. His roughly $128,000 investment account looks diversified across four funds but is quietly concentrated, with one fund near 42 percent and heavy overlapping technology exposure underneath. He is saving for a first home around $310,000, reachable only through a halal co-ownership structure that requires a larger 20 percent down payment plus a cash cushion afterward. His real question: when can he actually buy, and does he get there faster by trimming the concentrated fund or by leaving it alone and letting his true savings rate do the work.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 2026 pay statements (salary, retirement deferrals, benefit deductions, federal and Michigan withholding)
  - Non-interest checking and cash account statements totaling approx. $50,000, with any purification or interest-avoidance notes
  - Investment account statements: approx. $128,000 across four Sharia-compliant funds, with position weights
  - Fund fact sheets and top-holdings disclosures for each of the four funds, showing sector and issuer overlap
  - Lot-level cost basis detail for the concentrated fund near 42 percent, with acquisition dates and unrealized gain per lot
  - Retirement account statements: balances, contribution rate, Sharia-compliant fund lineup available in the plan
  - Halal co-ownership or diminishing-musharaka term sheet: 20 percent down payment requirement, monthly obligation, fees, post-close reserve expectation
  - Twelve months of checking and card statements covering household spending, recurring subscriptions, and remittances sent to family
  - Current lease and rent ledger
  - Prior-year tax return
  - Any target listings or price comparables around the $310,000 range

  **Goal:** Tell him a date, then tell him what changes it. Start with the money that is actually available: separate the approx. $50,000 in non-interest cash into emergency reserve, the post-close cushion the co-ownership structure expects, and what is genuinely spendable at closing. Then compute his true savings rate from twelve months of activity - treat remittances to family as a fixed obligation rather than discretionary spending, net refunds and reversals against the original charges, and state the monthly range next to the average. From that, calculate months to the 20 percent down payment of roughly $62,000 plus closing costs plus the required cushion, and give a purchase window as a date range rather than a vague horizon. Second, expose the concentration honestly: look through all four funds to the underlying holdings and show the real technology and single-issuer exposure, because four funds that share the same top names is one bet wearing four labels. Say what percentage of the $128,000 is truly duplicated. Third, price the trim rather than recommending it in the abstract: for the fund near 42 percent, compute long-term and short-term capital gains tax lot by lot and give after-tax proceeds, then show whether redeploying into the down payment moves the purchase date meaningfully or barely at all compared with leaving it invested and saving at his current rate. Answer the question he asked - trim or leave alone - with both timelines side by side. Respect the halal constraints throughout: no interest-based mortgage comparisons, no interest-bearing savings recommendations, and note where the co-ownership structure costs more than a conventional loan so the tradeoff is explicit rather than hidden. Close with the specific next actions: the monthly savings target, which subscriptions to cut and what that frees, whether to trim, and the earliest realistic month he can make an offer.
</48>
<49>
id: persona-57-bramwell
title: Persona 57 - Ulysses T. Bramwell (Sarasota retiree, trimming a low-basis position against a 25-year horizon)
content: |
  **Background:** Ulysses T. Bramwell, 69, is a retired Doctor of Dental Surgery living on Siesta Key in Sarasota, Florida, in a home that is fully paid off. He was widowed three years ago and now has a domestic partner, Viveca L. Renner, whom he has deliberately not married and to whom he sends monthly support. He lives off a roughly $1.8M taxable brokerage account at Gulfstream Capital plus a Traditional IRA, supplemented by Social Security. That taxable account has become concentrated in a single long-held, low-basis noncovered stock, Meridian Pacific Corp, and he wants to trim it back to about 10 percent of the account without misjudging the tax cost. He is also unsure what he actually spends to live each year versus what merely flows through his checking account, and he wants to know whether his spending, the support for Viveca, and his taxes are sustainable over a 25-year retirement horizon. His own note, in his words:

  > A NOTE FOR MY FINANCIAL REVIEW
  >
  > Ulysses T. Bramwell, 3820 Siesta Key Circle, Sarasota, FL 34242
  >
  > I retired from the dental practice a few years back and I live off the investment accounts and Social Security. The house is paid for. I'm comfortable, but three things have been nagging at me, and my advisor left me a half-finished worksheet I don't fully trust, so I want a clear-eyed second look.
  >
  > First - the big one. Meridian Pacific has been very good to me, but it has grown into far too large a piece of my Gulfstream taxable account. I don't want any single stock to be more than about ten percent of that account. I'd like to know the smallest amount of it I'd have to sell to get back under that line, and - this is what really matters - what selling it would actually cost me in taxes this year. I bought that block a long time ago and it's worth a great deal more than I paid, so I know there's a bill attached. I want the real number, done properly, not a scary back-of-the-envelope guess.
  >
  > Second, I keep telling everyone I live on about ninety thousand a year, but between the family, the boat, the club and everything else I honestly am not sure anymore. I've pulled a full year of the checking account. Cut through the noise of the year's one-off items and tell me what I actually spend to live - my honest, ongoing run-rate.
  >
  > Third, put those together for me. I send Viveca some help every month, and I'd like to keep doing that. Given what I really spend to live, that help for Viveca, and what I hold across the taxable account and the IRA, is this sustainable if I live another twenty-five years, or am I quietly drawing it down too fast?
  >
  > Everything the year produced is in this folder - the account statements, the cost-basis records, the tax return, the Social Security statement, the bank records, and the policy my advisor and I set. Tell me the right numbers, and show me how you got them.
  >
  > - U.T.B.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Gulfstream Capital taxable brokerage statements: approx. $1.8M in positions with weights, showing the Meridian Pacific Corp concentration
  - Lot-level cost-basis records for Meridian Pacific, including noncovered lots with acquisition dates and original purchase price
  - Traditional IRA statements: balance, holdings, and required minimum distribution schedule
  - Consolidated 1099 (INT, DIV, B): interest, ordinary and qualified dividends, capital-gain distributions
  - Social Security benefit statement (SSA-1099 and current benefit letter)
  - Prior-year filed tax return and any estimated tax payment records
  - Twelve months of checking account statements covering all inflows and outflows, including the monthly support sent to Viveca
  - Investment policy statement he and his advisor set, including the 10 percent single-position limit
  - The advisor's half-finished worksheet, whose conclusions he does not trust

  **Goal:** Answer his three questions with arithmetic he can check. (1) Compute the minimum Meridian Pacific sale that brings the position under 10 percent of the taxable account, remembering that selling shrinks both the position and the account, so the naive difference overstates the sale needed. Then price it properly: identify lots, use the noncovered basis records rather than any broker-reported figure, compute long-term capital gains at the correct bracket given his other income, add net investment income tax where it applies, and note that Florida has no state income tax. Show how the sale itself pushes taxable income and therefore may change the rate that applies to part of the gain, and state the after-tax proceeds. (2) Separate his true living run-rate from gross checking flow: strip out transfers between his own accounts, one-off items the year happened to contain, and reimbursements, then report an honest ongoing annual figure with a monthly range - and say plainly whether the $90,000 he quotes is right, low, or high, and by how much. (3) Build the 25-year sustainability picture: combine the run-rate, the monthly support to Viveca, Social Security, required minimum distributions from the IRA, and the tax drag including the year of the trim, then state the sustainable withdrawal rate his plan implies and whether he clears it. Flag that the support to Viveca is a gift rather than a deductible expense and that not being married changes his filing status, survivor benefits, and estate exposure - facts that belong in the answer even though he did not ask. Where his advisor's worksheet disagrees with your numbers, say which line is wrong and why. Close with the specifics: shares to sell, the tax bill, the honest run-rate, and whether the plan holds for twenty-five years or needs a named adjustment.
</49>
<50>
id: persona-58-prewitt
title: Persona 58 - Gerald and Nadine Prewitt (Houston retirees, a concentrated legacy position against an unsustainable draw)
content: |
  **Background:** Gerald and Nadine Prewitt are a retired married couple in their late 60s to early 70s in Houston, filing jointly. They live on Gerald's Meridian Energy pension, Social Security, income from a paid-off rental condo, and withdrawals from a $1.21M Crestline brokerage account. The portfolio is dangerously concentrated: legacy Meridian Energy stock is 48 percent of the account at a $23 cost basis, carrying roughly $492,000 of unrealized gain. They spend approximately $197,000 a year against about $132,000 of fixed income, quietly filling the gap by pulling from the portfolio at an unsustainable 5.94 percent rate. They want to trim the concentrated position to reduce risk but do not understand its true after-tax cost, and they believe their cash flow is fine. Gerald's own notes:

  > PERSONAL NOTES - Gerry and Nadine Prewitt, prepared for our year-end financial review
  >
  > **Background.** I retired four years ago after a long career as a reservoir engineer. Nadine and I are both in good health. We live on my Meridian Energy pension, our Social Security, income from the Bayou Bend rental condo, and withdrawals from the Crestline brokerage account. We own our home on Rosemont Oaks free and clear, and the rental condo has no mortgage either. We have four grandchildren and we would like to help them generously while we are still around to enjoy it.
  >
  > **Things I want to think through this year**
  >
  > 1. Concentration. A big chunk of the Crestline account is still in Meridian Energy stock (MEC) that I accumulated during my career. It has grown into far and away our largest holding and I know that is risky to leave sitting in one company. I am thinking about selling 1,500 of those shares to bring the position down. Before I do, I want to understand what it would actually cost us after taxes - the shares were bought decades ago at a very low price, so most of the proceeds would be gain. I do not want any surprises at tax time.
  > 2. Are we drawing down too fast? Every few months I wire money from the Crestline account into our Gulf Coast checking to cover the bills. I want to know what we are really spending each year to live, and whether the amount we are pulling from the portfolio is sustainable or whether we are drawing it down faster than is wise.
  > 3. Subscriptions and recurring charges. I suspect we are paying for a few things every month that we no longer use. I would like a clean list of the recurring charges so we can cancel what we do not need.
  >
  > I have gathered the year's statements and tax forms. The half-finished spreadsheet I started (tax_tracker_DRAFT) has some numbers in it but I am not confident I did the tax part right, and several cells are still blank where I need to pull figures from the statements.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Crestline brokerage statements: $1.21M in positions with weights, showing MEC at 48 percent
  - Lot-level cost-basis records for the MEC position ($23 basis, decades-old noncovered lots, approx. $492,000 unrealized gain)
  - Consolidated 1099 (INT, DIV, B): interest, ordinary and qualified dividends, capital-gain distributions
  - Meridian Energy pension statement and 1099-R
  - Social Security benefit statements (SSA-1099) for both spouses
  - Bayou Bend rental condo records: lease, rent roll, operating expenses, depreciation schedule
  - Twelve months of Gulf Coast checking statements, including the periodic wires in from Crestline
  - Card statements covering household spending and recurring subscriptions
  - Prior-year filed tax return and estimated tax payment records
  - tax_tracker_DRAFT spreadsheet: partly filled, with blank cells and a tax section Gerald does not trust

  **Goal:** Price the trim, then tell them the truth about the draw. (1) Compute the after-tax cost of selling 1,500 MEC shares: identify the lots, use the $23 basis records rather than any broker-reported figure for noncovered lots, compute the long-term gain, apply the correct capital-gains rate given all their other income, add net investment income tax where it applies, and note that Texas has no state income tax. Show how the sale itself lifts taxable income and can push part of the gain into a higher rate and increase the taxable share of Social Security, then state the net proceeds and what percentage of the account MEC still represents afterward - answering whether 1,500 shares is actually enough to fix the concentration. (2) Separate true living spending from gross checking flow: strip transfers between their own accounts, the Crestline wires, one-off items, and reimbursements, then report an honest annual run-rate with a monthly range against the roughly $132,000 of fixed income. Name the gap in dollars and compute the real portfolio withdrawal rate, and say plainly that a 5.94 percent draw against a concentrated portfolio is not sustainable over a retirement of their length - with the withdrawal amount and spending level that would be. (3) Give the clean recurring-charge list with monthly and annualized costs, flag duplicates and services matched to nothing they use, and recommend specific cancellations with the dollars each frees. Where tax_tracker_DRAFT disagrees with your numbers, say which line is wrong and why, and fill the blanks from the statements. Close with the decision as arithmetic: shares to sell, the tax bill, the honest run-rate, the sustainable draw, and what has to change - including how gifting to the four grandchildren fits once the spending gap is named.
</50>
<51>
id: persona-59-hollis
title: Persona 59 - Marcus T. Hollis (Sparks carpenter, buffer versus a 27.99 percent card after bankruptcy)
content: |
  **Background:** Marcus T. Hollis, 49, is a self-employed finish carpenter in Sparks, Nevada, two years past a Chapter 7 bankruptcy and rebuilding slowly. He files jointly with his wife Renata; their son Diego is 15. The household lives on one lumpy 1099 income of about $76,780 in net profit. His whole financial life runs through two lines of credit: a secured Visa at 27.99 percent APR pinned near its $1,500 limit, and a truck loan he needs in order to take jobs. He has roughly $8,000 in savings, no retirement after it was wiped out, and a 540 credit score he is climbing back from. The call he loses sleep over is whether to throw his thin cash flow at the punishing card to cut utilization and lift his score, or keep it as a buffer against the next lean month. His own notes:

  > MY MONEY - WHERE THINGS STAND (notes to myself). Marcus Hollis, Sparks, NV, July 2026
  >
  > The short version: I lost it all once. This time I'm building slow, and it's all mine.
  >
  > Two years out from the Chapter 7 now. Renata and I are doing this together - her, me, and Diego (he's 15, eats like he's 25). I'm back on my own tools as a finish carpenter. Work is steady enough; the money just comes in lumpy - big draws from the GCs some weeks, nothing the next, plus the smaller homeowner jobs. It's a 1099 life now, so nobody's taking taxes out for me. Everything runs through the one checking account and the little savings cushion I've been trying to protect.
  >
  > **What I'm carrying:**
  > - The secured Visa. Low limit, brutal APR. I put down the deposit to get it and I've been carrying a balance. The minimum barely moves it. It's the thing I lie awake on.
  > - The truck loan. I need the truck to take jobs, so that payment isn't optional.
  > - The savings buffer. It's not much. It's the thing that stands between us and the next lean month. Last time I had no cushion and it wrecked me.
  >
  > **What I'm trying to decide:**
  > - Do I throw my spare cash at that secured card to knock the balance down - lower the utilization, nudge my score up off 540 - or do I keep the cash as a buffer for when work slows down? If I've got money to put to debt, does it go to the card or the truck?
  > - I started a worksheet (the spreadsheet) but a buddy helped with the tax tab and I don't fully trust it. I still need to nail down what I actually owe for 2025 before I go throwing money around - the return isn't done, I just have the 1099s, the business books, and the estimated payments I made. First real full year on my own, so I honestly don't know where it lands.
  > - And I keep meaning to look at those recurring charges - streaming, the gym, whatever else - to see what's quietly leaking out each month.
  >
  > Goal: rebuild the credit, keep the family steady, don't blow up the buffer, and stop letting that card quietly eat my progress. Slow and steady. All of it mine this time.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - 1099-NEC and 1099-MISC forms from general contractors, plus records of cash and homeowner jobs not on any 1099
  - Business books: income and expense ledger, mileage log, tools and materials receipts, home-office notes
  - Estimated tax payment records for 2025 (dates and amounts), with no filed return yet
  - Prior-year return covering the partial self-employment year
  - Twelve months of checking statements showing lumpy deposits, transfers to savings, and household spending
  - Savings account statements, ending near $8,000
  - Secured Visa statements: $1,500 limit, 27.99 percent APR, balance, minimum payment, security deposit terms
  - Truck loan statement and amortization schedule: balance, APR, monthly payment, payoff date
  - Credit report summary showing the 540 score, the discharged Chapter 7 tradelines, and current utilization
  - Chapter 7 discharge paperwork
  - Card statements covering recurring subscriptions
  - A half-finished worksheet spreadsheet whose tax tab he does not trust

  **Goal:** Answer the question he actually asked - card or buffer - with numbers, not encouragement. (1) Nail the 2025 tax bill first, because he should not deploy cash he already owes: rebuild net profit from the 1099s plus the unreported cash and homeowner jobs, apply legitimate business deductions including mileage and the home office, compute self-employment tax and the deductible half, add income tax on the joint return with Diego's child tax credit, and note that Nevada has no state income tax. Compare the total to his estimated payments, state whether he owes or is covered and by how much, whether an underpayment penalty applies, and the exact catch-up amount and date. Where the spreadsheet's tax tab disagrees, say which line is wrong and why. (2) Find his true monthly spending and, because 1099 income is lumpy, report the range across the worst months, not just an average - then say how many months of that spending the $8,000 buffer actually covers, and how many it should cover for someone whose income arrives unevenly. (3) Compare the two uses of every spare dollar side by side: the interest saved by paying down a 27.99 percent card versus the truck loan's rate, and the separate credit-score effect of cutting utilization on a $1,500 limit, which moves the score far more per dollar than the balance size suggests. Say what utilization target to hit and the dollar payment that gets him there. Then answer the tradeoff honestly: how much buffer to protect before any extra goes to the card, given that a lost buffer would put the spending straight back on the same card at 27.99 percent. (4) List recurring charges with monthly and annualized costs and name specific cancellations and the dollars they free. Close with a concrete plan for the next three months: the tax reserve to set aside first, the buffer floor to hold, the card payment amount, and where the freed subscription dollars go.
</51>
<52>
id: persona-60-beaumont
title: Persona 60 - Nadia and Elliot Beaumont (Chicago consultants, four decisions that never talked to each other)
content: |
  **Background:** Nadia and Elliot Beaumont are married consultants in their mid-30s living in Chicago, with no kids and a combined income of about $360,000. Despite that income they have accumulated only around $250,000 in investments and roughly $40,000 in cash, and they carry no student loans. Most of the investments sit in one taxable brokerage account that is badly over-concentrated - a single industry stock is about 46 percent of it - and it has never been rebalanced. The $40,000 in cash sits idle in checking and savings earning almost nothing, while they carry a balance on a 24.99 percent travel credit card and still owe roughly $50,000 on a financed Porsche at 7.9 percent. Every dollar was decided in isolation, so their real question is whether the idle cash should pay down the car, stay as their only safety buffer, or go somewhere else entirely. Their own note:

  > PERSONAL NOTE - Beaumont household
  >
  > Nadia and Elliot Beaumont. Married five years, both in our thirties, no kids and none planned. We live in the West Loop and both consult - combined about $360,000 a year before tax. No student loans. On paper we should be in great shape. It doesn't feel that way.
  >
  > Here's what we actually have, and the thing is, none of it seems to talk to each other:
  > - Investments: roughly $250k total. Most of it is one joint brokerage account we opened years ago and basically never touched. It's all names from our own industry - the consulting/tech world we work in - and one of them has run up so much it's now almost half the account. We've never rebalanced. There are two small 401(k)s and an old rollover IRA from a job Nadia left; those just sit there too.
  > - Cash: about $40k sitting between checking and savings, earning basically nothing. It's the only real cushion we have.
  > - The car: I (Elliot) financed a Porsche a couple years ago. There's still a five-figure balance on it. Honestly I never questioned the rate when we signed.
  > - One travel credit card we lean on for trips; we carry a balance on it more months than not.
  >
  > The question we keep circling: should we take that idle $40k and just kill the car loan? Or is the cash better kept as our safety net - or put toward something else entirely? Would we have to sell some of that one big stock position to free up money, and does that cost us more than it saves? Every piece was decided on its own, and I have no idea if any of it is actually working together. We want one coherent answer, not four separate ones.
  >
  > - E.B.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Pay statements for both spouses (combined approx. $360,000; 401(k) deferrals, benefit deductions, federal and Illinois withholding)
  - Joint taxable brokerage statements: approx. $250,000 total with position weights, showing the single holding near 46 percent
  - Lot-level cost-basis detail for the concentrated position, with acquisition dates and unrealized gain per lot
  - Consolidated 1099 (INT, DIV): interest, ordinary and qualified dividends, capital-gain distributions
  - Two 401(k) statements plus Nadia's rollover IRA statement: balances, contribution rates, fund lineups and available match
  - Checking and savings statements totaling approx. $40,000, showing the interest rate actually earned
  - Travel credit card statements: 24.99 percent APR, balance, minimum payment, rewards terms
  - Auto loan statement and amortization schedule for the Porsche: approx. $50,000 balance, 7.9 percent APR, monthly payment, payoff date, any prepayment terms
  - Twelve months of card and checking activity covering household spending, travel, and recurring subscriptions
  - Prior-year filed tax return
  - Lease or housing records for the West Loop apartment

  **Goal:** Give them the single coherent answer they asked for, with the four pieces priced against each other. Start by ranking every available use of a dollar by its actual return: paying the 24.99 percent card is the highest guaranteed return available and should come before the car; any unmatched 401(k) room and any employer match left on the table belongs in the ranking too, because an unclaimed match beats every debt payoff. Then answer the car question directly: compute the interest saved by paying off roughly $50,000 at 7.9 percent versus keeping the cash, and say plainly that using the entire $40,000 cannot both retire the loan and preserve a buffer - so name the split. Set the buffer floor from their true monthly spending, calculated from twelve months of activity with travel and one-offs separated from ongoing costs, and note that two consultants with variable client income need more months of cushion than two salaried employees. Say what the idle cash is costing them per year against a high-yield alternative. Second, address the concentration on its own terms: state the real single-name exposure and note the compounding risk that the position is in the same industry that pays both their salaries, so a downturn hits income and portfolio together. Price the trim rather than assuming it: long-term and short-term capital gains lot by lot, the 3.8 percent net investment income tax at their income level, Illinois state tax, and after-tax proceeds - then answer whether selling to fund the car payoff costs more than the 7.9 percent it saves. Check asset location as well, since interest-bearing and high-turnover holdings belong in the retirement accounts and the rollover IRA is doing nothing by default. Third, name the real problem behind the numbers: $360,000 of income producing only $250,000 of investments means the savings rate, not the allocation, is the binding constraint - so compute their actual savings rate and the rate they need. Close with one sequenced plan: the card first, the buffer floor to hold in a high-yield account, whether and how much of the car to retire, whether to trim the concentrated position and by how much, the subscriptions to cut, and the monthly savings target that makes the rest work.
</52>
<53>
id: persona-61-cardenas
title: Persona 61 - Marisol A. Cardenas (Westminster medical biller, four ways to kill one card)
content: |
  **Background:** Marisol A. Cardenas is 43, a medical biller in Westminster, California earning about $61,440, and she runs the finances for a three-generation household made up of her spouse, two kids, and both aging parents. As her parents age she has taken over their affairs and filed Form 56 to become their IRS point person, and they own a $715,000 home carrying a $364,790 mortgage. She holds roughly $51,824 in a 401(k), the family pools about $19,843 in savings, and an $11,240 credit card sits in her name. She wants that balance gone and is weighing four ways to do it. Her own note:

  > PERSONAL NOTE - Marisol Cardenas
  >
  > Three generations, one bank account I basically run. My husband, our two kids, and now both of my parents - everyone's needs land on my spreadsheet. This year I filed Form 56 and took over my parents' finances, so I'm their IRS point person too.
  >
  > The thing keeping me up is the Summit Rewards Visa in my name. The balance is around eleven thousand and the interest feels like it never moves. I want it gone. I keep going in circles on HOW to kill it:
  >
  > (A) Just pay it down from our income / cash flow each month.
  > (B) Drain our savings and wipe it out in one shot.
  > (C) Pull the money out of my 401(k) - it's my own money, right?
  > (D) Have my parents open a HELOC on their house and gift me the payoff.
  >
  > I also wonder whether it makes sense to transfer my parents' house into my name at some point, or whether the HELOC-and-gift idea is smart or a mistake.
  >
  > What I actually need to figure out:
  > - Which option really costs the least once you count everything (penalties, taxes, fees, risk), not just the sticker interest rate?
  > - Realistically, how many months until this card hits zero, and what would it take?
  > - Is there money hiding in our monthly spending that I could redirect at it?
  >
  > Everything I have is in this folder - pay stub, bank statements, the card statement, the 401(k) statement, my parents' mortgage statement, the HELOC disclosure, our tax return, and my half-finished tracker spreadsheet. Help me think it through.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Pay stub showing approx. $61,440 annual wages, 401(k) deferral, benefit deductions, and federal and California withholding
  - Checking and savings statements for the pooled household account, with savings near $19,843
  - Summit Rewards Visa statements: approx. $11,240 balance, APR, minimum payment, and interest charged each month
  - 401(k) statement showing approx. $51,824 balance, fund lineup, employer match terms, and loan and hardship-withdrawal provisions
  - Parents' mortgage statement on the $715,000 home: approx. $364,790 balance, rate, payment, and escrow
  - HELOC disclosure for the proposed line on the parents' home: rate structure, draw terms, fees, and closing costs
  - Prior-year filed joint tax return
  - Form 56 filing confirming she is the IRS point person for her parents
  - Twelve months of card and checking activity covering household spending and recurring subscriptions
  - A half-finished tracker spreadsheet with blank cells and untrusted tax figures

  **Goal:** Price all four options against each other and give her one payoff plan. (1) Compute the all-in cost of each path, not the sticker rate: (A) paying from cash flow - total interest and months to zero at a specific monthly payment; (B) draining savings - the interest saved against the cost of having no cushion for a household supporting six people, and what it would cost to rebuild it; (C) the 401(k) withdrawal - federal income tax at her marginal rate, California income tax, the 10 percent early-withdrawal penalty at 43, and the lost future growth, then state the gross withdrawal actually required to net $11,240 and compare a 401(k) loan as the cheaper variant if her plan allows one; (D) the parents' HELOC - closing costs and variable-rate risk, the fact that it puts her parents' $715,000 home behind her consumer debt, and the gift-tax reporting question, with the caveat that the parents' income and ability to service a new line cannot be determined from these documents. Rank the four plainly and say which she should not do. (2) Address the house-transfer question separately: name the loss of stepped-up basis at death, the Medicaid look-back exposure, the property-tax reassessment risk under California rules, and say this is a decision for an estate attorney, not a debt-payoff move. (3) Find her true monthly spending from twelve months of activity, separate household costs from her parents' costs since she manages both, and state the realistic surplus available for the card each month. (4) List recurring charges with monthly and annualized cost and name specific cancellations and the dollars they free. Where the tracker spreadsheet's tax figures are wrong, say which line and why. Close with the arithmetic she asked for: the monthly payment, the months to zero, the total interest paid, the savings floor to protect, and the freed subscription dollars redirected at the card.
</53>
<54>
id: persona-62-greenwood-ade
title: Persona 62 - Philippa and Trevor Greenwood-Ade (Charlotte empty nesters, three levers that interact)
content: |
  **Background:** Philippa Greenwood-Ade is a 53-year-old HR director in Charlotte earning $120,000, married to Trevor, a 55-year-old electrician earning $85,000. Their youngest has just left for college, and after twenty years of steering every spare dollar toward their kids they suddenly have room in the monthly budget they are not used to seeing. They hold $850,000 in retirement accounts, keep $40,000 in cash, owe about $90,000 on a $400,000 home, and still carry a Parent PLUS loan from the college years. Three problems stack: the $850,000 has drifted off its glide path with one fund holding an outsized share while idle cash acts as a drag, the freed-up money could go toward either the low-rate mortgage or the higher-rate Parent PLUS loan, and it is unclear whether the portfolio reaches their retirement number in twelve years. The decisions interact, so pulling any single lever in isolation produces the wrong answer, and the emotionally satisfying choice is rarely the mathematically correct one. Their note:

  > A NOTE TO OURSELVES - PLANNING FOR WHAT'S NEXT
  >
  > Philippa & Trevor Greenwood-Ade
  > 4127 Sharon Chase Drive, Charlotte, NC 28210
  >
  > For twenty years every dollar had a job that wasn't us: daycare, then braces, then travel teams, then two college move-ins. Our older son Simon has graduated and is on his own, and this fall our youngest, Nadia, leaves for her first year. For the first time in a very long while the budget has room in it that we don't quite recognize.
  >
  > We want to be deliberate about that room instead of letting it drift. So we're writing down where we stand and the choices we keep going back and forth on, and we're going to sit down with everything - the statements, the loan bills, the retirement account - and work it through.
  >
  > WHERE WE STAND
  >
  > - Philippa is 53 and Trevor is 55. We'd like to retire together in about twelve years, when Philippa turns 65 (Trevor will be 67).
  > - Our retirement number is $2,400,000 - the balance we want to have in our retirement accounts by then. At roughly a 4% withdrawal that's about $8,000 a month from our savings, and Social Security would come on top of that.
  > - Our retirement savings are all in the accounts on the consolidated statement.
  > - We keep our savings account as our cash cushion. It has been sitting at about the same balance for a long time.
  > - Each month now there is money left over after the bills, the mortgage, and the student loan - more than there used to be.
  > - We still owe on the house and we still owe on the parent loan we took out for the kids' college. The details are on the mortgage statement and the student-loan statement.
  >
  > WHAT SOCIAL SECURITY TELLS US (from our online estimates, in today's dollars)
  >
  > - Philippa: about $3,140 a month at full retirement age (67); about $2,830 a month if she claims at 65.
  > - Trevor: about $2,180 a month at full retirement age (67); about $1,970 a month at 65.
  >
  > THE CHOICES WE KEEP GOING BACK AND FORTH ON
  >
  > 1. The extra money each month, and the cash we've built up - should it go toward the house, toward the parent loan, or into retirement savings?
  > 2. Are our retirement accounts set up the right way for people our age, this close to retiring?
  > 3. If we keep going the way we are, do we actually reach $2,400,000 by the time we retire - and if not, what would we need to change?
  >
  > We don't want to guess at these. We want to look at the actual numbers and decide.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Pay statements for both spouses (approx. $120,000 and $85,000; 401(k) deferrals, benefit deductions, federal and North Carolina withholding)
  - Consolidated retirement statement: approx. $850,000 across both 401(k)s and any IRAs, with balances, contribution rates, employer match terms, fund lineup and position weights showing the outsized single fund
  - Checking and savings statements totaling approx. $40,000, with the interest rate actually earned on the cash cushion
  - Mortgage statement and amortization schedule: approx. $90,000 balance on a $400,000 home, rate, payment, escrow, payoff date
  - Parent PLUS loan statement: balance, interest rate, monthly payment, repayment plan and payoff date
  - Social Security estimate printouts for both spouses at ages 65 and 67
  - Twelve months of checking and card activity covering household spending and recurring subscriptions, spanning the transition out of child-related costs
  - Prior-year filed joint tax return

  **Goal:** Answer their three questions as one connected plan, not three separate ones. First, establish the true monthly surplus from twelve months of activity, separating costs that ended with the kids leaving from ongoing household costs, and state what is genuinely available each month. Then rank every use of that dollar by actual return: the Parent PLUS rate against the mortgage rate against any unclaimed employer match and unused 401(k) and catch-up contribution room at ages 53 and 55, noting that an unclaimed match beats every debt payoff and that a low-rate mortgage is usually the worst place to send money. Say plainly which debt to attack and why, and name the emotionally satisfying choice they should not make. Second, set a cash-cushion floor from their real monthly spending and quantify what the idle $40,000 is costing per year against a high-yield alternative, then say how much of the cash is genuinely deployable. Third, audit the portfolio for two people twelve years from retirement: state the real single-fund concentration and overall stock-bond mix, whether it matches an appropriate glide path at their ages, and check asset location so interest-bearing holdings sit in the tax-deferred accounts. Note that rebalancing inside retirement accounts triggers no tax, so this lever is free. Fourth, run the projection: grow $850,000 for twelve years at a stated, reasonable real return with their current contributions, compare the result to $2,400,000, and name the gap in dollars. If they fall short, solve for the additional monthly contribution required, and show how the surplus and freed debt payments close it. Finally, combine the Social Security estimates with a 4 percent withdrawal on the projected balance to test whether the retirement-income target holds, and note the difference between claiming at 65 and 67. Close with one sequenced plan: the match and contribution increases first, the debt to target with the surplus, the cash floor to hold in a high-yield account, the rebalancing to execute, the subscriptions to cut, and the monthly savings number that makes the twelve-year target real.
</54>
<55>
id: persona-63-merriweather-cole
title: Persona 63 - Sasha Merriweather-Cole (Salt Lake City, thirty-plus recurring charges and no trustworthy number)
content: |
  **Background:** Sasha Merriweather-Cole, 34, is a UX researcher in Salt Lake City earning $92,000 at Cirrus Meridian, married to Dorian, 36, an ER nurse at Jordan River paid every other Friday, so some months carry two paychecks and some three. No kids, renting in the Avenues. Cash flow is not the problem. Between them they have accumulated more than thirty recurring digital charges: three video streamers, two music logins, several cloud plans, a gym neither uses, free trials that quietly converted, apps billed annually that spike once a year, and a meal kit that pauses and resumes. They feel nickel-and-dimed and want one defensible monthly number plus a cut list they can agree on. Five traps interact: a look-alike that is not a duplicate (two music accounts on different plans), an annual charge misread as monthly, a price increase that reads as a new merchant, a paused meal kit misread as recurring, and a subscription whose payment migrated from one card to another. Her note:

  > WHERE THE MONEY IS ACTUALLY GOING - NOTES BEFORE WE DECIDE ANYTHING
  >
  > Sasha, 34. Dorian, 36. Married, no kids, renting in the Avenues. My salary is $92,000 at Cirrus Meridian; Dorian is an ER nurse at Jordan River and is paid every other Friday, so some months there are two paychecks and some months three. Between us the money is fine. That is not the problem.
  >
  > The problem is that neither of us can say what we actually pay every month for things that renew by themselves. Every time I try to add it up I get a different number, and the number I get is always big enough to be annoying and never solid enough to act on.
  >
  > WHAT I WANT OUT OF THIS
  >
  > 1. One number: what we truly pay per month for everything that renews on its own, across both credit cards and the checking account. Not a guess, and not a number that changes depending on which month I happen to look at.
  > 2. A cut list I can actually defend to Dorian - what goes, what stays, and what each cut is worth over a year.
  > 3. Once I have the true recurring number, what is genuinely left over each month after the rent, the bills, the savings transfer and the renewals.
  >
  > WHAT I HAVE PULLED TOGETHER
  >
  > - The full six-month checking export, February through July 2026.
  > - The full six-month export from the Summit Visa.
  > - The last three monthly statements for the checking and savings accounts, the Summit Visa and the Alta Mastercard.
  > - Both of our payroll statement packets.
  > - The tracker spreadsheet I started in February.
  >
  > I do not trust the tracker. Most of it was typed in from memory on a Sunday afternoon, and every time I sit down with it and an actual statement the two do not agree. Dorian and I have now had the same argument about it three times and got a different answer each time, which is how I know the argument is not really about the money.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Six-month checking transaction export, February through July 2026
  - Six-month Summit Visa transaction export, February through July 2026
  - Last three monthly statements each for checking, savings, the Summit Visa and the Alta Mastercard
  - Payroll statement packets for both spouses (approx. $92,000 salary; biweekly ER nursing pay with shift differentials)
  - A self-built tracker spreadsheet started in February, typed from memory and known to disagree with the statements

  **Goal:** Produce one defensible recurring-cost number and a cut list. (1) Build the recurring inventory from the statements and exports only, never from the tracker: for each charge name the merchant, the card or account it hits, the billing cadence, the amount, and the evidence. Normalize everything to a monthly figure - annual charges divided by twelve, and state the month each annual charge actually lands so the cash-flow spike is visible. (2) Resolve the five traps explicitly and show the work on each: the two music logins are different plans on different accounts, not a duplicate, so do not delete one as a double-count; the annually billed app is not a monthly charge; the merchant that appears new is the same service after a price increase, so report the old and new price and the increase; the meal kit paused for part of the window, so state its true frequency rather than assuming twelve billings; and the subscription that moved from the Visa to the Mastercard is one subscription, not two. Name each trap and the line items involved. (3) State the single true monthly recurring total, plus the annualized total, and reconcile it against the tracker line by line, saying which tracker rows are wrong and by how much. (4) Give the cut list in three tiers - cancel now, renegotiate or downgrade, keep - with the annual dollars each cut frees, starting with the unused gym and the converted trials. (5) Compute the honest monthly surplus: pay from both packets normalized for the three-paycheck months, minus rent, utilities and non-recurring essentials, minus the true recurring total, minus the savings transfer, and state what is genuinely left. Close with the number Sasha asked for, the cut list total, and the surplus after cuts.
</55>
<56>
id: persona-64-bustos-delgado
title: Persona 64 - Emiliano "Milo" Bustos-Delgado (Tacoma, 15+ subscriptions across three cards and no true burn number)
content: |
  **Background:** Emiliano "Milo" Bustos-Delgado, 38, is a freelance motion designer in Tacoma, Washington, single and renting, netting about $85,000 a year on variable project income. His 15+ creative software and SaaS subscriptions are scattered across three payment methods: a debit card tied to his credit union checking account, plus two credit cards that both carry revolving balances, so some tools quietly cost more than their face price as interest compounds. The stack includes two overlapping design suites, three stock asset libraries, usage-based cloud render credits that vary month to month, two invoicing apps he never finished migrating between, and personal streaming plus a coworking membership mixed in alongside. He cannot tell what his true software burn is, which tools are redundant, which are business versus personal, or how much credit card interest is inflating the total. In his words: "I pay for two of everything, on three different cards, because I never finished switching off the first one." His note:

  > Emiliano "Milo" Bustos-Delgado - working note, kept with the 2025 papers
  >
  > The problem I keep failing to sit down and solve: I do not know what my tools actually cost me last year.
  >
  > Every year I tell myself I will work it out and every year I get as far as opening a spreadsheet and then a job lands. The situation, as best I understand it:
  >
  > - I pay for tools on three different cards. Some come off the debit card attached to the credit union checking account. Some go on the Sound Federal Visa. Some go on the Meridian Mastercard. There is no logic to which is which. It is whichever card was in the browser at the time.
  > - Both credit cards carry a balance from month to month. They have done for about two years now. So a tool that bills forty dollars is not costing me forty dollars, and I have never sat down and worked out what it is actually costing me.
  > - I am fairly sure I am paying for two of some things. I switched invoicing apps and I am not confident I ever canceled the first one. I also have more than one place I pull stock footage and music from, and more than one suite that does broadly the same job, and I could not tell you which one I actually opened last month.
  > - Some of it is the business and some of it is just me. My accountant treats the business tools as deductible, which is fine, but the categories on my return came from me guessing in March and I would not want to defend them.
  >
  > What I want to end up with:
  >
  > 1. What my tools genuinely cost me across the whole of 2025, all three cards together, with the credit card interest accounted for rather than ignored.
  > 2. Which of them are doing the same job as something else I am also paying for.
  > 3. A clean split between what belongs to the business and what is just my life.
  > 4. A number I could act on - if I canceled the obvious duplicates tomorrow, what would I stop paying.
  >
  > What I have to hand:
  >
  > - Statements from the credit union for the whole year, and the transaction export.
  > - Statements from both credit cards for the whole year, plus the year-end summaries they send in January.
  > - The tax package from the invoicing platform, and last year's return.
  > - A spreadsheet I started and abandoned. I would not trust the numbers in it. I built it from memory one evening in February and I know for a fact I did not have all the statements open when I did.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Full-year 2025 credit union checking statements plus the twelve-month transaction export (debit-card subscription charges, variable client deposits)
  - Full-year 2025 Sound Federal Visa statements plus the January year-end summary (balances, APR, interest charged, minimum payments)
  - Full-year 2025 Meridian Mastercard statements plus the January year-end summary (balances, APR, interest charged, minimum payments)
  - Invoicing platform year-end tax package (1099-K/gross receipts) and the prior-year filed return with its Schedule C expense categories
  - A self-built subscription spreadsheet, typed from memory in February without the statements open and known to be unreliable

  **Goal:** Produce a defensible 2025 software-and-subscription burn number and a cut list. (1) Build the recurring inventory from statements and exports only, never from the spreadsheet: for each charge name the merchant, which of the three payment methods it hits, the billing cadence, the amount, and the evidence. Normalize everything to a monthly figure, dividing annual charges by twelve while naming the month each annual charge actually lands, and treat the usage-based render credits separately - report their range, monthly average and annual total rather than a single fixed figure. (2) Trace charges that moved between cards or changed price and confirm they are one subscription, not two, and identify any charge that stopped mid-year so the annual total reflects real billings rather than twelve assumed ones. (3) Quantify the interest inflation: using each card's APR and the interest actually charged during 2025, allocate a defensible interest cost to the subscriptions carried on revolving balances, and restate the true cost of a nominal $40 monthly tool on each card. Say plainly how much of the annual burn is interest rather than software. (4) Identify redundancy by function, not by name: the two overlapping design suites, the three stock asset libraries, and the two invoicing apps. For each cluster, state which is likely the survivor based on the evidence available, flag where the statements cannot prove usage, and say what he needs to check before canceling. (5) Split the stack cleanly into business and personal, name any charge that is genuinely mixed-use and how to apportion it, and compare the resulting business total against the Schedule C categories on the prior-year return, calling out where the filed numbers do not match the statements. (6) Reconcile against the abandoned spreadsheet line by line, stating which rows are wrong and by how much. Close with four numbers: the true 2025 annual burn across all three payment methods, the interest portion of it, the business-versus-personal split, and the immediate annual savings from canceling the obvious duplicates - plus a short sequenced action list, cheapest and least risky first.
</56>
<57>
id: persona-65-osterhout
title: Persona 65 - Marlene Osterhout (Louisville, post-divorce cash leak and legacy commitments)
content: |
  **Background:** Marlene Osterhout, 47, is a dental office manager in Louisville, Kentucky earning $61,000, divorced within the past year after selling the $480,000 marital home and splitting the equity. She has downsized to an $1,800 a month one bedroom, which on her roughly $3,705 net monthly take-home consumes 48.6 percent of her income before anything else. Her checking account still auto-debits the commitments of a bigger life: a timeshare's annual maintenance fee, a storage unit holding furniture she will never reuse, a family cell plan carrying her two adult children's lines, two subscription boxes, a country club social membership she has not visited all year, and a home security contract on the house she no longer lives in. She believes she is saving, but the balance keeps shrinking, and each month a standing transfer moves money from the high-yield savings account holding her divorce proceeds into checking to cover the gap. In her words: "I moved out a year ago and I'm still paying for a house I don't live in." Her note:

  > Trying to get my arms around this
  >
  > I keep meaning to sit down with all of it and I keep not doing it, so I am writing it out instead.
  >
  > The short version is that I moved out of the house in August of last year, the divorce was final in November, and I am still not sure where my money is going. I make sixty-one thousand a year at the dental office, which is not nothing, and the apartment is a one bedroom. On paper that should work. It does not seem to work.
  >
  > Here is what actually happens. I get paid, the money lands, and by the middle of the month there is less in there than I expected. So every month I move some over from the savings account, the one holding what I got out of the house, and that tops it back up. I have been telling myself that is temporary. I set it up as a standing transfer so I would stop thinking about it, and I think that is part of the problem, because now I do not think about it.
  >
  > The other thing is that a lot of what comes out of that account is left over from the old life. Some of it I signed up for years ago with Greg and never touched again. Some of it I am paying for other people. Some of it I am pretty sure I could cancel tomorrow and some of it I am pretty sure I could not, and I honestly cannot tell which is which without going through it properly. I know there is at least one thing where I looked into cancelling and the person on the phone said a number that made me hang up and not call back.
  >
  > I have started a spreadsheet. It is not finished and I do not trust the bottom line on it. When I did the arithmetic it came out looking like I am roughly breaking even, which does not match the fact that the balance keeps going down. One of those two things is wrong and I suspect it is my arithmetic.
  >
  > What I want to know
  >
  > 1. What is the real number. Not what the spreadsheet says. Each month, how much more am I spending than I am bringing in, and how is that gap being covered.
  > 2. Where is it going. I want it laid out - what each of these things costs me over a year, including the ones that are not billed monthly and the ones somebody is supposed to be paying me back for.
  > 3. What can I actually get rid of, and what does it cost me to do it. I do not want a list of things to cancel. I want to know which ones are genuinely worth cancelling once you account for what it costs to walk away, and which ones I am better off riding out.
  > 4. If nothing changes, how long does the savings account last.
  >
  > The lease is up at the end of August and I need to answer them before then, so I would rather know all of this now than in October.

  **Documents (in this folder):**
  - Twelve months of checking statements plus the transaction export, showing the standing monthly transfer in from savings
  - High-yield savings statements for the divorce proceeds, showing balance trajectory and interest earned
  - Payroll statement packet ($61,000 salary; net take-home roughly $3,705 monthly)
  - Apartment lease ($1,800 a month, term ending end of August)
  - Legacy commitment paperwork: timeshare annual maintenance invoice and cancellation/transfer terms, storage unit agreement, family cell plan bill itemized by line, two subscription box confirmations, country club social membership agreement with resignation terms, home security contract with early-termination clause
  - Divorce settlement documents covering the $480,000 home sale and equity split
  - A partly finished self-built spreadsheet that shows roughly break-even and is known not to reconcile

  **Goal:** Produce the real monthly deficit and a defensible exit plan for the legacy commitments. (1) Compute the true monthly shortfall from the statements only, never from the spreadsheet: net income against all outflows, and identify the standing savings-to-checking transfer as deficit financing rather than income. State the number and how the gap is covered. (2) Build the full annualized commitment inventory, normalizing every cadence to a monthly figure - the annual timeshare maintenance fee divided by twelve with the month it lands named so the cash-flow spike is visible - and separate charges that are genuinely hers from those she is fronting for others, chiefly the two adult children's cell lines and any amount she is owed but not reimbursed for. Quantify the unreimbursed subsidy in annual dollars. (3) Show housing burden explicitly: rent as a percentage of net take-home, and what rent she could actually support at a sustainable ratio, since the lease decision is due before end of August. (4) For each legacy commitment, price the cost of exit against the cost of staying: read the timeshare, security contract and club agreement terms for early-termination fees, notice periods and transfer restrictions, then rank the commitments by net annual savings after exit costs, naming which are worth cancelling now, which are worth riding out to term, and which need a specific action or call with a stated dollar threshold. Address the home security contract on a house she no longer occupies as its own item. (5) Compute the runway: at the true deficit, how many months the savings balance lasts, and recompute the runway after the recommended cuts and after a lower-rent scenario. (6) Reconcile against her spreadsheet line by line, saying which rows are wrong and by how much, and why it shows break-even when the balance is falling. Close with four numbers: the real monthly deficit, the total annual cost of the legacy commitments, the annual dollars freed by the recommended cuts net of exit costs, and the savings runway before and after - plus a sequenced action list ordered by deadline, with the lease decision first.
</57>
\`\`\`
`,v_=`---
title: Knowledge Check
---

One quiz, five questions - one for each step of the workflow. Answer all five, then submit to see your score.

\`\`\`quiz
title:
passThreshold: 80
passMessage: **You are calibrated, move on.**
failMessage: **Reread the step pages for the questions you missed, then try again.** Scoring below 80% (3/5 or fewer correct) means it is worth another pass through the workflow before you start writing.
<1>
question: Step 1 - Write the prompt. Which opener best clears the difficulty bar?
correct: b
<a>"What is the 2026 standard deduction for a married couple filing jointly?" || Single-fact lookup. No workspace required, no persona voice, no hidden finding.</a>
<b>"My wife and I are 18 months from buying in Marietta and putting $2,500/mo into savings, but our Chase and Capital One are full of Wayfair and Lowes charges. Look across both cards for March and April and tell us whether we are actually on track for $80k - and if not, where the gap is." || Persona voice, layered context, multiple sub-asks, explicit must-nots, requires the workspace, hides a headline finding.</b>
<c>"Use your best judgment to tell me if my finances look okay." || Ambiguous on every axis. Any failure is the prompt author's fault, not the model's.</c>
</1>
<2>
question: Step 2 - Identify Critical Elements. Which item belongs in the Critical Elements list?
correct: c
<a>"The response should be helpful and well-written." || Vibe words. Not a critical element - it cannot be checked against the workspace.</a>
<b>"The response should use bullet points and bold headers." || Formatting is not a critical element. The headline finding and the constraints are.</b>
<c>"The headline number is $304.95/month after cancelling exactly one of the two duplicate $159 Orangetheory charges and one of the two duplicate $22.99 Netflix charges." || Correct. Critical Elements are the specific facts, numbers, and constraints a correct answer must hit.</c>
</2>
<3>
question: Step 3 - Write failure justifications. Which justification meets the bar?
correct: b
<a>"Response 2 was confusing and had some math issues. Overall it would fail." || Too generic. Could be copy-pasted onto any task. No quoted output, no specific numbers, no tie to user intent.</a>
<b>"Response 2 says 'OrangeTheory is the big one: I'm calling it not redundant,' keeping both $159 charges because two people 'plausibly' have separate memberships. But the charge is identical on both cards in both months - a confirmed duplicate. Keeping it inflates the monthly total by $159 and lands at $463.95 instead of the correct $304.95." || Correct. Names the model, quotes the offending text, cites specific numbers, and explains why a careful reviewer would refuse to ship it.</b>
<c>"Model failed to handle the math correctly." || Not a justification. A grader could not find the failure from this alone.</c>
</3>
<4>
question: Step 4 - Golden Trajectory. What is the Golden Trajectory?
correct: a
<a>The ideal response you had in mind when you wrote the prompt - the answer that would earn the maximum score against your rubric. || Correct. The Golden Trajectory is the reference response that demonstrates every Critical Element resolved correctly.</a>
<b>A transcript of whichever model came closest to passing, lightly edited. || No. The Golden Trajectory is authored, not borrowed from a model rollout.</b>
<c>The full chain-of-thought trace the model produces while answering. || No. That is the model's reasoning trace, not the Golden Trajectory.</c>
</4>
<5>
question: Step 5 - Edit the rubric, score, and submit. Which rubric criterion passes the 4 Principles (atomic, self-contained, objective, verifiable)?
correct: c
<a>"Response is helpful and clearly addresses the user's situation." || Vibe words ('helpful,' 'clearly'). Violates Objective.</a>
<b>"Response correctly handles the math AND uses the requested heading structure." || Stacked criteria with 'AND.' Two failures get conflated. Violates Atomic - split it into two rows.</b>
<c>"Response calculates net monthly income of $10,500 (+/- 3%) for the 50/30/20 framework." || Correct. Atomic, self-contained, objective, verifiable. Tolerance is named.</c>
</5>
\`\`\`
`,sk=`---
title: Collecting form responses
---

Apps built on ^Citadel^ can include forms that save user responses directly to your project's data store. All you need to connect a form is a small config block - copy it from the admin tool and hand it to the agent. After that, you can reference the form by name in any future prompt.

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: The config is the contract
content:
The agent reads \`key_schema\` from the config and renders one submitted value per key. Do not invent extra fields outside that schema unless the admin config changes first.
\`\`\`

## Getting your form config

Every form in the admin tool has a config block you can copy to your clipboard. It looks like this:

\`\`\`code-block
language: json
content:
{
  "form_registration_id": "reg_abc123",
  "key_schema": ["response", "confidence", "notes"]
}
\`\`\`

\`form_registration_id\` is the unique identifier for this form. \`key_schema\` is the list of fields it expects - 1 entry per input you want to collect.

Copy that block from the admin tool, then paste it into your prompt the first time you set up that form. The agent saves it to \`app-config.json\` - the same file that holds your project role settings - so you only need to provide it once.

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: The agent asks first, then remembers
content:
When you ask for a form, the agent will ask for your config before it does anything else. Once you paste it, the config is saved to app-config.json and reused automatically any time you reference that form by name in a future prompt.
\`\`\`

## Setting up a form for the first time

When a form hasn't been connected yet, paste its config alongside your description. The agent reads the \`key_schema\` to know what inputs to render and saves the config so you don't need to paste it again.

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Create a form that collects a response, a confidence level, and optional notes. Use this config to connect it to our store:

{
  "form_registration_id": "reg_abc123",
  "key_schema": ["response", "confidence", "notes"]
}
\`\`\`

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Add an annotation form with fields for a label and a justification. Save the data when the user clicks Submit. Here is the config:

{
  "form_registration_id": "reg_xyz789",
  "key_schema": ["label", "justification"]
}
\`\`\`

## Using a form that's already been set up

Once a form's config has been saved, you can reference it by name. No need to paste the config again.

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
The Submit button on this page should save the form data using the annotation form.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Wire up the feedback form to the Submit button on this page.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Add a submit button that saves using the annotation form.
\`\`\`

## Confirmation and error feedback

Every form submission automatically shows a brief confirmation message when data is saved successfully. If something goes wrong, an error message appears instead. You don't need to ask for this - it's included by default. If you want different wording:

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
After the annotation form submits, show a message that says "Response recorded" instead of the default confirmation.
\`\`\`

## Preview behavior in ^Lovable^

In ^Lovable^ and development previews, form submissions don't send data to the real backend. Instead, the submitted values are printed to the browser console so you can verify the form is wired up correctly. You can test the full submit flow without affecting production data.
`,lk=`---
title: Submit to ^LLM^
---

Use \`submitToEval\` when a learner writes something and the app should return model-generated feedback, critique, or scoring. You usually do not need to ask the agent to write the wiring from scratch - describe the learner experience you want, and the eval-submission skill handles the implementation pattern.

\`\`\`callout
variant: important
title: Ask for the experience, not the plumbing
content:
The eval-submission skill handles the hook, config lookup, pending state, and backend polling. Your prompt should describe the learner interaction and the evaluator project, not the endpoint implementation.
\`\`\`

## When to ask for it

Ask for eval submission when the app needs to respond to free text: a reflection answer, a justification, a rubric explanation, or a short response that should receive coaching. Do not use it just to save a response; if the goal is persistence, use form submission instead.

\`\`\`example-pair
good: "Add a feedback step for this reflection. Use the answerFeedback eval and show the returned message below the learner answer."
bad: "Call \`/submitToEval\` with this system prompt, model, temperature, and polling loop."
context: The first prompt describes the product behavior the learner should experience. The second bypasses the skill and asks the browser to control settings that belong on the backend.
\`\`\`

## What to include in your prompt

A strong prompt gives the agent 4 things: the learner task, the question context, the eval name or config, and where the returned feedback should appear.

\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: Name the Learner Task
content: Say what the learner will write and what question or prompt their answer responds to.
</1>
<2>
title: Name the Evaluator
content: Reference the saved eval name if it already exists, or paste the eval config from the admin tool if this is the first setup.
</2>
<3>
title: Describe the Returned Effect
content: Tell the agent where the evaluator response should appear: below the answer, in a feedback panel, as an unlock condition, or as the next step in the activity.
</3>
<4>
title: Let the Skill Wire It
content: The agent will store config in \`app-config.json\`, use \`submitToEval\`, disable controls while pending, and keep model settings server-side.
</4>
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: If the eval is not configured yet
content:
Paste the eval config once or name the eval and let the agent ask for the project key. After it is saved in \`app-config.json\`, future prompts can refer to the eval by name.
\`\`\`

## Prompt examples

For first-time setup, include the eval config so the agent can save it before wiring the interaction.

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Add a written reflection activity. Ask the learner: "What evidence would you check before approving this response?"

When they click Get feedback, send their answer to the answerFeedback eval and show the evaluator's response below the input.

Use this eval config:
{
  "project": "answer-feedback-v1"
}
\`\`\`

Once the config exists, reference the eval by name.

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
On the practice page, add an AI feedback step after the learner writes their justification.

Use the existing answerFeedback eval. The button should say "Get feedback" and the returned message should appear in a feedback panel under the answer.
\`\`\`

You can also shape the tone of the returned experience without specifying backend settings.

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Create a short-answer practice item. The learner should explain why one annotation is stronger than another.

Submit the learner's answer to the rubricFeedback eval and show the response as coaching, not as a pass/fail grade.
\`\`\`

## Preview behavior

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Preview behavior
content:
In Lovable and local development, the app returns \`resources/mocks/eval-response.json\` instead of calling the live evaluator. Edit that mock response when you want a predictable preview.
\`\`\`
`,ck=`---
title: Using user data
---

Every app built on Citadel has access to the authenticated user's ^Handshake^ profile - their name, what they study, their account type, and the roles they hold on annotation projects. You can use that data to personalize content without writing any code yourself. Just describe what you want in plain language.

## Prompting for personalization

The examples below show how to ask ^Claude^ or ^Lovable^ to wire up profile-based conditions. The agent reads the profile data automatically and implements the logic - you only need to describe the condition you want.

### Greet the user by name

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Show a personalized greeting using the current user's name.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
The welcome page should say "Hello" followed by the user's first name.
\`\`\`

### Show different content by field of study

Describe what you want to show and which field it applies to. The agent understands abbreviations and common names - you don't need to match any exact value.

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
If the user is a CS major, show a computer science training module.
If they study econ, show an economics track instead.
Otherwise, show the general fellow training page.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Show a biology-specific intro for users with a life sciences background.
Everyone else should see the standard onboarding.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Show a machine learning resource section only to users with an ML or AI background.
\`\`\`

The agent recognizes majors, subfields, abbreviations, and related terms - "CS", "comp sci", "computer science", "ML", "AI", "orgo", "pre-med", "econ", "neuro", and many more.

### Show content by broad domain

Use domain names when you want to group related fields together rather than target a specific subfield.

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Show a quantitative methods callout for users in engineering or math.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
If the user has a business background, surface the finance and accounting modules first.
\`\`\`

### Gate content by account type

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
This section should only be visible to internal Handshake users.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Show an "internal preview" banner to Handshake employees.
\`\`\`

### Gate content by email domain

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Only show the university resource hub to users with a .edu email address.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
If the user has a handshake.com email, show the employee note at the top of the page.
\`\`\`

### Gate content by project role

When asking for role-based conditions, either name the project or expect the agent to ask you for a project ID. The agent will save it to \`app-config.json\` so you only have to provide it once per app.

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Only show the review queue to users who are Reviewers in this project.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
If the user is an annotator, show the annotation guide.
If they are a reviewer, show the quality checklist instead.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: The agent asks once, then remembers
content:
The first time you add a role-based condition, the agent will ask for your HAI annotation project ID if it hasn't been set. After you provide it, it's saved to app-config.json and used automatically for all future role checks in this app.
\`\`\`

---

## Advanced usage

The following sections explain the underlying implementation for developers who want to write or customize profile-based logic directly.

### Getting the current user

Import and call \`useCurrentUserProfile\` in any component. The hook returns a \`profile\` object once loaded, or \`undefined\` while the request is in flight.

\`\`\`code-block
language: typescript
content:
import { useCurrentUserProfile } from "@/hooks/useCurrentUserProfile";

function MyComponent() {
  const { data: profile, isLoading } = useCurrentUserProfile();

  if (isLoading) return <p>Loading...</p>;
  if (!profile) return null;

  return <p>Hello, {profile.fullName}</p>;
}
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: warning
title: Always guard on isLoading
content:
Never render profile-dependent UI before isLoading resolves. In Lovable the hook returns fixture data immediately; in production it awaits an API call. Skipping the guard produces flash-of-wrong-content bugs that only appear after deployment.
\`\`\`

In development and in ^Lovable^, the hook reads from \`resources/mocks/current-user-profile.json\` - no network calls. In production (\`VITE_APP_ENV=production\`), it fetches from \`/currentUserProfile\` with cookie auth.

### What the profile contains

| Field | Type | Notes |
|-------|------|-------|
| \`fullName\` | \`string\` | Display name - use for greetings |
| \`email\` | \`string\` | Primary email address |
| \`status\` | \`string\` | \`"ACTIVE"\` or \`"INTERNAL"\` (^Handshake^ employees) |
| \`attributes\` | \`ProfileAttribute[]\` | Academic fields, majors, and expertise areas |
| \`projectRoles\` | \`ProjectRole[]\` | Roles in ^HAI^ annotation projects |
| \`countryCode\` / \`stateCode\` | \`string\` | ISO location codes |
| \`handshakeUserId\` | \`number\` | ^Handshake^ platform user ID |
| \`currentOnboardingStage\` | \`string\` | Onboarding progress stage |

TypeScript types for all fields are exported from \`src/lib/resource-manifest.ts\`.

### Expertise and domain helpers

Import from \`src/lib/userProfileHelpers.ts\` - do not re-implement matching logic inline.

\`hasDomain\` checks top-level areas. \`hasExpertiseOrMajor\` checks both top-level areas and specific subfields. Both accept abbreviations and aliases.

\`\`\`code-block
language: typescript
content:
import { hasDomain, hasExpertiseOrMajor } from "@/lib/userProfileHelpers";

// Multi-branch routing with fallback:
const track = hasExpertiseOrMajor(profile, "CS")
  ? "cs"
  : hasExpertiseOrMajor(profile, "econ")
  ? "econ"
  : "general";
\`\`\`

### Account type and email helpers

\`\`\`code-block
language: typescript
content:
import { isInternalUser, hasEmailDomain } from "@/lib/userProfileHelpers";

{isInternalUser(profile) && <InternalPreviewBanner />}
{hasEmailDomain(profile, "university.edu") && <StudentResourceLink />}
\`\`\`

### Project role helper

\`hasProjectRole\` requires a \`projectId\`. Read it from \`app-config.json\` at the project root.

\`\`\`code-block
language: typescript
content:
import { hasProjectRole } from "@/lib/userProfileHelpers";
import appConfig from "../../app-config.json";

const projectId = appConfig.defaultProjectId;

{hasProjectRole(profile, "reviewer", projectId) && <ReviewerQueue />}
\`\`\`

The role name accepts aliases: \`"annotator"\` / \`"labeler"\`, \`"reviewer"\` / \`"validator"\`, \`"admin"\` / \`"administrator"\`, \`"fellow"\` / \`"fellows"\` / \`"fellowship"\`, \`"qa"\` / \`"quality analyst"\`.

Role names in HAI projects are often compound strings such as \`"Project Acme Fellow - Good"\`. Querying \`"fellow"\` (or any of its synonyms) matches any role whose name contains the word "fellow", so you don't need to spell out the full role name.

### Customizing the fixture

Edit \`resources/mocks/current-user-profile.json\` to simulate any user during local development. Swap \`status\`, change \`attributes\` values, or update \`projectRoles\` to test any branch of your conditional logic without touching the API.
`,uk=`---
title: Resources
---

The resource system lets Citadel apps fetch and display external data from APIs. Resources automatically switch between fixture data in development and real API endpoints in production.

## How it works

Resources follow a 3-step pattern: define fixture data for local development, register the resource in a manifest, and fetch it in your component using a hook.

\`\`\`code-block
language: typescript
content:
// 1. Create fixture data: resources/mocks/my-data.json
{ "id": 1, "title": "Example item" }

// 2. Register in src/lib/resource-manifest.ts
import myDataMock from "../../resources/mocks/my-data.json";

export const RESOURCES = {
  myData: {
    key: "myData",
    apiUrl: "/myEndpoint",
    mockData: myDataMock,
  },
};

// 3. Fetch in a component
const { data, isLoading } = useResource<MyType>("myData");
\`\`\`

In Lovable and local development, the hook returns your fixture data directly with no network calls. In production, it fetches from the configured API URL with cookie authentication. This means you can build and test your UI without a live backend.

## Current user profile

The \`currentUserProfile\` resource provides access to the authenticated user's Handshake profile. It uses \`credentials: 'include'\` so that learn-apps cookies are forwarded automatically - the same auth pattern used by the milestone system. The API returns the profile wrapped in a \`"profile"\` key; the resource's \`transform\` unwraps this automatically so the hook returns a flat \`UserProfile\` object.

\`\`\`code-block
language: typescript
content:
import { useCurrentUserProfile } from "@/hooks/useCurrentUserProfile";

function MyComponent() {
  const { data: profile, isLoading, error } = useCurrentUserProfile();

  if (isLoading) return <p>Loading...</p>;
  if (!profile) return null;

  return <p>Welcome, {profile.fullName}</p>;
}
\`\`\`

The hook logs the profile to the browser console via \`console.table\` by default, so you can inspect the data shape during development.

## Fixture data

The fixture file at \`resources/mocks/current-user-profile.json\` ships with the template so that new projects have working data out of the box. It mirrors the real API envelope with the \`"profile"\` wrapper. You can customize the inner fields to match your testing needs.

\`\`\`code-block
language: json
content:
{
  "profile": {
    "id": "prof-local-1",
    "name": "jdoe",
    "fullName": "Jane Doe",
    "lastName": "Doe",
    "email": "jane.doe@example.com",
    "handshakeUserId": 12345,
    "currentOnboardingStage": "FULLY_ONBOARDED",
    "countryCode": "US",
    "stateCode": "CA",
    "profileAttributes": { "role": "fellow" },
    "status": "ACTIVE",
    "attributes": [
      {
        "id": "attr-1",
        "attribute": {
          "value": "engineering",
          "metadata": { "source": "manual" }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: tip
title: Inspect it in the console
content:
In development and Lovable, \`useCurrentUserProfile\` logs the full profile object to the browser console via \`console.table\` when data loads. Open DevTools to inspect the shape of the data and verify your fixture matches what you expect.
\`\`\`
