`,k_=`---
title: Leaving feedback
---

When you fix or send back a task, your comments are how the fellow learns and how the task gets to an approvable state. Because **you own the task through any re-submission**, incomplete feedback just buys you another round-trip. Get it all out the first time.

\`\`\`callout
variant: purple
title: What the ideal comment set does
content:
It tells the fellow what they did well, and gives constructive, specific feedback on everything that needs to change - comprehensively enough that, if they action all of it, you can approve the task. No surprises in round two.
\`\`\`

## The anatomy of a strong comment set

\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: Open with what's working
content: Name the specific things the fellow did well - a strong persona voice, a genuinely hard prompt, a well-targeted justification. This is honest and it tells them what to keep doing.
</1>
<2>
title: Then each issue, in three parts
content: For every problem: (1) what's wrong, quoted or pointed at specifically; (2) why it matters for the deliverable or the user; (3) exactly what to change. No vague "this is off."
</2>
<3>
title: Close by confirming completeness
content: State that this is the full set of changes needed to approve. The fellow should be able to action your comments and land an approvable task without a third pass.
</3>
\`\`\`

## The 4 rules

\`\`\`good-neutral-bad-table
<1>
topic: Specific
good: "Quote the exact number, criterion, or sentence. 'Criterion 12 says $715.95; the correct figure from the files is $486.91.'"
bad: "'Some of the numbers look off.'"
</1>
<2>
topic: Actionable
good: "Say what to do: 'Rewrite criterion 12 to state $486.91 and re-check the dependent total in 15.'"
bad: "'The rubric needs work.'"
</2>
<3>
topic: Comprehensive
good: "Cover everything blocking approval in one pass, across prompt, justifications, and rubric."
bad: "Flag one issue, approve the re-submit, then discover three more."
</3>
<4>
topic: Constructive
good: "Neutral, helpful tone that assumes good faith and starts from what worked."
bad: "Harsh or accusatory phrasing that just lists faults."
</4>
\`\`\`

## Weak vs. strong comment set

\`\`\`example-pair
beforeTitle: Weak (do not do this)
before:
The prompt is okay but the rubric has issues and some justifications are wrong. Please fix and resubmit.
afterTitle: Strong (this is the bar)
after:
Nice work - the persona voice is strong and the duplicate-billing angle is a genuinely hard, well-hidden finding. Three things to fix before I can approve: (1) Justification for Model 2 says March spending is $8,097.54, but the statements total $8,282.54 - it dropped the $185 Progressive charge; correct the figure and the "true surplus" line that depends on it. (2) Rubric criterion 9 ("Response correctly calculates cash advances") uses a process + vibe word - rewrite as "Response states cash-advance savings as $315.99." (3) Criteria 6 and the negative in 26 both penalize the balance-gap math - drop the mirror negative. Action these three and it's approvable.
context: The strong version praises specifics, quotes exact numbers and criterion IDs, says exactly what to change, and confirms that's the complete set for approval.
\`\`\`

## Practice: is this comment ready to send?

\`\`\`flag-and-fix
instruction: Decide whether each reviewer comment is good to send, or needs to be rewritten before it goes to the fellow.
keyLesson: Specific, actionable, comprehensive, constructive - and complete enough that actioning it leads to approval.
flagLabel: Rewrite first
okLabel: Send it
buttonOrder: flag-ok
<1>
id: a
text: "This is wrong, please redo the rubric."
isProblematic: true
reason: Not specific, not actionable, no praise, not comprehensive.
improved: Name each rubric criterion that fails, why, and the exact rewrite - and lead with what worked.
</1>
<2>
id: b
text: "Strong, hard prompt and clean persona. Two fixes to approve: criterion 4 references march_statement.pdf - make it self-contained by stating the value ($2,939.77); and criterion 18 duplicates 17, so remove 18. That's everything."
isProblematic: false
reason: Praise, specific fixes with IDs and exact changes, and an explicit completeness statement.
</2>
<3>
id: c
text: "I fixed a few things. Looks fine now."
isProblematic: true
reason: Gives the fellow nothing to learn from and doesn't document what changed or why.
improved: List each edit you made and why, so the fellow improves and the audit trail is clear.
</3>
\`\`\`
`,T_=`---
title: Your role as a reviewer
---

Reviewers are the quality gate on Project Lighthouse. Every task that passes through you is delivered to the client. 3 pieces make up that delivery:

- **The prompt** - the consumer-finance question the fellow wrote.
- **The model failure justifications** - the written case for why specific models failed.
- **The rubric** - the weighted criteria used to score the model responses.

The **role introduction** attached to each task is context for you to understand the scenario - you review and edit it, it is not itself a deliverable.

\`\`\`callout
variant: important
title: You are the approval - and approval releases payment
content:
Lighthouse pays per approved task, and you are the approval. When you approve, a fellow gets paid for that task. A loose approval pays out work that should not have passed; an unfair rejection costs a fellow work that was actually fine. Keep that in mind on every review.
\`\`\`

## Why being a reviewer is worth it

- **Hourly pay** - Reviewers are paid hourly, (based on the task time limit)
- **A higher task limit** - Reviewing lifts your ceiling on the project.
- **Direct influence on the project** - Your feedback shapes how the project runs and what "good" means.
- **Priority for future projects** - Your work is tracked on the backend, so strong reviewers get prioritized for other projects as they open up.

## Getting started - the onboarding gate

\`\`\`step-sequence
<1>
title: Attend the onboarding session
content: Required before you review anything.
</1>
<2>
title: Start capped at 1–2 reviews
content: New reviewers are throttled while a specialist checks their early work.
</2>
<3>
title: Get signed off to unthrottle
content: After your first one or two reviews, post in Slack and get sign-off from one of the project specialists. If your work meets the quality bar, they unthrottle you and you can continue reviewing at full capacity.
</3>
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: warning
title: Your reviews are audited
content:
A downstream audit layer spot-checks reviewer decisions. Over-rejections and approvals of tasks that don't meet the quality bar both show up. Reviewers who consistently miss in either direction get coached, and patterns that don't improve may affect your spot on the project. Your approvals carry weight - treat them that way.
\`\`\`

## Live sessions

- **Reviewer office hours with Malik and Mo**, Tuesdays, 4:00 PM PT - [Join](https://us06web.zoom.us/j/86019360880?pwd=w7z3liWjwFAoEsy16SZfTJkTuzpfoO.1)
- **Reviewer office hours with Malik and Mo**, Fridays, 11:00 AM PT - [Join](https://us06web.zoom.us/j/81656483441?pwd=fq2ziS5XuefIJOQ5EaJa7oMCapxwQm.1)

## Getting your first 2 reviews signed off

Before you get unthrottled, your first 2 reviews must be signed off by a team member. After you submit each of your first 2 reviews, post a message in the reviewer Slack channel, tagging the project team, with the following:

\`\`\`code-block
language: text
content:
Task ID: ...
Decision: Approve/Reject
SQS: (Score out of 5)
Notes:
\`\`\`

Use **Notes** for a brief heads-up on anything the team should be aware of when reviewing your review - edge cases you weren't sure about, judgment calls you made, or context that explains your decision.

A project specialist will review your decision and reasoning, and reply in thread to let you know whether they agree with your review or if anything needs to change. Once both reviews are signed off and meet the quality bar, you'll be unthrottled and can review at full capacity.

## Owning a task

When you claim a task, you own it end-to-end. **You must see every task all the way through to the end - no exceptions.**

\`\`\`callout
variant: important
title: Claiming a task is a commitment, not a placeholder
content:
The moment you claim a task, it is yours until it is fully resolved - approved or finally rejected after every re-submission. Walking away from a claimed task, letting it sit overnight, or expecting someone else to finish it is not acceptable. Reviewers who don't see their tasks through get pulled from the project.
\`\`\`

- **Finish what you start - same day, every time.** Every task you claim must be completed the same day you claim it. Do not leave a claimed task open overnight. If you can't finish it today, don't claim it.
- **See every send-back through to resolution.** When a fellow re-submits, you are the 1 who reviews the re-submission - and the 1 after that, and the 1 after that, until the task is approved or finally closed. Sending a task back is not the end of your job; it is the middle of it.
- **Never abandon a claim.** A claimed task is not a maybe. Do not drop it for someone else to pick up, do not unclaim it because it got harder than you expected, and do not leave it half-reviewed. Own it to resolution.
`,E_=`---
title: Reviewing the prompt
---

This is where most send-backs come from, because on a Rejections-on queue the prompt can't be edited. Read it as a real person would, then **solve it yourself against the environment files** before judging anything else.

*If Rejections are off on your queue, broken prompts become your fix instead of a send-back - see the mode callout on [Workflow and send-backs](/reviewer-workflow).

\`\`\`callout
variant: warning
title: Always solve the prompt first
content:
You can't tell whether a model failed - or whether a justification is right - until you know the correct answer from the files. Work the prompt end to end before you read the responses.
\`\`\`

## What a good prompt has

\`\`\`checklist
<1>
id: persona
label: Clearly uses the assigned persona
hint: The persona's occupation, income, family, and spending show through in the question.
</1>
<2>
id: natural
label: Sounds like a real person
hint: Natural, first-person phrasing - the kind of question someone actually types, not a templated paragraph.
</2>
<3>
id: no-llm
label: No LLM-generated text
hint: Strictly not allowed. Machine-written prompts get sent back.
</3>
<4>
id: hard
label: A real ask that still breaks models
hint: A genuine personal-finance question that is meaningfully difficult and produces real model failures.
</4>
\`\`\`

## What counts as a meaningful model failure

A meaningful model failure is something the model got **objectively wrong** in a way that changes the answer or violates the prompt. Cosmetic differences, style preferences, and harmless variations don't count.

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Hallucination
content:
**Counts** - the model invented a charge, account, or figure that isn't in the files.<br/>**Doesn't count** - the model used a slightly awkward synonym for a real figure.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Miscalculation
content:
**Counts** - wrong total, wrong rate, or a dropped transaction.<br/>**Doesn't count** - a rounding difference in the last cent that doesn't change the answer.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Files and data
content:
**Counts** - the model couldn't find the statements, or fabricated data not in the workspace.<br/>**Doesn't count** - the model opened the files in a different order than you would.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Answer
content:
**Counts** - the model reached no valid answer, or no answer at all.<br/>**Doesn't count** - the model reached the right answer but formatted it differently than you'd prefer.
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Instructions
content:
**Counts** - the model explicitly violated a constraint the prompt stated.<br/>**Doesn't count** - the model did something the prompt never asked it not to do.
\`\`\`

## The 2 most common prompt-section problems

\`\`\`accordion
<1>
title: 1. The prompt doesn't resolve to a correct answer from the files
content: "Work the prompt yourself against the documents. If you can't reach a single defensible answer - because the files contradict each other, a needed number is missing, or the question is ambiguous - the prompt is broken. On a Rejections-on queue, send it back; on a Rejections-off queue, patch the prompt in place and re-run the models (see [Workflow and send-backs](/reviewer-workflow)). A failure that comes from an ambiguous prompt is the author's fault, not the model's."
</1>
<2>
title: 2. The justification is wrong, so the failure is false
content: "Sometimes the model actually answered correctly, and the justification arrives at different values that aren't the real answer. That isn't a model failure - it's a mistake in the justification. Verify every number in the justification against the files before you trust that a model failed."
</2>
\`\`\`

## Practice: is this a real failure?

\`\`\`flag-and-fix
instruction: For each claimed model failure, decide whether it's a meaningful failure worth delivering, or a minor non-failure that should not be the basis of a send-back or justification.
keyLesson: Meaningful failures are wrong numbers, hallucinations, missing/invalid answers, or violated constraints - verifiable against the files. Phrasing nitpicks and unrequested "shoulds" are not failures.
flagLabel: Not a real failure
okLabel: Meaningful failure
buttonOrder: ok-flag
<1>
id: a
text: "The model reported March spending of $8,097.54, but the statements total $8,282.54 - it dropped a real $185 charge by wrongly treating it as a duplicate."
isProblematic: false
reason: A verifiable math error that changes the answer. This is a meaningful failure.
</1>
<2>
id: b
text: "The justification fails the model for 'not reconciling its totals against the account balances,' even though every figure the model reported is correct against the files."
isProblematic: true
reason: The numbers are right. 'Didn't show extra reconciliation' is process depth the prompt never required - a false failure.
improved: Only fail the model if a stated figure is wrong, a constraint was violated, or the answer is missing/invalid.
</2>
<3>
id: c
text: "The model recommended opening a new credit card, which the prompt explicitly forbade."
isProblematic: false
reason: A direct, verifiable constraint violation. Meaningful failure.
</3>
<4>
id: d
text: "The justification dings the model because its executive summary 'could have been phrased more clearly.'"
isProblematic: true
reason: Pure phrasing nitpick. Not a substantive failure and not grounds for a send-back.
improved: Drop this point; it doesn't belong in a failure justification.
</4>
\`\`\`
`,S_=`---
title: Reviewer quiz & case files
---

If you were not promoted directly to reviewer, you may have been invited to take the reviewer quiz. The quiz tests your judgment on real-shaped cases before you start reviewing live tasks.

Read every document in each case below before answering the quiz questions.

## Case files

\`\`\`callout
variant: note
title: Case 1 — Subscriptions / cash flow
content:
**Priya Vance — Denver, CO.** Registered nurse, around 30, renter. She and her husband Marcus (a construction project manager) share two credit cards and a joint checking account, and she wants to clean up subscriptions they may be paying for twice.

---

**Files**

- [card_marcus_chase_2026-04.pdf](/case-files/card_marcus_chase_2026-04.pdf)
- [card_priya_amex_2026-04.pdf](/case-files/card_priya_amex_2026-04.pdf)
- [checking_joint_firstpeak_2026-04.pdf](/case-files/checking_joint_firstpeak_2026-04.pdf)
- [subscriptions_export_2026-03_2026-04.csv](/case-files/subscriptions_export_2026-03_2026-04.csv)
- [savings_firstpeak_2026-04.pdf](/case-files/savings_firstpeak_2026-04.pdf)
- [credit_report_summary_2026.pdf](/case-files/credit_report_summary_2026.pdf)
\`\`\`

\`\`\`callout
variant: purple
title: Case 2 — Goal-based / scenario planning
content:
**Daniel Okafor — San Francisco, CA.** 32, single, renter, senior software engineer (~$230,000/yr). He is planning a 20% down payment on a ~$900k condo and wants a realistic read on what he can deploy, the monthly savings needed, and what to watch.

---

**Files**

